[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'Access to sensitive data shall be granted only to authorized users based on the principle of least privilege and role-based access control (RBAC).', 'rationale': 'Aligned with NIST SP 800-53 control SC-12 (Access Control) and FISMA requirements for minimizing exposure of sensitive data.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user request for access to a data asset;
WHEN the access request is evaluated against role-based policies;
THEN access is granted only if the user has a defined, approved role and the data asset is classified appropriately.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'All access attempts to sensitive data must be logged with timestamps, user ID, action type, and outcome (success/failure).', 'rationale': 'Supports auditability and accountability as required by NIST SP 800-53 control IA-4 (Audit and Accountability) and the Privacy Act.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a system access event;
WHEN the event occurs;
THEN a log entry is generated and stored for at least 18 months with immutable storage and access controls.'}, {'id': 'NON-001', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'requirement': 'The access control system shall maintain system availability with a mean time between failures (MTBF) of at least 99.99% over a 30-day period.', 'rationale': 'Ensures continuous operation of critical systems, consistent with NIST SP 800-53 control SI-4 (System Availability).', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a 30-day operational period;
WHEN the system is under normal load;
THEN system downtime shall not exceed 5 minutes in total, with no single incident exceeding 1 minute.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The system shall evaluate access requests using attribute-based policies defined in accordance with NIST SP 800-178 and XACML v3.0 standards.', 'rationale': 'Aligns with established standards for ABAC and provides a defensible, interoperable access control framework.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user request to access sensitive data;
WHEN the system evaluates the request against an ABAC policy;
THEN the system shall return an access decision based on user attributes and policy rules, as defined in NIST SP 800-178 and XACML v3.0.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-001', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'The system shall support dynamic access decisions based on real-time user attributes such as job role, department, and clearance level.', 'rationale': 'Enables context-aware access control, consistent with least privilege and dynamic policy enforcement.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a user attempting to access a data service;
WHEN the user's attributes are retrieved and validated;
THEN the system shall apply an ABAC policy to determine whether access is granted or denied."}, {'id': 'NON-001', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'text': 'The access decision engine shall respond to access requests within 500 milliseconds under normal load conditions.', 'rationale': 'Ensures timely access decisions without degrading user experience or system performance.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a high-volume access request scenario;
WHEN the system processes a request;
THEN the response time shall not exceed 500 milliseconds.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The system shall require multi-factor authentication (MFA) for all administrative accounts and for any user attempting to perform privileged actions such as configuration changes or large financial transactions.', 'rationale': "MFA is the most effective defense against credential stuffing and password spraying attacks, as demonstrated by Microsoft's analysis showing it stops 99.9% of account compromises.", 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a login attempt by an administrator for a privileged action;
WHEN MFA is triggered;
THEN the user must complete a second authentication factor (e.g., FIDO2 Passkey) before access is granted.
AND the system shall log the MFA event and the outcome (success/failure).'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The system shall detect and respond to suspicious login attempts based on the following criteria: a new browser/device, unusual location, known proxy or VPN IP, multiple failed attempts from a single IP, or a scripted login pattern.', 'rationale': 'These indicators are common in credential stuffing and password spraying attacks, and triggering MFA or CAPTCHA in such cases reduces the risk of unauthorized access.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a login attempt from an IP address that has attempted multiple logins;
WHEN the system detects a pattern of repeated failed attempts;
THEN it shall require a CAPTCHA or MFA challenge before allowing access.
AND the system shall record the IP address and the number of failed attempts for monitoring purposes.'}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The system shall generate and maintain metrics on detected and mitigated attack volumes, including filtering by IP address and login source, to enable continuous monitoring and detection of defense failures.', 'rationale': 'Metrics provide a detective mechanism to identify when defenses are being bypassed or when new attack patterns emerge, supporting proactive defense adjustments.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a login attempt that triggers a defense mechanism;
WHEN the system detects an attack;
THEN it shall record the event in a metrics log with fields including IP address, attack type, and outcome (detected/mitigated).
AND the system shall allow filtering of logs by IP address or attack type for analysis.'}, {'id': 'SEC-004', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'text': 'The system shall allow configuration of trusted IP ranges where users from those ranges are exempt from MFA requirements, to balance security and usability.', 'rationale': 'Enterprise users may be located in trusted geographies or networks, and requiring MFA for all connections may negatively impact usability without sufficient risk.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user attempting to log in from a known trusted IP range;
WHEN the system validates the IP against a pre-configured allowlist;
THEN MFA shall not be required for that login attempt.
AND the system shall maintain an audit log of exempted logins for compliance and review.'}]

[{'id': 'FPR-01', 'type': 'functional', 'description': 'The system shall allow a user to request a password reset by entering their email or username.', 'rationale': 'Enables users to recover access to their accounts when they forget their passwords.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user enters a valid email or username;
WHEN they submit the request;
THEN the system shall return a consistent message regardless of whether the account exists or not.'}, {'id': 'FPR-02', 'type': 'functional', 'description': 'The system shall generate a cryptographically secure, randomly generated URL token and send it via email to the user.', 'rationale': 'Provides a secure, verifiable method for users to reset their passwords without exposing account data.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user requests a password reset;
WHEN the system generates a token;
THEN the token shall be embedded in a URL and sent via email using HTTPS, with the host header validated against a trusted domain list.'}, {'id': 'FPR-03', 'type': 'security', 'description': 'The system shall ensure that the time taken to respond to a password reset request is uniform, regardless of whether the account exists or not.', 'rationale': 'Prevents account enumeration attacks by avoiding timing differences that could reveal account existence.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user submits a password reset request;
WHEN the system responds;
THEN the response time shall be consistent across all inputs, and no timing differences shall be observable.'}, {'id': 'FPR-04', 'type': 'security', 'description': 'The system shall generate password reset tokens using a cryptographically secure random number generator and ensure they are sufficiently long to resist brute-force attacks.', 'rationale': 'Prevents attackers from guessing valid tokens through brute-force attempts.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a token is generated;
THEN the token length shall be at least 32 characters and generated using a cryptographically secure random number generator.'}, {'id': 'FPR-05', 'type': 'security', 'description': 'The system shall invalidate the password reset token after it has been used to reset the password or after a specified period (e.g., 15 minutes), whichever comes first.', 'rationale': 'Prevents reuse of tokens and reduces the risk of unauthorized access via token interception.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user uses a token to reset their password;
THEN the token shall be invalidated immediately after use or after 15 minutes, whichever occurs first.'}, {'id': 'FPR-06', 'type': 'non-functional', 'description': 'The system shall implement rate limiting on password reset requests per user account to prevent automated attacks.', 'rationale': 'Mitigates the risk of flooding email inboxes or SMS with invalid reset requests.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an attacker attempts to submit more than 10 password reset requests per minute;
THEN the system shall reject additional requests and return a generic error message.'}, {'id': 'FPR-07', 'type': 'security', 'description': "The system shall not send the user's new password in any email or communication channel.", 'rationale': 'Prevents exposure of sensitive credentials to unauthorized parties.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a password reset is completed;
THEN the system shall send an email confirming the reset without including the new password.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'The JWT must be signed using HMAC-SHA256 with a secret key of at least 32 bytes, generated and stored securely in the backend server environment.', 'rationale': 'HMAC-SHA256 provides a secure, deterministic, and computationally feasible signature method suitable for shared secrets. It avoids the complexity of public-key infrastructure and reduces the risk of key exposure.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a JWT is issued by the backend server;
WHEN the token is validated by the frontend;
THEN the signature must be verified using HMAC-SHA256 with a backend-generated secret key;
AND the validation must succeed only if the signature matches the expected value.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': "The JWT must include standard claims such as 'sub' (subject), 'name', 'iat' (issued at), and 'exp' (expiration time), and must not contain any sensitive data in the claims section.", 'rationale': 'Standard claims ensure interoperability and provide a consistent structure for user identity and session metadata, reducing the risk of misinterpretation or injection attacks.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a user logs in;
WHEN the backend issues a JWT;
THEN the token must contain the 'sub', 'name', 'iat', and 'exp' claims;
AND no sensitive data (e.g., passwords, PII) must be included in the claims."}, {'id': 'FUNC-001', 'category': 'Functional', 'requirement': "The system shall issue a JWT upon successful user login, with a default expiration time of 15 minutes, and the token must be stored in the client's browser local storage.", 'rationale': 'A short-lived token reduces the window of opportunity for misuse and aligns with best practices for stateless session management.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a user enters valid credentials;
WHEN the login endpoint is called;
THEN a JWT with 'exp' set to 15 minutes from issuance must be returned;
AND the token must be stored in the browser's local storage for subsequent requests."}, {'id': 'NON-001', 'category': 'Non-functional', 'requirement': "The system shall not allow token replay attacks by validating the 'iat' claim and rejecting tokens issued more than 15 minutes ago.", 'rationale': 'Preventing replay attacks ensures that tokens cannot be reused after expiration, which is critical for session security.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a token is issued;
WHEN it is used in a subsequent request;
THEN the system must validate the 'iat' claim;
AND reject any request where the token was issued more than 15 minutes ago."}]

[{'id': 'MFA-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "The application shall require multifactor authentication (MFA) for all user logins, using at least one factor from the 'Something You Know', 'Something You Have', or 'Something You Are' categories.", 'rationale': "MFA is the best defense against password-related attacks such as brute-force and credential stuffing, as demonstrated by Microsoft's analysis showing it would have stopped 99.9% of account compromises.", 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user attempts to log in to the application;
WHEN the user provides a valid username and password;
THEN the system shall prompt for a second authentication factor (e.g., TOTP code) and only allow access upon successful verification.'}, {'id': 'MFA-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The application shall require MFA for any action that modifies user account settings, including changing passwords, email addresses, or security questions.', 'rationale': 'Sensitive actions that alter user data are high-risk and should be protected by MFA to prevent unauthorized changes.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user attempts to change their password or email address;
WHEN the user has not completed MFA;
THEN the system shall require MFA before allowing the action to proceed and only permit it after successful MFA verification.'}, {'id': 'MFA-003', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The application shall enforce a time-to-live (TTL) of no more than 30 seconds for one-time passwords (OTPs) generated during authentication.', 'rationale': 'OTPs must be short-lived to prevent interception and reuse, reducing the risk of account compromise.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an OTP is generated during login;
WHEN the user attempts to use it after 30 seconds;
THEN the system shall reject the request and prompt for a new OTP.'}, {'id': 'MFA-004', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The application shall not store or log OTP values in plaintext or in any form that allows reconstruction of the original value.', 'rationale': 'Improper handling of OTPs can lead to user account compromise, as OTPs are authentication secrets and must be treated like passwords.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an OTP is generated;
WHEN it is stored or logged;
THEN the system shall ensure that the value is not stored in plaintext and is not logged in any form that could allow reconstruction.'}, {'id': 'MFA-005', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'text': 'The application shall provide a user-friendly interface to enable and manage MFA via TOTP (Time-based One-Time Password) tokens.', 'rationale': 'Users must be able to easily configure MFA, especially if they are not technically proficient, to ensure adoption and usability.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a user is on the application login page;
WHEN they select 'Enable MFA';
THEN they shall be guided through a step-by-step process to set up a TOTP token using a mobile app, with clear instructions and error messages."}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The application shall use the Proof Key for Code Exchange (PKCE) mechanism to protect authorization codes during the OAuth 2.0 authorization code grant flow.', 'rationale': 'PKCE mitigates the risk of authorization code interception attacks, which are particularly critical for public clients like mobile apps.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user initiates authentication via the app AND the authorization server supports PKCE;
WHEN the app generates a PKCE code challenge and includes it in the authorization request;
THEN the authorization server shall validate the corresponding code verifier at the token endpoint, and no unauthorized client shall be able to redeem a stolen authorization code.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'Access tokens shall be bearer tokens issued by the authorization server and shall be restricted to a single audience (resource server) to limit the impact of token leakage.', 'rationale': 'Bearer tokens are simple to implement but require strict audience restrictions to prevent unauthorized access if tokens are intercepted.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a bearer token is obtained from an authorization server;
WHEN the token is intercepted by an attacker;
THEN the token shall only be valid for the intended resource server and shall not be usable by any other server.'}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'Refresh tokens shall be protected using sender-constraining mechanisms (e.g., DPoP or mTLS) or shall be rotated at regular intervals or upon user action.', 'rationale': 'Refresh tokens are high-value credentials that, if compromised, can lead to long-term access. Sender-constraining mechanisms ensure only the legitimate client can use them.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a refresh token is issued to the client;
WHEN the token is intercepted;
THEN the client shall not be able to use it to obtain new access tokens without a valid sender-constrained proof or a new token issued after rotation.'}, {'id': 'NON-001', 'category': 'Non-functional', 'text': 'The application shall not include open redirectors in any URL redirections that forward user browsers to arbitrary URIs.', 'rationale': 'Open redirectors can be exploited to exfiltrate authorization codes or access tokens, violating OAuth security best practices.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user is redirected to a URL via the app;
WHEN the URL is constructed using a query parameter;
THEN the URL must not be arbitrary and must be validated against a whitelist of allowed domains.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'Access tokens shall be sender-constrained using either Mutual TLS (mTLS) or Demonstration of Proof of Possession (DPoP) as defined in RFC 8705 and RFC 9449, respectively, to prevent token replay attacks.', 'rationale': 'Sender-constrained tokens cryptographically bind the token to a specific client via proof-of-possession of a private key, reducing the risk of replay attacks.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a token issued with mTLS or DPoP;
WHEN a client attempts to use the token to access a resource;
THEN the Resource Server shall validate that the token was issued by the correct client and reject the request if the sender is not authenticated or the token is not valid.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'Refresh tokens shall be sender-constrained using DPoP or mTLS, and shall be rotated by issuing new tokens and immediately invalidating old ones upon issuance to detect replay attempts.', 'rationale': 'Combining sender-constrained refresh tokens with rotation provides defense-in-depth against replay attacks and token misuse.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a refresh token is issued;
WHEN a client attempts to use an old refresh token to obtain a new access token;
THEN the system shall invalidate the old token and issue a new one with sender-constrained authentication.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-001', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': "Access tokens shall be restricted to a specific Resource Server (audience restriction) as defined by the 'aud' parameter in the token, and every Resource Server shall verify that the token was issued for that server before processing a request.", 'rationale': 'Audience restriction prevents unauthorized access to resources by ensuring tokens are only valid for intended servers.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a request to a Resource Server;
WHEN the access token is sent with the request;
THEN the Resource Server shall validate the token's audience and reject the request if the audience does not match the target server."}, {'id': 'FUNC-002', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': "Access tokens shall be restricted to specific resources and actions (e.g., read, write) as defined by the 'scope' and 'authorization_details' parameters, and every Resource Server shall verify that the token is authorized for the requested action on the requested resource.", 'rationale': 'Scope and action-level restrictions prevent overprivileged access and reduce the impact of token leakage.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a request to perform an action on a resource;
WHEN the access token is sent with the request;
THEN the Resource Server shall validate that the token includes the required scope and action permissions and reject the request if insufficient privileges are present.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'Passwords shall be stored using a strong, slow hashing algorithm such as Argon2id, bcrypt, or PBKDF2, and shall never be stored in plain text.', 'rationale': 'Hashing is a one-way function that prevents attackers from retrieving the original password even if the database is compromised.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a password is stored in the database;
WHEN the database is accessed by an attacker;
THEN the attacker cannot retrieve the original password value, and the hash must be irreversibly derived.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'Each password shall be hashed with a unique salt to prevent precomputed attacks such as rainbow table attacks.', 'rationale': 'Salting ensures that even if two users have the same password, their hashes will differ, making bulk cracking significantly harder.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN two users have the same password;
WHEN their passwords are hashed;
THEN the resulting hashes must be different due to unique salts.'}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'For new systems, Argon2id shall be used with a minimum configuration of 19 MiB of memory, an iteration count of 2, and 1 degree of parallelism.', 'rationale': 'Argon2id is a memory-hard algorithm that makes brute-force attacks expensive and time-consuming.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a password is hashed using Argon2id;
WHEN an attacker attempts to brute-force the password;
THEN the attack must take at least 10 seconds on a standard workstation and must consume at least 19 MiB of memory.'}, {'id': 'SEC-004', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'If Argon2id is not available, scrypt shall be used with a minimum CPU/memory cost parameter of (2^17), a minimum block size of 8 (1024 bytes), and a parallelization parameter of 1.', 'rationale': 'Scrypt provides a memory-hard alternative when Argon2id is unavailable, reducing the feasibility of GPU-based attacks.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a password is hashed using scrypt;
WHEN an attacker uses a GPU or cloud-based service to perform brute-force attacks;
THEN the attack must require at least 2^17 CPU cycles and consume at least 8 KB of memory per block.'}, {'id': 'SEC-005', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'For legacy systems using bcrypt, a work factor of 10 or more and a password limit of 72 bytes shall be enforced.', 'rationale': 'A higher work factor increases the computational cost of brute-force attacks on bcrypt, making it more secure.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a password is hashed using bcrypt;
WHEN an attacker attempts to brute-force the password;
THEN the computation must take at least 10 iterations and the password length must not exceed 72 characters.'}, {'id': 'SEC-006', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'If FIPS-140 compliance is required, PBKDF2 shall be used with a work factor of 600,000 or more and an internal hash function of HMAC-SHA-256.', 'rationale': 'PBKDF2 with a high work factor provides a FIPS-140-compliant method for password hashing in regulated environments.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a password is hashed using PBKDF2;
WHEN an attacker attempts to brute-force the password;
THEN the attack must require at least 600,000 iterations and must use HMAC-SHA-256 as the internal hash function.'}, {'id': 'SEC-007', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'A pepper shall be used as an additional defense in depth, and shall be stored in a hardware security module (HSM) or a secrets vault, not in the database or in public configuration files.', 'rationale': 'Peppering adds an extra layer of protection when the database is compromised, as the pepper is not stored with the password hash.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an attacker has access to the database and the hash values;
WHEN the attacker attempts to reverse-engineer the original password;
THEN the attacker must also have access to the pepper stored in a secrets vault to succeed.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-SAML-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'All SAML messages must be transmitted over TLS 1.2 or higher to ensure confidentiality and integrity at the transport layer.', 'rationale': 'TLS 1.2 is the standard for securing transport-layer communication and prevents eavesdropping and man-in-the-middle attacks as specified in the SAML Security Cheat Sheet.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a SAML request or response is sent over the network;
WHEN the connection uses TLS 1.2 or higher;
THEN the message is protected from eavesdropping and man-in-the-middle attacks.'}, {'id': 'SEC-SAML-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'All SAML messages must include a unique request ID in the AuthnRequest and be referenced in the response via the InResponseTo attribute to ensure authenticity and prevent replay attacks.', 'rationale': 'The InResponseTo attribute ensures that responses are tied to specific requests, preventing forged or replayed responses as identified in the SAML Profile requirements.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a SAML AuthnRequest is issued;
WHEN the response includes the InResponseTo attribute matching the request ID;
THEN the response is authenticated and not a replay or forged message.'}, {'id': 'SEC-SAML-003', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "All SAML responses must contain a digitally signed assertion using the IdP's private key to ensure message integrity and authenticity.", 'rationale': 'Digital signatures prevent message modification and forged assertions, as outlined in SAML Security section 4.3 and the XML Signature Wrapping attack mitigation recommendations.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a SAML response is received;
WHEN it contains a digitally signed assertion with the IdP's private key;
THEN the assertion has not been tampered with and is authentic."}, {'id': 'SEC-SAML-004', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'Schema validation must be performed on all SAML messages prior to processing, using only locally stored, trusted schema copies. No schema downloads from third-party sources are permitted.', 'rationale': 'Schema validation prevents attacks such as XML signature wrapping and ensures that only valid message structures are processed, as recommended in the SAML Security Cheat Sheet.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a SAML message is received;
WHEN schema validation is performed using a locally stored, trusted schema;
THEN the message structure is valid and no malicious schema injection occurs.'}, {'id': 'SEC-SAML-005', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'SAML responses must not be cached by the service provider, or if caching is allowed, it must be done with strict expiration policies to prevent stolen assertion and replay attacks.', 'rationale': 'Caching SAML responses can lead to stolen assertion or replay attacks, as specified in the SAML Binding section for HTTP POST binding.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a SAML response is received by a service provider;
WHEN it is cached;
THEN it is stored with a short expiration time and is invalidated upon use to prevent replay attacks.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The web application shall enforce the use of secure and HttpOnly flags on all session cookies in HTTP responses via the Set-Cookie header.', 'rationale': 'Prevents session ID exposure via client-side scripts and ensures cookies are transmitted over secure channels, reducing the risk of session hijacking.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user logs in via a secure connection;
WHEN the web application sets a session cookie;
THEN the Set-Cookie header must include the Secure and HttpOnly flags.
AND the session ID must not be visible in JavaScript or other client-side scripts.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The web application shall detect and terminate a session if a new authentication event occurs from a different client IP address or User-Agent than the previously active session, unless explicitly allowed by user configuration.', 'rationale': 'Prevents attackers from maintaining access through session reuse in shared networks or proxies by terminating old sessions upon new logins.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user is logged in from IP X with User-Agent U;
WHEN a new login occurs from IP Y or User-Agent V;
THEN the system shall either terminate the previous session or prompt the user to confirm which session to retain.'}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The web application shall log a salted-hash of the session ID instead of the full session ID in all session-related logs, including creation, renewal, destruction, and invalid activity events.', 'rationale': 'Protects against unauthorized disclosure of session IDs while enabling correlation of log entries to specific sessions.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a session is created or terminated;
WHEN a log entry is generated;
THEN the log must contain a salted-hash of the session ID, not the full session ID.
AND the log must include timestamp, source IP, target resource, HTTP headers (User-Agent, Referer), and user ID.'}, {'id': 'SEC-004', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The web application shall provide a user-facing interface to view, monitor, and remotely terminate active sessions at any time, and shall log all such actions in an account activity history.', 'rationale': 'Enables support personnel to resolve session-related issues and provides accountability for session management actions.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user accesses the administrative interface;
WHEN they view active sessions;
THEN they can see session details including IP, User-Agent, login time, and idle time.
AND they can manually terminate a session.
AND all such actions must be recorded in an account activity log.'}, {'id': 'SEC-005', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The web application shall integrate with a Web Application Firewall (WAF) to enforce session management protections, including session ID renewal on privilege changes, sticky sessions, and session expiration enforcement.', 'rationale': 'Provides external defense against session-based attacks when source code modifications are not feasible or when full redesign is impractical.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user changes privileges within the application;
WHEN the session ID is renewed on the client side;
THEN the WAF must detect and enforce this renewal.
AND the WAF must verify the relationship between the session ID and client properties (IP or User-Agent) to enforce sticky sessions.
AND the WAF must enforce session expiration by triggering client and server-side session termination.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The JWT token shall be transmitted only over encrypted channels using Transport Layer Security (TLS) to prevent interception and unauthorized disclosure of sensitive information.', 'rationale': 'As per the source, information must be transmitted over encrypted channels to prevent disclosure to unintended parties.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client attempts to authenticate via JWT in an OAuth 2.0 flow;
WHEN the connection uses TLS;
THEN the JWT token is not visible to intermediaries or unauthorized parties.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'In cases where sensitive information must not be disclosed to the client, the JWT shall be encrypted to the authorization server.', 'rationale': 'The source specifies that JWTs should be encrypted to the authorization server when preventing disclosure of certain information is desired.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user attempts to authenticate with a sensitive identity;
WHEN the system determines that disclosure to the client must be prevented;
THEN the JWT is encrypted and sent only to the authorization server.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-001', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'The system shall include only the minimum amount of information necessary to complete the authentication exchange in the JWT token.', 'rationale': 'The source emphasizes that deployments should determine the minimal information needed and include only such claims in the JWT.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a user requests authentication;
WHEN the system evaluates required claims;
THEN only essential claims (e.g., 'sub') are included in the JWT, and no extraneous data is transmitted."}, {'id': 'FUNC-002', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': "The 'sub' (subject) claim in the JWT may represent an anonymous or pseudonymous user, as defined in Section 6.3.1 of RFC 7521.", 'rationale': "The source references the OAuth Assertion Framework [RFC7521] for the use of 'sub' as a value for anonymous or pseudonymous users.", 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a user with no real identity;
WHEN the system generates a JWT;
THEN the 'sub' claim shall contain a pseudonymous identifier, not a real user identifier."}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The client shall generate a cryptographically strong code_verifier using a secure random number generator and store it securely in memory during the authorization flow.', 'rationale': 'A strong code_verifier ensures that the code_challenge is sufficiently random and resistant to brute-force or prediction attacks.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a mobile app using OAuth 2.0 with the Authorization Code Grant;
WHEN the app generates a code_verifier;
THEN the code_verifier shall be at least 43 characters long and use a secure random generator, and shall not be stored in persistent storage or shared across sessions.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "The client shall generate a code_challenge from the code_verifier using the S256 method and include it in the authorization request as the 'code_challenge' parameter.", 'rationale': 'The code_challenge is used to verify the code_verifier at the token endpoint, preventing attackers from using intercepted authorization codes to obtain access tokens.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an authorization request;
WHEN the client includes the code_challenge parameter;
THEN the server shall validate the code_challenge against the stored code_verifier before issuing an access token.'}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The client shall send the authorization code and the original code_verifier to the token endpoint during token exchange.', 'rationale': 'This ensures that the server can verify the integrity of the authorization code using the code_verifier, preventing reuse or interception attacks.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a successful authorization code from the server;
WHEN the client sends the authorization code and code_verifier to the token endpoint;
THEN the server shall reject the request if the code_verifier does not match the stored value, and return an error response.'}, {'id': 'NON-001', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'text': 'All client-server communication during the OAuth 2.0 flow shall be protected by Transport Layer Security (TLS) using at least TLS 1.2.', 'rationale': 'TLS protects the authorization code and other sensitive data from being intercepted during transmission, especially in unsecured OS-level communication paths.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client requesting an authorization code;
WHEN the request is sent to the authorization server;
THEN the communication shall be encrypted using TLS 1.2 or higher, and the client shall not use unsecured channels (e.g., HTTP).'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "The native app shall not initiate an OAuth 2.0 authorization request directly using an embedded user-agent. Authorization requests shall be made exclusively through an external user-agent, such as the user's browser.", 'rationale': 'Embedded user-agents share the same security domain as the native app, allowing access to cookies and page content, which increases the risk of session hijacking and client impersonation. Using a browser as an external user-agent ensures a clear trust boundary.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user attempts to authenticate via the native app;
WHEN the app attempts to initiate an OAuth 2.0 authorization request;
THEN the app shall redirect the user to a browser-based authorization endpoint and shall not attempt to render or handle the authorization page within the app itself.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The authorization server shall support OAuth 2.0 with browser-based authorization flows as defined in RFC 8252, including the use of URI redirections for receiving authorization responses.', 'rationale': 'The specification requires authorization servers to support browser-based flows to ensure interoperability and security with external user-agents.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN the native app redirects the user to an authorization endpoint;
WHEN the user completes authentication in the browser;
THEN the browser shall redirect the user back to the native app via a URI scheme (e.g., https://) and the authorization server shall validate the response and issue an authorization code or token.'}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The native app shall validate all incoming authorization responses using a secure URI scheme (e.g., https://) and shall reject any redirections using private-use URI schemes or loopback interfaces unless explicitly permitted by the authorization server.', 'rationale': 'Private-use URI schemes and loopback redirections can be exploited to bypass security controls or perform cross-app request forgery attacks. Only secure, public URI schemes are acceptable.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a redirection from the browser to the native app;
WHEN the redirection uses a private-use URI scheme or loopback interface;
THEN the app shall reject the response and prompt the user to retry the authorization flow via the browser.'}, {'id': 'SEC-004', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'text': 'The authorization flow shall complete within 30 seconds of user initiation, including browser redirection and response handling, to ensure usability.', 'rationale': 'A long authorization flow degrades user experience and may lead to abandonment. The 30-second threshold ensures a responsive and user-friendly experience.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user initiates authentication;
WHEN the app redirects to the browser and receives a response;
THEN the entire flow shall complete within 30 seconds, measured from the start of the request to the receipt of the final token.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The application shall not set the SO_REUSEPORT or SO_REUSEADDR socket options when establishing a socket for handling authorization responses, to prevent other applications from binding to the same socket and causing conflicts or security vulnerabilities.', 'rationale': 'The RFC 8252 best practice explicitly recommends avoiding these socket options to prevent interference with other apps and maintain secure socket behavior on Linux.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a Linux desktop environment with multiple running applications;
WHEN the app attempts to bind to a socket for authorization response;
THEN the app shall not set SO_REUSEPORT or SO_REUSEADDR, and the socket binding shall succeed without conflict.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-001', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': "The application shall open the OAuth 2.0 authorization request in the user's default browser using a distro-specific command, such as 'xdg-open', to ensure compatibility across Linux distributions.", 'rationale': "The RFC 8252 specifies that opening authorization requests in a browser requires distro-specific commands, with 'xdg-open' being one such tool.", 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a Linux system with a configured default browser;
WHEN the app invokes the browser to display the authorization request;
THEN the browser shall open successfully using the 'xdg-open' command or equivalent distro-specific mechanism."}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The application shall use the loopback redirect as the recommended method for receiving the authorization response on Linux desktop apps, to ensure secure and reliable communication between the app and the authorization server.', 'rationale': 'The RFC 8252 recommends the loopback redirect for desktop apps on Linux to receive the authorization response, as it provides a secure and predictable path for handling redirects.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a Linux desktop app running with a user session;
WHEN the authorization server redirects the user back to the app;
THEN the app shall receive the response via the loopback interface and process it correctly without requiring external network access.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The authorization server metadata MUST be transmitted over HTTPS to prevent man-in-the-middle attacks.', 'rationale': 'HTTPS ensures confidentiality and integrity of metadata during transmission, as specified in Section 6.1.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client attempts to retrieve metadata from a well-known URI;
WHEN the connection is made over HTTP;
THEN the client SHALL receive an error indicating the request is insecure and SHALL not proceed.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'When metadata is provided as a JWT, the server MUST sign the metadata using a valid cryptographic algorithm (e.g., RS256) and include a valid issuer claim.', 'rationale': 'JWT signing provides authenticity and integrity of metadata, as described in Section 6.3 and the role of the issuer in vouching for validity.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client receives metadata in a JWT format;
WHEN the JWT is validated against a known issuer and valid signature;
THEN the client SHALL accept the metadata and use it to configure authorization flows.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-001', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'The client SHALL retrieve authorization server metadata from a well-known URI (e.g., /.well-known/oauth-authorization-server) as defined in Section 3.1.', 'rationale': 'The specification defines a standard method for metadata retrieval via well-known URIs.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client configured with an authorization server URI;
WHEN it sends a GET request to the well-known URI;
THEN it SHALL receive a valid JSON document containing endpoint locations and capabilities.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-002', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'The client SHALL validate the metadata for correctness and completeness using the schema defined in the specification, including endpoint locations and supported grant types.', 'rationale': 'Correctness of metadata is essential for secure and functional OAuth 2.0 interactions, as per Section 2 and 3.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client receives metadata;
WHEN it validates the schema;
THEN it SHALL reject any metadata that lacks required fields such as the authorization endpoint or token endpoint.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The authorization server shall authenticate client requests to the token endpoint using mutual TLS with X.509 certificates.', 'rationale': 'Mutual TLS provides stronger client authentication than shared secrets, reducing risk of impersonation.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client sends a request to the token endpoint with an X.509 certificate;
WHEN the authorization server receives the request;
THEN it shall validate the certificate against a trusted certificate store and reject the request if validation fails.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-002', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': "Access tokens issued by the authorization server shall be bound to the client's X.509 certificate via a certificate thumbprint.", 'rationale': 'Token binding ensures that only the legitimate client holding the private key can use the token to access protected resources.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client presents a valid certificate to the authorization server;
WHEN an access token is issued;
THEN the token shall include a certificate thumbprint that matches the presented certificate.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-003', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'The protected resource shall validate the access token by checking its certificate thumbprint before allowing access to protected APIs.', 'rationale': 'Ensures that the token was issued to the correct client and has not been intercepted or forged.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a client sends a request to a protected resource with a certificate-bound access token;
WHEN the resource validates the token;
THEN it shall verify the token's certificate thumbprint against the client certificate and reject the request if mismatched."}, {'id': 'SEC-004', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The authorization server shall support both PKI-based and self-signed certificate mutual TLS authentication methods.', 'rationale': 'Provides flexibility for environments with varying PKI maturity or deployment constraints.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a client uses a self-signed certificate;
WHEN it attempts to authenticate to the token endpoint;
THEN the authorization server shall accept the request if the certificate is valid and trusted by the server's configuration."}, {'id': 'NON-005', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'text': 'The system shall support TLS 1.2 or higher for all client-server communications.', 'rationale': 'Ensures forward compatibility and protection against known TLS vulnerabilities.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client connects to the authorization server or protected resource;
WHEN the connection is established;
THEN the TLS version used shall be at least TLS 1.2.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'type': 'security', 'text': 'The authorization server shall bind access tokens to an asymmetric key pair held by the client, using a proof-of-possession mechanism to ensure that only the client possessing the private key can access protected resources.', 'rationale': 'This aligns with the design of mutual-TLS client authentication and certificate-bound access tokens as described in RFC 7009 and the provided appendix, which define a proof-of-possession mechanism for access tokens.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a client attempting to access a protected resource with an access token;
WHEN the authorization server validates the token against a client's public key;
THEN the server shall reject the request if the client cannot prove possession of the corresponding private key via mutual TLS or certificate binding."}, {'id': 'FUNC-001', 'type': 'functional', 'text': "The system shall support the issuance of access tokens that are bound to an X.509 certificate or a JSON Web Key (JWK) representing a client's public key.", 'rationale': 'The RFC 7009 and RFC 7518 specifications define token binding and JWA, which are foundational to the token-certificate relationship described in the excerpt.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client with a valid X.509 certificate or JWK;
WHEN the authorization server issues an access token;
THEN the token shall include a binding reference to the certificate or JWK, and the server shall validate the binding during token consumption.'}, {'id': 'NON-001', 'type': 'non-functional', 'text': 'The system shall support mutual TLS client authentication with a minimum performance overhead of 100 milliseconds during the initial handshake.', 'rationale': 'Mutual TLS has been established as a widely supported mechanism, and performance is a key factor in real-world deployment as noted in the excerpt.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client connecting to the authorization server via TLS;
WHEN the server performs mutual TLS handshake;
THEN the total handshake time shall not exceed 100 milliseconds under normal network conditions.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'requirement': 'The system shall use OAuth 2.0 as defined in RFC 7523 for client authentication and authorization grants.', 'rationale': 'RFC 7523 provides a standardized profile for JWT-based OAuth 2.0 client authentication, ensuring interoperability and security.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client attempting to authenticate via OAuth 2.0;
WHEN the client presents a valid JWT;
THEN the system shall validate the JWT against the issuer and audience claims and return a successful authentication response.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'requirement': 'The system shall enforce token expiration using a minimum lifetime of 300 seconds for access tokens and 1800 seconds for refresh tokens.', 'rationale': 'Short-lived tokens reduce the window of opportunity for misuse and align with zero-trust principles of minimizing privilege duration.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user receives an access token;
WHEN the system checks the token expiration time;
THEN the system shall reject any request with a token that has expired more than 300 seconds ago.'}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'requirement': 'The system shall support dynamic client registration as defined in RFC 7591 to allow secure client registration without pre-configured credentials.', 'rationale': 'Dynamic client registration enables secure, automated client onboarding, reducing configuration errors and improving scalability.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a new client application submits a registration request;
WHEN the request includes a valid public key and client metadata;
THEN the system shall register the client and issue a client ID and secret, with the secret stored securely and not exposed in logs.'}, {'id': 'SEC-004', 'requirement': 'The system shall validate JWTs using the JSON Web Token Best Current Practices (RFC 8725) to ensure proper signature, claims, and structure validation.', 'rationale': 'RFC 8725 establishes best practices for JWTs, including signature integrity and claim validation, which are critical for preventing forgery and tampering.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a JWT token is received;
WHEN the system validates the signature and claims;
THEN the system shall reject any token with a malformed signature or invalid claims.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "The authorization server shall validate DPoP proofs in access token requests by verifying the JWT signature and binding of the token to the client's public key.", 'rationale': 'Prevents replay attacks by ensuring the client possesses the private key associated with the token.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an access token request with a DPoP header containing a valid JWT;
WHEN the authorization server validates the JWT signature and key binding;
THEN the request shall be accepted and the token issued without error.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The resource server shall verify the DPoP header in all protected resource access requests to ensure the token is bound to a valid client key pair.', 'rationale': 'Provides assurance that the client presenting the token has possession of the private key, reducing risk of token misuse.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a protected resource access request with a DPoP header;
WHEN the resource server validates the DPoP JWT;
THEN the request shall be processed only if the DPoP proof is valid and the token is bound to a known client key.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-001', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'The system shall support DPoP for both access and refresh tokens when issued to public clients.', 'rationale': 'Ensures protection against token misuse even when clients lack authentication credentials.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a public client requesting a refresh token;
WHEN a DPoP proof is included in the request;
THEN the refresh token shall be issued and bound to the client's public key."}, {'id': 'NON-001', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'text': 'DPoP validation shall not introduce more than 50ms of latency in request processing.', 'rationale': 'Ensures acceptable performance for real-time web applications.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a typical access token request;
WHEN the DPoP header is validated;
THEN the total request processing time shall not exceed 50ms.'}]

Actors/Assets: Two network devices (e.g., firewalls or routers) establishing an IPsec tunnel; the data payload transmitted over the tunnel.

Key Functional Concerns: Encryption and data origin authentication of IPsec ESP payloads using AES-GCM mode; construction of ESP payload data with an 8-octet IV and ciphertext;

Non-Functional Concerns: Performance at high data rates (e.g., 10+ Gbps); efficiency in both hardware and software implementations;

Security Concerns: Prevention of nonce reuse; integrity and confidentiality of data; protection against replay and tampering;

Assumptions: The devices support IPsec ESP and AES-GCM; the key management system (e.g., IKE) is operational and configured to prevent nonce/key reuse;

Missing Information: Specific network topology, device types, data types being transmitted, key lifecycle management, and operational environment (e.g., public vs private network).

[{'id': 'ESP-SEC-01', 'requirement': 'The IPsec ESP protocol shall provide confidentiality, data origin authentication, connectionless integrity, an anti-replay service, and limited traffic flow confidentiality for all data transmitted in the tunnel.', 'rationale': 'ESP is designed to offer a comprehensive set of security services as defined in RFC 4303, ensuring data protection at rest and in transit.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a packet is transmitted between two endpoints via an ESP tunnel;
WHEN the ESP header is processed;
THEN the packet shall be authenticated, encrypted, and protected against replay attacks as specified in RFC 4303.'}, {'id': 'ESP-PROC-01', 'requirement': 'The ESP header shall be inserted after the IP header and before the next layer protocol header (transport mode) or before an encapsulated IP header (tunnel mode).', 'rationale': 'This defines the correct placement of the ESP header in both transport and tunnel modes as per RFC 4303.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an IP packet is being processed;
WHEN it is in transport mode;
THEN the ESP header shall be inserted between the IP header and the transport header.
AND WHEN it is in tunnel mode;
THEN the ESP header shall be inserted before the encapsulated IP header.'}, {'id': 'ESP-INT-01', 'requirement': 'The integrity check value (ICV) shall be calculated and verified for each packet using a cryptographic hash function (e.g., SHA-256) as defined in RFC 4303.', 'rationale': 'ICV ensures data integrity and prevents tampering during transmission, as specified in the ESP protocol.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a packet is received at the destination;
WHEN the ICV is verified;
THEN the packet shall be accepted only if the calculated ICV matches the received ICV.'}, {'id': 'ESP-SEQ-01', 'requirement': 'A sequence number shall be generated and used for each packet to support anti-replay protection, with an extended (64-bit) sequence number as defined in Appendix A of RFC 4303.', 'rationale': 'The anti-replay service prevents replay attacks by ensuring packets are processed in sequence and not reused, as specified in the ESP protocol.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a packet is received with a sequence number;
WHEN the sequence number is compared against the expected range;
THEN the packet shall be accepted only if it falls within the valid anti-replay window and has not been previously processed.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'requirement': 'IKEv2 messages shall be fragmented before cryptographic operations (encryption and authentication) are applied to the payload.', 'rationale': 'Fragmentation before encryption ensures each fragment is individually authenticated and encrypted, enabling the receiver to detect forged fragments during reassembly and prevent them from being stored in the reassembling queue.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a message is fragmented before encryption;
WHEN a forged fragment is injected into the network;
THEN the receiver shall detect the forgery during reassembly and reject the fragment without storing it in the reassembling queue.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'requirement': 'Each IKEv2 fragment message shall be individually encrypted and authenticated using the same cryptographic suite as the full message.', 'rationale': 'Individual fragment protection ensures that any forged or malformed fragment can be identified and rejected at the fragment level, preventing a successful DoS attack that relies on forged fragments being accepted.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a fragmented message is received;
WHEN a fragment is found to have invalid cryptographic signatures or malformed headers;
THEN the receiver shall reject the fragment and not include it in the reassembly process.'}, {'id': 'PERF-001', 'requirement': 'The total payload size of an encrypted IPsec message (including headers and padding) shall not exceed the IP datagram size by more than 105 bytes in IPv4 and 85 bytes in IPv6 (without extension headers).', 'rationale': 'This ensures that fragmentation is necessary only when the message exceeds the available IP datagram size, based on standard header sizes and cryptographic overheads as defined in RFC 7383.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a message with encrypted payload;
WHEN the total size of the encrypted payload and headers is computed;
THEN the size shall not exceed the IP datagram size by more than 105 bytes (IPv4) or 85 bytes (IPv6 without extension headers).'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-REQ-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The IKEv2 AUTH payload shall use RSASSA-PSS with SHA-256 as the hash algorithm for signature generation and verification.', 'rationale': 'SHA-256 provides stronger resistance to collision attacks compared to SHA-1, which is deprecated due to known vulnerabilities.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an IKEv2 AUTH payload with a RSASSA-PSS signature;
WHEN the hashAlgorithm is SHA-256 (OID 2.16.840.1.101.3.4.2.1);
THEN the signature shall be valid and the payload shall be correctly parsed using ASN.1 structure defined in RFC 7427.'}, {'id': 'SEC-REQ-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The RSASSA-PSS signature shall use a salt length of 32 octets and a trailer field value of 1.', 'rationale': 'The default parameters in PKCS#1 specify a salt length of 32 for SHA-256, enhancing entropy and resistance to chosen-plaintext attacks.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a signature generated with RSASSA-PSS and SHA-256;
WHEN the saltLength is set to 32 and trailerField is set to 1;
THEN the signature shall pass validation against the defined ASN.1 structure and be resistant to length extension attacks.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-REQ-001', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'The IKEv2 AUTH payload shall begin with a fixed 15-byte ASN.1 structure containing the sha1WithRSAEncryption or RSASSA-PSS identifier and hash algorithm.', 'rationale': 'The structure is defined in RFC 7427 and must be correctly formatted to ensure interoperability with IKEv2 peers.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an IKEv2 AUTH payload;
WHEN the payload starts with the ASN.1 sequence for sha1WithRSAEncryption or RSASSA-PSS;
THEN the payload shall be correctly formatted and the signature shall follow immediately after the header.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-TLS-1.3-001', 'requirement': 'The TLS 1.3 implementation shall prevent eavesdropping, tampering, and message forgery during client-server communication.', 'rationale': 'TLS 1.3 is designed to provide confidentiality and integrity through cryptographic protocols, as specified in RFC 8446.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client and server communicating over TLS 1.3;
WHEN a message is transmitted;
THEN the message must be encrypted and authenticated, and no unauthorized party can decrypt or modify it without detection.'}, {'id': 'SEC-TLS-1.3-002', 'requirement': 'The implementation shall support the use of elliptic curve key exchange (e.g., P-256, P-384) as defined in the TLS 1.3 handshake protocol.', 'rationale': 'RFC 8446 specifies key exchange mechanisms using elliptic curve cryptography, which provide strong security with smaller key sizes.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client and server with compatible elliptic curve parameters;
WHEN they perform a TLS 1.3 handshake;
THEN the key exchange must use a supported group (e.g., P-256) and produce a shared secret that is cryptographically secure.'}, {'id': 'SEC-TLS-1.3-003', 'requirement': 'The implementation shall support 0-RTT data transmission, with proper validation to prevent replay attacks.', 'rationale': 'TLS 1.3 introduces 0-RTT data to reduce handshake latency, as defined in Section 4.2.10 of RFC 8446, but requires secure validation mechanisms.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client that sends 0-RTT data after a handshake;
WHEN the server receives and processes the data;
THEN the data must be validated against the handshake transcript to prevent replay or injection attacks.'}, {'id': 'SEC-TLS-1.3-004', 'requirement': 'The implementation shall support certificate verification using trusted certificate authorities (CAs) as defined in Section 4.2.4 of RFC 8446.', 'rationale': 'Certificate verification ensures authenticity and prevents man-in-the-middle attacks, as specified in the TLS 1.3 protocol.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a server presenting a certificate;
WHEN the client performs certificate verification;
THEN the certificate must be issued by a trusted CA and must be valid and not expired.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-TLS-001', 'requirement': 'The API service shall only support TLS 1.2 or higher for all client-to-server communications.', 'rationale': 'TLS 1.0 and TLS 1.1 are deprecated due to cryptographic weaknesses and lack of support for modern cipher suites, including authenticated encryption with associated data (AEAD).', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client attempting to connect to the API service using TLS 1.0 or TLS 1.1;
WHEN the connection is attempted;
THEN the connection shall be rejected with a protocol error, and the client shall receive a clear error message indicating that TLS 1.0 and TLS 1.1 are no longer supported.'}, {'id': 'SEC-TLS-002', 'requirement': 'The API service shall not use any cipher suites that rely on SHA-1 for message hashing or signature authentication.', 'rationale': 'SHA-1 is considered cryptographically broken and is problematic in TLS 1.0 and 1.1, where it is used for handshake integrity and peer authentication.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a TLS handshake using a cipher suite that includes SHA-1;
WHEN the handshake is initiated;
THEN the handshake shall fail with a protocol error, and the server shall not accept the connection.'}, {'id': 'SEC-TLS-003', 'requirement': 'All DTLS-based services, if any, shall use DTLS 1.2 or higher and shall not support DTLS 1.0.', 'rationale': 'DTLS 1.0 is deprecated and lacks support for modern cryptographic mechanisms; DTLS 1.2 is the current standard for secure datagram communication.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client attempting to establish a DTLS connection using DTLS 1.0;
WHEN the connection is attempted;
THEN the connection shall be rejected with a protocol error, and the client shall receive a clear error message indicating DTLS 1.0 is no longer supported.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-TLS-001', 'requirement': 'The TLS protocol version shall be 1.2, as specified in RFC 5246.', 'rationale': 'TLS 1.2 provides improved cryptographic strength and security over earlier versions, including resistance to known attacks on older protocols.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client-server connection using TLS 1.2;
WHEN the handshake completes;
THEN the connection shall use the TLS 1.2 protocol as defined in RFC 5246, and the client and server shall agree on a cipher suite that supports at least one of the mandatory cipher suites defined in Section 9.'}, {'id': 'SEC-TLS-002', 'requirement': 'All data transmitted over the TLS connection shall be protected against eavesdropping, tampering, and message forgery.', 'rationale': 'The TLS protocol is designed to provide confidentiality and integrity of data in transit, as stated in the abstract.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client sends a message to a server;
WHEN the message is transmitted over a TLS connection;
THEN the message shall be encrypted and authenticated such that an unauthorized party cannot read or modify it without detection.'}, {'id': 'SEC-TLS-003', 'requirement': 'The handshake protocol shall support both RSA and Diffie-Hellman key exchange mechanisms as defined in Section 7.4.7.1 and 7.4.7.2.', 'rationale': 'The TLS 1.2 protocol supports multiple key exchange methods, enabling flexibility and compatibility with diverse client and server implementations.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client and server with different key exchange capabilities;
WHEN they perform a TLS handshake;
THEN the handshake shall succeed using either RSA or Diffie-Hellman key exchange, depending on mutual support.'}, {'id': 'SEC-TLS-004', 'requirement': 'The master secret shall be computed using the cryptographic methods defined in Section 8.1.1 and 8.1.2, ensuring secure key derivation.', 'rationale': 'Secure key derivation is critical to the integrity of the session keys used for encryption and authentication.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a TLS handshake with a client and server;
WHEN the master secret is derived;
THEN the derived master secret shall be computed using the specified cryptographic methods and shall be used to generate session keys for data encryption and message authentication.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The TLS implementation shall use at least 2048-bit RSA key sizes for digital signatures and key exchange, as specified in [KEYSIZ] and aligned with current cryptographic standards.', 'rationale': 'Smaller key sizes are vulnerable to attacks such as those described in [KPR03] and [SUBGROUP]; 2048-bit is the minimum recommended size to resist known factorization attacks.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client-server TLS handshake using RSA key exchange;
WHEN a 1024-bit or smaller RSA key is used;
THEN the handshake shall fail with a protocol error or be rejected by the server.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The TLS implementation shall use elliptic curve cryptography (ECC) in cipher suites as defined in [TLSECC], with at least a 256-bit curve (e.g., secp256r1), to provide equivalent security with smaller key sizes.', 'rationale': 'ECC offers stronger security per bit of key length compared to RSA, reducing computational overhead and improving performance, as per [TLSECC].', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client-server TLS handshake using an ECC-based cipher suite;
WHEN a curve smaller than secp256r1 is used;
THEN the handshake shall be rejected or fail with a protocol error.'}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The TLS implementation shall avoid using Diffie-Hellman groups with small subgroups, as defined in [SUBGROUP], to prevent small-subgroup attacks on key agreement.', 'rationale': 'Small-subgroup attacks can compromise the confidentiality of exchanged keys, as described in [SUBGROUP]; using validated MODP groups (e.g., [MODP]) mitigates this risk.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client-server TLS handshake using a Diffie-Hellman group;
WHEN the group is identified as having a small subgroup;
THEN the handshake shall be rejected or fail with a protocol error.'}, {'id': 'NON-001', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'text': 'The TLS handshake process shall complete within 100 milliseconds under normal network conditions to ensure acceptable user experience.', 'rationale': 'Performance is critical for user experience, especially in high-traffic environments; timing constraints are relevant to avoid denial-of-service via slow-handshake attacks.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client connecting to the server over HTTPS;
WHEN the connection is established;
THEN the handshake shall complete within 100 milliseconds, measured from client connection initiation to first data receipt.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'A client that requests an OCSP response and receives a response from a server using a compromised key must either contact the OCSP server directly or abort the handshake.', 'rationale': 'Prevents an attacker from impersonating a server with a compromised key by ensuring the client validates OCSP responses independently or terminates the connection if trust is compromised.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client requests an OCSP response from a server using a compromised key;
WHEN the client receives an OCSP response;
THEN the client shall either validate the response directly with the OCSP server or abort the handshake.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-002', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'The Server Name extension shall specify only ASCII representation and shall allow a ServerNameList containing multiple names of any name_type.', 'rationale': 'Aligns with RFC 6066 to ensure interoperability and avoid encoding issues with non-ASCII domain names.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client sends a Server Name extension;
WHEN the server receives a name that is not recognized;
THEN the server shall either continue the handshake without error or send a fatal error, and shall not send a warning-level message.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-003', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'The Client Certificate URLs extension shall require the presence of a hash value in the extension data.', 'rationale': 'Ensures integrity of certificate URL references and prevents tampering or spoofing of certificate retrieval paths.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client sends a Client Certificate URLs extension;
WHEN the extension is received;
THEN the extension shall contain a mandatory hash value for each URL.'}, {'id': 'SEC-004', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'TLS servers shall not follow HTTP redirects when retrieving certificates.', 'rationale': 'Prevents potential redirection attacks that could lead to unauthorized certificate retrieval or man-in-the-middle scenarios.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a server attempts to retrieve a certificate via an HTTP request;
WHEN an HTTP redirect is encountered;
THEN the server shall reject the request and not follow the redirect.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'requirement': 'The TLS implementation shall support the use of FFDHE groups as defined in RFC 7919, including ffdhe6144 and ffdhe8192.', 'rationale': 'FFDHE groups are standardized in RFC 7919 and provide forward secrecy through ephemeral key exchange, which is essential for modern TLS deployments.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a TLS handshake with a peer using ffdhe6144 or ffdhe8192;
WHEN the peer sends a key exchange message;
THEN the implementation shall correctly parse and process the group parameters (modulus and generator) and perform the key exchange using the specified group.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'requirement': 'Peers using ffdhe6144 that wish to optimize key exchange with a short exponent shall use a secret key of at least 375 bits.', 'rationale': 'The FFDHE 6144-bit group has an estimated symmetric-equivalent strength of 175 bits, and a minimum secret key size of 375 bits is required to maintain security against small-exponent attacks as per RFC 7919, Section 5.2.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client using ffdhe6144 with a short exponent;
WHEN generating a secret key;
THEN the secret key shall be at least 375 bits in length and shall not be smaller than the minimum required for security.'}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'requirement': 'Peers using ffdhe8192 that wish to optimize key exchange with a short exponent shall use a secret key of at least 400 bits.', 'rationale': 'The FFDHE 8192-bit group has an estimated symmetric-equivalent strength of 192 bits, and a minimum secret key size of 400 bits is required to maintain security against small-exponent attacks as per RFC 7919, Section 5.2.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client using ffdhe8192 with a short exponent;
WHEN generating a secret key;
THEN the secret key shall be at least 400 bits in length and shall not be smaller than the minimum required for security.'}, {'id': 'PERF-001', 'requirement': 'The key exchange operation shall complete within 150 milliseconds on a standard 2-core CPU under typical load conditions.', 'rationale': 'Performance optimization is critical for client-side experience, especially in mobile and IoT environments where latency is a key concern.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a TLS handshake with a peer using ffdhe6144 or ffdhe8192;
WHEN the key exchange is performed;
THEN the operation shall complete within 150 milliseconds on a 2-core CPU with 4GB RAM.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-TLS-REG-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "The system shall prepend 'TLS' to all registry names for TLS and DTLS parameters, including Application-Layer Protocol Negotiation (ALPN) Protocol IDs, ExtensionType Values, Heartbeat Message Types, and Heartbeat Modes, to ensure consistency with IETF standards.", 'rationale': "This aligns with IANA's update in RFC 8447, which mandates a consistent naming convention for TLS-related registries to improve clarity and interoperability.", 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a TLS implementation using ALPN, extension types, or heartbeat messages;
WHEN the system is queried for registry names;
THEN all registry names shall include the prefix 'TLS'."}, {'id': 'SEC-TLS-REG-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "The system shall use an 'IETF Review' registration policy for TLS Authorization Data Formats and TLS Supplemental Data Formats (SupplementalDataType), as updated in RFC 8447, to ensure proper governance and review of new entries.", 'rationale': "RFC 8447 updates these registries to 'IETF Review' to align with RFC 8126, which recommends this policy for registries with limited code space and high security implications.", 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a new parameter is proposed for registration in TLS Authorization Data Formats or Supplemental Data Formats;
WHEN the proposal is submitted;
THEN it shall undergo an IETF Review process before being accepted into the registry.'}, {'id': 'SEC-TLS-REG-003', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "The system shall only implement parameters marked as 'Recommended' in the TLS registries (e.g., cipher suites, supported groups, certificate types), and shall not use any parameter with 'Not Recommended' or unmarked status unless explicitly justified for a specific use case.", 'rationale': "The 'Recommended' column in TLS registries indicates parameters that are generally advised for implementation; using non-recommended parameters may expose the system to known vulnerabilities.", 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a new TLS parameter is proposed for use;
WHEN it is not marked as 'Recommended' in the IANA registry;
THEN the implementation shall include a documented justification for its use and a risk assessment indicating why it is acceptable."}, {'id': 'SEC-TLS-REG-004', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'text': 'The system shall support the Session Ticket TLS Extension as defined in RFC 8447, to enable secure session resumption and reduce the risk of session state exposure.', 'rationale': 'The Session Ticket TLS Extension improves session management and reduces the need for full handshake reestablishment, enhancing both performance and security.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client reconnects to the server after a session timeout;
WHEN the server receives a session ticket;
THEN the server shall validate and use the ticket to resume the session without requiring a full handshake.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The designated expert pool shall evaluate each registration request to determine whether it duplicates existing functionality, is likely to be of general applicability, or is useful only for a single application.', 'rationale': 'As per RFC 8447, experts must assess whether a proposed registration adds value beyond existing functionality or is limited to a single use case.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a registration request for a new cipher suite;
WHEN the expert pool evaluates it;
THEN it shall either confirm no duplication and general applicability OR identify a specific instance of duplication or limited applicability.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The registration description shall be clear and complete, and the expert pool shall ensure that the description meets the clarity criteria defined in RFC 8447.', 'rationale': 'Clarity of the registration description is a key criterion for expert evaluation as specified in the source material.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a registration request;
WHEN the expert pool reviews the description;
THEN it shall confirm that the description is clear and complete, and shall reject any request that lacks clarity or completeness.'}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'If a registration request is denied, the decision shall include a written explanation and, if applicable, suggestions for how to make the request successful.', 'rationale': 'RFC 8447 requires that denials include explanations to support transparency and improvement of future requests.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a denied registration request;
WHEN the expert pool issues a decision;
THEN the decision shall include a written explanation and, if applicable, specific suggestions for improvement.'}, {'id': 'SEC-004', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'text': 'All registration requests shall be directed to the <tls-reg-review@ietf.org> mailing list, and IANA shall only accept registry updates from the designated expert pool.', 'rationale': 'The source specifies that IANA must only accept updates from designated experts and that all requests should be sent to the review mailing list.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a registration request;
WHEN it is submitted;
THEN it shall be sent to the <tls-reg-review@ietf.org> mailing list and shall not be accepted by IANA unless approved by the designated expert pool.'}, {'id': 'SEC-005', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "The 'Recommended' status in the registry shall not be interpreted as an endorsement of the cryptographic strength of a cipher suite or supported group.", 'rationale': "The source explicitly states that the 'Recommended' status does not constitute endorsement, and implementers must verify ongoing security.", 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a cipher suite listed with 'Recommended' status;
WHEN an implementer uses it;
THEN they shall independently verify that the algorithm continues to provide expected security levels, as cryptographic advances may render it obsolete over time."}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "The client shall advertise one or more GREASE values in the 'cipher_suites' field of the ClientHello message, using values from the reserved GREASE set: {0x0A,0x0A} through {0xFA,0xFA} as defined in RFC 8701.", 'rationale': 'This ensures that new cipher suites can be introduced without causing interoperability failures due to bugs in implementations that reject unknown values.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client sends a ClientHello with GREASE cipher suite values;
WHEN a server receives the ClientHello;
THEN the server shall ignore any GREASE value and continue the handshake with a valid cipher suite, and the connection shall succeed.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The client shall advertise one or more GREASE extension values in the ClientHello message, using values from the reserved GREASE set: 0x0A0A through 0xFAFA as defined in RFC 8701.', 'rationale': 'This prevents extensibility failures by ensuring that unknown extension values are not treated as errors during handshake negotiation.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client sends a ClientHello with a GREASE extension value;
WHEN a server receives the extension;
THEN the server shall ignore the value and proceed with the handshake using valid extensions, and the connection shall succeed.'}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "The client shall reject any GREASE value when it appears in a ServerHello, HelloRetryRequest, or any extension field in a ServerHello or HelloRetryRequest, including the 'version' field, 'cipher_suite' field, or 'namedcurve' field in a ServerKeyExchange.", 'rationale': 'This ensures that implementations that incorrectly reject unknown values will fail to interoperate, exposing the bug before it spreads across the ecosystem.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a server sends a ServerHello with a GREASE value in the 'version' field;
WHEN the client receives it;
THEN the client shall terminate the handshake and fail the connection with a TLS handshake failure error."}, {'id': 'NON-001', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'text': 'The TLS implementation shall maintain backward compatibility with all known TLS versions and cipher suites defined in RFC 8446 and prior specifications.', 'rationale': 'Ensures that the service remains interoperable with legacy systems and clients that do not support GREASE or newer extensibility features.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client using a legacy TLS version;
WHEN it connects to the service;
THEN the connection shall succeed and operate as per the standard TLS specification without requiring GREASE values.'}]

[{'id': 'KR-01', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The application shall use symmetric-key algorithms to provide confidentiality for data at rest and in transit, with key lengths of at least 128 bits as specified in NIST SP 800-57 Part 1.', 'rationale': 'Symmetric-key algorithms are efficient and suitable for data encryption at rest and in transit. A 128-bit key provides sufficient strength against current and foreseeable attacks.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN user data is stored locally or transmitted via HTTPS;
WHEN the data is encrypted using a symmetric key;
THEN the data shall be unreadable without the correct key, and the key shall not be disclosed to unauthorized entities.'}, {'id': 'KR-02', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The application shall use asymmetric-key algorithms to authenticate end devices and generate digital signatures, with key pairs of at least 2048 bits as specified in NIST SP 800-57 Part 1 and the NSA Commercial National Security Algorithm Suite 2.0.', 'rationale': 'Asymmetric-key algorithms provide secure device authentication and digital signatures. A 2048-bit key pair offers strong resistance to brute-force and factorization attacks, especially in the context of quantum-resistant future threats.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a device attempts to connect to the application;
WHEN the device presents a valid certificate or public key;
THEN the application shall verify the device's identity using asymmetric key authentication and reject unauthorized devices."}, {'id': 'KR-03', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The application shall implement HMACs using a cryptographic hash function (e.g., SHA-256) to provide data origin authentication and integrity protection for transmitted data, as defined in FIPS 198.', 'rationale': 'HMACs provide a secure way to verify data origin and integrity without requiring public-key infrastructure. SHA-256 is a widely approved and secure hash function per FIPS 180.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN data is transmitted from the device to the server;
WHEN the data is signed with an HMAC using a shared symmetric key;
THEN the server shall validate the HMAC and reject any data that fails integrity checks.'}, {'id': 'KR-04', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'text': 'All cryptographic keys shall be generated using cryptographically secure random number generators (CSPRNGs) as defined in NIST SP 800-90A, and stored in a secure, isolated environment (e.g., device secure element or OS keychain).', 'rationale': 'Keys must be generated with true randomness to prevent predictability. Secure storage prevents exposure during device operation or during memory access.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a key is generated for data encryption;
WHEN the key is generated using a CSPRNG;
THEN the key shall not be reproducible or predictable, and it shall be stored in a secure, non-volatile, isolated storage mechanism.'}, {'id': 'KR-05', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The application shall ensure that key material is zeroized when no longer needed, and key compromise shall be handled through a defined recovery or destruction process as per key management best practices.', 'rationale': 'Key zeroization prevents residual data exposure. A defined process ensures that compromised keys are not reused or retained, reducing risk of re-use attacks.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a key is no longer required for use;
WHEN the key is no longer used;
THEN the key material shall be zeroized, and the key shall be removed from all memory and storage.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': "The mobile application shall validate the remote server's certificate during connection establishment by comparing the certificate's public key against a pre-defined pinset.", 'rationale': 'Pinning ensures that only expected certificates are accepted, reducing the risk of MITM attacks via rogue certificates.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a secure connection to the remote server;
WHEN the server presents a certificate;
THEN the app shall validate that the certificate's public key matches one of the pinned keys in the pinset.
AND if no match is found, the app shall reject the connection and notify the user."}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'The application shall pin the leaf certificate of the remote server, with a backup pin for the intermediate CA in case of certificate failure or rotation.', 'rationale': 'Pinning the leaf certificate provides 100% certainty that only intended endpoints are trusted, while the backup pin ensures resilience during certificate rotation or failure.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a certificate rotation event;
WHEN the leaf certificate changes;
THEN the app shall fall back to the intermediate CA pin until a new app update is released.
AND the app shall not accept any certificate issued by a different CA.'}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'requirement': 'The application shall not perform certificate pinning if it cannot securely update the pinset or if updating the pinset requires a full application redeployment.', 'rationale': 'Pinning introduces risk of service outage; if pinset updates are not secure or disruptive, pinning is not recommended to avoid operational disruption.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a scenario where pinset update requires a full app redeployment;
WHEN the app is updated;
THEN pinning shall be disabled unless explicitly authorized by the organization's risk acceptance process."}, {'id': 'SEC-004', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': "The application shall not pin the root CA or any intermediate CA unless explicitly authorized by the organization's risk acceptance process.", 'rationale': 'Pinning root or intermediate CAs increases trust in unintended certificates and exposes the app to broader CA-based vulnerabilities.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a request to pin a root or intermediate CA;
WHEN the request is made;
THEN the app shall reject the request unless the organization has formally approved it through a risk acceptance process.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "The application shall implement SSL certificate pinning using the electron-ssl-pinning library to validate the server's certificate against a known, static pinned certificate.", 'rationale': 'Prevents man-in-the-middle attacks by ensuring the server presents a certificate that matches a known, trusted certificate.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN the application connects to a backend server over HTTPS;
WHEN the server presents a certificate that does not match the pinned certificate;
THEN the connection shall be rejected and an error message shall be displayed to the user.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-001', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': "The application shall allow configuration of pinned certificates via a secure configuration file stored in the user's home directory.", 'rationale': 'Provides flexibility for different deployment environments or certificate updates without requiring code changes.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a configuration file with a pinned certificate;
WHEN the application loads the configuration;
THEN it shall load and use the pinned certificate for validation during HTTPS connections.'}, {'id': 'TEST-001', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'text': 'The application shall include automated testing of certificate pinning using the OWASP Mobile Security Testing Guide (MSTG) network communication testing methodology.', 'rationale': 'Ensures that the pinning implementation is effective and behaves as expected in real-world scenarios.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a test environment with a server presenting a known certificate;
WHEN the application connects to the server;
THEN the connection shall succeed and the certificate shall be validated against the pinned certificate.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'requirement': 'The system shall use the ACME protocol (RFC 8555) to register and manage certificate requests.', 'rationale': "ACME is the standardized protocol for automated certificate issuance and is widely adopted by CAs such as Let's Encrypt and Sectigo.", 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a domain owner submits a certificate request via the web interface;
WHEN the system performs DNS-based validation;
THEN it shall successfully issue a certificate using the ACME protocol and return a valid certificate to the user.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'requirement': 'The system shall validate domain ownership using DNS TXT records with a specific format as defined in RFC 8555 and RFC 6844.', 'rationale': 'DNS TXT records provide a standardized, secure, and widely supported method for domain ownership validation.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a domain owner provides a domain name;
WHEN the system attempts to validate ownership by creating a DNS TXT record;
THEN the record must be successfully created and verified within 10 minutes of issuance.'}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'requirement': 'All issued certificates shall be signed using RSA-2048 or stronger cryptographic algorithms as specified in RFC 7515 and RFC 7518.', 'rationale': 'Strong key sizes are required to ensure long-term security against cryptographic attacks, as per CA/Browser Forum baseline requirements.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a certificate is issued;
WHEN the certificate is validated;
THEN the public key shall be at least 2048 bits in length and use RSA or ECDSA as specified in RFC 7515 and RFC 7518.'}, {'id': 'SEC-004', 'requirement': 'The system shall support certificate renewal automatically, with a minimum of 30 days before expiration, using the ACME protocol.', 'rationale': 'Automated renewal prevents certificate expiration and maintains continuous service availability.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a certificate is issued with a 90-day validity period;
WHEN the system detects that 30 days remain before expiration;
THEN it shall initiate a renewal request via ACME and successfully obtain a new certificate.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'DNSSEC shall be implemented using the Resource Record (RR) types defined in RFC 4034 and RFC 4035, including DNSKEY, DS, NSEC, and AD bits, to ensure authenticated and signed DNS responses.', 'rationale': 'These RR types are the foundational components of DNSSEC as specified in RFC 4034 and RFC 4035, enabling data integrity and authenticity during DNS resolution.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a DNS query for a domain with a valid DNSSEC signature;
WHEN the resolver performs DNSSEC validation;
THEN the response shall be authenticated and the AD bit shall be set to indicate DNSSEC validation was performed.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'All DNSKEY records shall include a secure entry point (SEP) flag as defined in RFC 3757 to prevent unauthorized key usage and ensure key integrity.', 'rationale': 'The SEP flag in DNSKEY records provides a mechanism to protect against misuse of DNS keys by ensuring only authorized keys are used in the signing process.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a DNSKEY record with the SEP flag set;
WHEN a resolver attempts to use the key for signing;
THEN the key shall be considered valid only if the SEP flag is present and correctly interpreted.'}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'text': 'DNSSEC validation shall complete within 100 milliseconds for 95% of queries under normal load conditions.', 'rationale': 'Performance is critical for user experience; a 100ms threshold ensures minimal latency in DNS resolution while maintaining security.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a high-traffic DNS environment with 10,000 concurrent queries;
WHEN DNSSEC validation is performed;
THEN 95% of queries shall complete in under 100 milliseconds.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The DNS infrastructure shall support the DNSKEY resource record to store public keys for domain name signing.', 'rationale': 'DNSKEY is a core DNSSEC resource record that enables authentication of DNS responses via public key cryptography.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a DNS zone with a configured DNSKEY record;
WHEN a DNS query is made;
THEN the DNS server shall return the DNSKEY record in the response and validate its format as defined in RFC 4034.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The DNS infrastructure shall support the RRSIG resource record to provide digital signatures for DNS resource records.', 'rationale': 'RRSIG provides cryptographic signatures for DNS records, ensuring data integrity and authenticity.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a DNS record with an RRSIG signature;
WHEN a DNS query is made;
THEN the DNS server shall validate the signature using the corresponding DNSKEY and return the record only if the signature is valid.'}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The DNS infrastructure shall support the NSEC resource record to authenticate the non-existence of DNS records.', 'rationale': 'NSEC enables authenticated denial of existence, preventing DNS spoofing by proving that a record does not exist in a secure manner.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a DNS query for a non-existent domain name;
WHEN the DNS server responds;
THEN the response shall include an NSEC record that proves the non-existence of the record using a chain of trust.'}, {'id': 'SEC-004', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The DNS infrastructure shall support the DS resource record to establish trust between DNS zones via delegation signing.', 'rationale': 'DS records are used to link parent and child zones, enabling chain-of-trust validation across DNS hierarchies.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a zone delegation from a parent zone to a child zone;
WHEN the child zone is queried;
THEN the parent zone's DS record shall be used to validate the child zone's DNSKEY record."}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'requirement': 'The resolver shall validate new DNSKEY records at a trust point using an existing trust anchor to authenticate the addition of a new trust anchor.', 'rationale': 'This enables automated trust anchor updates without requiring manual intervention, aligning with the mechanism described in RFC 5011.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a DNSKEY record with the Secure Entry Point bit set is added to a trust point and validated by an existing trust anchor;
WHEN the resolver receives the updated DNSKEY record;
THEN the resolver shall add the new key to its set of valid trust anchors for that trust point.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'requirement': 'The resolver shall require knowledge of the private key of a DNSKEY to revoke that key from its trust anchor set.', 'rationale': 'This prevents an attacker from invalidating other trust anchors by removing a key from the trust chain without authorization, as specified in Section 2.1 of RFC 5011.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a DNSKEY has been compromised;
WHEN the zone operator uses the private key to revoke the DNSKEY;
THEN the resolver shall no longer accept that key as a valid trust anchor for the trust point.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-001', 'requirement': 'The DNSKEY record format shall include a single flag bit to support trust anchor update operations.', 'rationale': 'This flag bit enables the mechanism described in RFC 5011 to distinguish between regular and updated trust anchors, as defined in Section 3.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a DNSKEY record is received;
WHEN the flag bit is set;
THEN the resolver shall treat the key as a candidate for trust anchor update and validate it against existing anchors.'}, {'id': 'NON-001', 'requirement': 'The mechanism shall not require changes to DNS resolution behavior, only to resolver management behavior.', 'rationale': 'The mechanism is designed to operate at the resolver management layer, preserving compatibility with existing DNS resolution processes.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a DNS query is made;
WHEN the resolver performs DNSSEC validation;
THEN the resolution behavior remains unchanged, and only the trust anchor management is affected.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The DNS infrastructure shall support the NSEC3 resource record to provide authenticated denial of existence for domain name records.', 'rationale': 'NSEC3 enables authenticated denial of existence, preventing attackers from enumerating domain names by exploiting DNS responses.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a DNS query for a non-existent domain name;
WHEN the authoritative server responds with an NSEC3 record;
THEN the response shall include a valid hash of the next hashed owner name and type bit maps, and the resolver shall validate the chain of trust using the NSEC3PARAM record.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The DNS infrastructure shall use a hash algorithm (e.g., SHA-256) and a minimum of 10 iterations for NSEC3 hashing to prevent collision attacks.', 'rationale': 'Hashing with sufficient iterations and strong algorithms mitigates the risk of collision attacks and ensures cryptographic integrity of the NSEC3 records.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a domain name query with a non-existent record;
WHEN the server computes the NSEC3 hash using SHA-256 with at least 10 iterations;
THEN the resulting hash shall be unique and collision-resistant as defined in RFC 5155, Section 3.1.1 and 3.1.3.'}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The DNS infrastructure shall include an NSEC3PARAM record for each signed zone, specifying the hash algorithm, iteration count, salt length, and salt value.', 'rationale': 'The NSEC3PARAM record defines the parameters for NSEC3 hashing and is required for correct validation of NSEC3 records across the DNS hierarchy.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a DNS zone with a signed zone;
WHEN a resolver queries the zone;
THEN the zone shall respond with an NSEC3PARAM record containing a hash algorithm (e.g., SHA-256), iteration count (≥10), salt length (≥8), and a non-empty salt value.'}, {'id': 'NON-001', 'category': 'Non-functional', 'text': 'The DNS infrastructure shall support backward compatibility with NSEC records during zone transitions from NSEC to NSEC3.', 'rationale': 'Ensures continuity of service during migration and avoids disruption to existing DNS clients and resolvers.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a zone transitioning from NSEC to NSEC3;
WHEN a resolver receives both NSEC and NSEC3 records;
THEN the resolver shall prioritize NSEC3 records and validate them using the NSEC3PARAM record, and shall fall back to NSEC if NSEC3 is not supported.'}]

[{'id': 'SRS-DNS-001', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'The DoH service shall use standard HTTP semantics for DNS queries and responses, ensuring compatibility with existing HTTP-based web infrastructure.', 'rationale': 'HTTP semantics provide a well-understood and widely supported transport layer, enabling broad interoperability with existing web services and clients.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client using standard HTTP methods to send a DNS query;
WHEN the DoH service receives the request;
THEN it shall process the query using standard HTTP semantics and return a valid DNS response in the expected format.'}, {'id': 'SRS-DNS-002', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'The DoH service shall support all DNS queries that would normally be sent over DNS over UDP, including queries that use DNS extensions, but not those requiring multiple responses.', 'rationale': 'This ensures backward compatibility with existing DNS implementations and supports full DNS protocol flexibility.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a DNS query with EDNS options;
WHEN the query is sent via DoH;
THEN the service shall process it and return a response that includes all EDNS options as defined in RFC 6891 and RFC 7858.'}, {'id': 'SRS-DNS-003', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'The DoH service shall permit the addition of new formats for DNS queries and responses, allowing future protocol extensions without requiring major reimplementation.', 'rationale': 'This enables the system to evolve with new DNS features while maintaining backward compatibility and interoperability.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a new DNS extension format defined in a future RFC;
WHEN the extension is used in a query;
THEN the service shall accept and process it, and return a response in a format that supports the extension.'}, {'id': 'SRS-DNS-004', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The DoH service shall use a secure transport layer (HTTPS) that meets the requirements for TLS as defined in RFC 8446, ensuring confidentiality and integrity of DNS queries and responses.', 'rationale': 'HTTPS with TLS ensures that DNS queries and responses are protected from eavesdropping and tampering during transmission.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client attempting to send a DNS query over DoH;
WHEN the connection is established using TLS;
THEN the query and response shall be encrypted and authenticated, and no data shall be visible to unauthorized parties.'}, {'id': 'SRS-DNS-005', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'text': 'The DoH service shall ensure interoperability by specifying a single mandatory format for requests and responses that supports future modifications to the DNS protocol, including the inclusion of one or more EDNS options.', 'rationale': 'A single, mandatory format ensures that all implementations can interoperate and evolve together without breaking compatibility.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN two different implementations of DoH;
WHEN they exchange DNS queries and responses;
THEN the queries and responses shall be correctly parsed and processed by both systems, regardless of future DNS extensions.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-01', 'requirement': 'All OSPF routing protocol exchanges shall be authenticated using a shared key or password mechanism.', 'rationale': 'The source specifies that all OSPF routing protocol exchanges are authenticated, which prevents unauthorized route injection and ensures data integrity.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an OSPF router receives a Link State Advertisement (LSA) from a neighbor;
WHEN the LSA header lacks a valid authentication signature;
THEN the router shall reject the LSA and log the event.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-01', 'requirement': 'Each OSPF router shall maintain a consistent link-state database describing the topology of the Autonomous System.', 'rationale': 'OSPF is a link-state protocol that relies on routers having identical databases to calculate shortest-path trees.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a network topology change;
WHEN a router detects a change in its link state;
THEN it shall update its database and propagate the update to adjacent routers within 30 seconds.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-02', 'requirement': 'OSPF shall support equal-cost multipath routing to distribute traffic across multiple paths of equal cost.', 'rationale': 'The source explicitly states that OSPF provides support for equal-cost multipath, enabling load balancing.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN two equal-cost paths between two routers;
WHEN traffic is routed via the path with the lowest hop count;
THEN at least 50% of the traffic shall be distributed across both paths.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-03', 'requirement': 'OSPF shall support area-based routing with a backbone area (Area 0) and inter-area routing to reduce routing protocol traffic.', 'rationale': 'The source describes area routing as providing an additional level of routing protection and reducing protocol traffic.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a router in Area 1 with a route to a network in Area 2;
WHEN the router receives an inter-area route update;
THEN it shall update its routing table and forward traffic via the designated inter-area path.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'BGP-4 speaking systems shall validate all received UPDATE messages using message header and path attribute integrity checks to prevent unauthorized route advertisements.', 'rationale': 'The BGP-4 specification defines message formats and path attributes that can be used to validate reachability and origin; this ensures that only legitimate ASes contribute to the routing graph.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a BGP-4 UPDATE message with an invalid AS_PATH or malformed header;
WHEN received by a BGP speaker;
THEN the message shall be rejected and a NOTIFICATION message shall be sent with error code 2 (Malformed Message).'}, {'id': 'FUNC-001', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'BGP-4 systems shall advertise network reachability information as IP prefixes, eliminating the use of network classes, and support route aggregation via AS_PATH aggregation.', 'rationale': 'RFC 4271 specifies that BGP-4 supports Classless Inter-Domain Routing (CIDR) and route aggregation, enabling more efficient and flexible routing.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a network prefix (e.g., 2001:db8::/32);
WHEN advertised by a BGP speaker;
THEN the prefix shall be transmitted as a single IP prefix in the UPDATE message, and aggregation of AS_PATHs shall be supported if configured.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-002', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'BGP-4 systems shall implement a finite state machine (FSM) to manage session states and handle TCP connection events, including hold timer expiration and message-based events.', 'rationale': 'The FSM in BGP-4 governs session establishment, maintenance, and failure detection, ensuring reliable peer communication and preventing connection collisions.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a BGP peer connection;
WHEN the hold timer expires without a KEEPALIVE message;
THEN the BGP speaker shall send a NOTIFICATION message with error code 3 (Hold Timer Expired) and enter a disconnected state.'}, {'id': 'NON-001', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'text': 'BGP-4 systems shall minimize route advertisement frequency and reduce routing traffic overhead by aggregating routing information and reducing message size through information reduction techniques.', 'rationale': 'The BGP-4 specification includes mechanisms to reduce routing traffic overhead, which is critical for scalability in large AS environments.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a large AS with hundreds of routes;
WHEN route aggregation is applied;
THEN the number of UPDATE messages shall be reduced by at least 50% compared to unaggregated routing.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-ICMP-ESP-AUTH', 'requirement': 'The network shall use the Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP) header for authentication of ICMPv6 messages when transmitting across untrusted segments.', 'rationale': 'The RFC 4443 revision explicitly recommends ESP for authentication, enhancing integrity and preventing spoofing attacks on ICMPv6 messages.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an ICMPv6 message is transmitted across a segment with untrusted boundaries;
WHEN the message is authenticated using ESP;
THEN the message shall be accepted without integrity violations and shall not be processed by the system if authentication fails.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-ICMP-ECHO-REPLY-ADDR', 'requirement': 'An Echo Reply message in response to an anycast Echo Request shall originate from a unicast address, as specified in RFC 4443.', 'rationale': 'The standard clarifies that the source address of an Echo Reply must be unicast to avoid ambiguity in multicast environments.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an anycast Echo Request is sent to a group of addresses;
WHEN an Echo Reply is generated;
THEN the source address of the Echo Reply shall be a unicast address, not a multicast address.'}, {'id': 'SEC-ICMP-UNAUTH-MSG', 'requirement': 'The system shall allow the administrator to disable the sending of ICMP destination unreachable messages, and this setting shall be configurable via a network policy.', 'rationale': 'The RFC 4443 revision introduces a SHOULD requirement for allowing disabling of such messages, which supports operational flexibility in constrained environments.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a network administrator configures the system to disable ICMP destination unreachable messages;
WHEN the system attempts to send such a message;
THEN the message shall be suppressed and no error notification shall be sent to the source.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-ICMP-SOURCE-SELECT', 'requirement': 'The source address selection for ICMPv6 messages shall follow the simplified procedure defined in RFC 4443, ensuring consistency with the general packet format in Sections 3 and 4.', 'rationale': 'The standard simplifies source address selection to reduce implementation complexity and improve interoperability.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an ICMPv6 message is being generated;
WHEN the source address is selected;
THEN the selection shall follow the simplified procedure in RFC 4443 and shall not result in a non-compliant address assignment.'}]

[{'id': 'RA-FILTER-001', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'The Layer-2 switch shall filter incoming Router Advertisements (RAs) based on a configurable set of criteria, including source address, port, and optional SEND validation status.', 'rationale': 'This ensures that rogue or unauthorized RAs are blocked before reaching end-devices, reducing the risk of spoofing and misconfiguration.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a router attempting to send an RA from an unauthorized source or port;
WHEN the RA is received by a Layer-2 switch configured with RA-Guard;
THEN the RA shall be dropped or blocked, and a log entry shall be generated indicating the source and reason for rejection.'}, {'id': 'RA-FILTER-002', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'The Layer-2 switch shall support a configuration mode that allows administrators to define a list of authorized router addresses from which RAs are permitted to originate.', 'rationale': 'This provides a mechanism to authorize specific routers, enabling fine-grained control over which devices can influence network configuration.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a network administrator configures a list of authorized router addresses;
WHEN a router attempts to send an RA from an unauthorized address;
THEN the RA shall be blocked, and a log entry shall be generated with the unauthorized address and timestamp.'}, {'id': 'RA-FILTER-003', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The RA-Guard mechanism shall not rely on SEND validation for all filtering decisions, as some devices (e.g., sensors) may not support SEND.', 'rationale': 'Ensures compatibility with devices that lack SEND support, maintaining operability in heterogeneous environments.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a sensor device attempts to send an RA;
WHEN the device does not support SEND;
THEN the RA shall be filtered based on source address or port, not SEND validation, and a warning log shall be generated.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-ND-001', 'requirement': 'Neighbor Discovery messages shall not use the IPv6 Fragmentation Header, as defined in RFC 6980, to prevent attackers from concealing malicious payloads through packet fragmentation.', 'rationale': 'The use of IPv6 fragmentation in Neighbor Discovery messages enables attackers to fragment malicious packets, evading detection by layer-2 filtering and monitoring tools such as RA-Guard and NDPMon. Forbidding fragmentation ensures that all packets are transmitted as complete messages, enabling reliable detection and filtering.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a Neighbor Discovery message is sent via IPv6;
WHEN the message is fragmented;
THEN the system shall reject the transmission and log a security event, and the message shall not be processed or forwarded.'}, {'id': 'SEC-ND-002', 'requirement': 'All routers and hosts shall implement strict filtering of Neighbor Discovery messages at the layer-2 level, using mechanisms such as RA-Guard, to prevent rogue router advertisements from reaching end devices.', 'rationale': 'Layer-2 filtering of Neighbor Discovery messages is a proven mitigation technique against spoofing attacks, and its effectiveness is preserved when fragmentation is not used, as complete messages can be validated in full.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a rogue IPv6 Router Advertisement is transmitted;
WHEN it is fragmented into multiple fragments;
THEN the layer-2 device shall detect the fragmentation and block the entire message, or log a security event indicating potential attack.'}, {'id': 'SEC-ND-003', 'requirement': 'Network monitoring tools (e.g., NDPMon, ramond) shall be configured to detect anomalies in Neighbor Discovery traffic, including unexpected message patterns or reassembly failures, and shall trigger alerts when such anomalies are detected.', 'rationale': 'Even though monitoring tools can theoretically reassemble fragmented messages, the process is vulnerable to attacker forgery and is unreliable. Monitoring for anomalies ensures early detection of potential attacks when fragmentation is not used.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a Neighbor Discovery message is sent with unexpected content or sequence;
WHEN the monitoring tool detects a discrepancy between advertised information and local database;
THEN it shall generate a real-time alert and log the event for further investigation.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The network shall support IPv6 authentication and data integrity using defined extension headers, and optional data confidentiality mechanisms.', 'rationale': 'The RFC specifies authentication and privacy capabilities as extensions to IPv6, enabling secure communication at the network layer.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an IPv6 packet with a valid authentication extension header;
WHEN the packet is received by a router;
THEN the packet shall be verified for data integrity and authenticated using the specified mechanisms, and any unauthorized packets shall be dropped.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-001', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'The IPv6 header shall use a simplified format with reduced mandatory fields to minimize packet processing overhead and reduce bandwidth consumption.', 'rationale': 'The RFC specifies header simplification to reduce common-case processing cost and limit header bandwidth usage.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a standard IPv6 packet;
WHEN processed by a router;
THEN the packet shall be forwarded with minimal header processing, and the processing time shall not exceed 10 microseconds for typical packet sizes.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-002', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'The network shall support flow labeling to enable the identification and prioritization of packet flows for quality of service and traffic management.', 'rationale': 'The RFC introduces flow labeling to allow the sender to request that a sequence of packets be treated as a single flow.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a sender that assigns a flow label to a packet;
WHEN the packet is transmitted over the network;
THEN the receiver shall be able to identify the flow and apply consistent forwarding policies based on the label.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-003', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'The network shall support multicast addressing with a scope field to enable hierarchical and scoped multicast routing.', 'rationale': 'The RFC introduces a scope field in multicast addresses to improve scalability and routing of multicast traffic.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a multicast packet sent to a group with a defined scope;
WHEN received by a node in the scope;
THEN the packet shall be delivered to the correct subset of nodes within the scope, and no packets shall be delivered outside the scope.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "IPv6 extension headers, except the Hop-by-Hop Options header, shall not be processed, inserted, or deleted by any node along a packet's delivery path.", 'rationale': 'This aligns with RFC 8200 Section 4, which resolves Erratum ID 4662 by restricting header manipulation to the source node, ensuring integrity and preventing spoofing or tampering.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a packet with an extension header received at an intermediate node;
WHEN the header is not the Hop-by-Hop Options header;
THEN the node shall not process, insert, or delete the header, and the packet shall be forwarded unmodified.'}, {'id': 'FMT-001', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'IPv6 nodes shall not create overlapping fragments during fragmentation and shall silently discard any datagram that contains overlapping fragments upon reassembly.', 'rationale': 'As specified in RFC 8200, Section 4.5, overlapping fragments must be discarded to prevent data corruption and ensure correct reassembly.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a packet with a fragment that has an overlapping offset;
WHEN the packet is received by a node;
THEN the entire datagram and all its fragments shall be silently discarded, and no ICMP error message shall be sent.'}, {'id': 'FMT-002', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'All headers through the first upper-layer header shall be included in the first fragment of an IPv6 datagram.', 'rationale': 'This is required by RFC 8200 to ensure that the full header structure is preserved in the initial fragment, enabling correct reassembly and interpretation at the destination.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a datagram to be fragmented;
WHEN the first fragment is created;
THEN all headers up to and including the first upper-layer header shall be included in that fragment.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "The Hop-by-Hop Options header shall be processed 'may' by nodes, and any processing must follow the guidance in the IANA registry for IPv6 Extension Header Types.", 'rationale': "RFC 8200 updates the Hop-by-Hop Options header from 'must' to 'may', indicating optional processing and requiring adherence to IANA-defined types for consistency.", 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a packet with a Hop-by-Hop Options header;
WHEN a node receives it;
THEN the node may process it, but if it does, it must follow the defined header types in the IANA registry.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'requirement': 'The API shall validate all user inputs to prevent injection attacks, including SQL, command, and script injection, using parameterized queries or input sanitization.', 'rationale': 'Injection attacks are a top OWASP risk category. The NVD dataset shows that 160k CVEs have CVSSv2 scores and 156k have CVSSv3 scores, indicating widespread vulnerability to injection flaws. CVSSv3 impact scores have increased by nearly 1.5 points on average, highlighting the severity of such risks.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user submits an input string via API endpoint;
WHEN the input is processed;
THEN the system shall reject any input containing SQL-like syntax or shell commands and return a 400 Bad Request error with a descriptive message.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'requirement': 'The API shall enforce role-based access control (RBAC) to ensure that users only access data and functions they are authorized to use.', 'rationale': 'Broken Object Level Authorization is a top OWASP risk. The NVD dataset shows that 643 unique CWEs are mapped to CVEs, with significant overlap in authentication and authorization flaws.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user attempts to access a resource;
WHEN the user is not authorized;
THEN the system shall return a 403 Forbidden error and log the unauthorized access attempt.'}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'requirement': 'All API endpoints shall be secured with HTTPS using TLS 1.2 or higher, and all data in transit shall be encrypted end-to-end.', 'rationale': 'Security Misconfiguration is a top OWASP risk. The NVD dataset includes 175k CVEs mapped to CWEs, with many involving insecure transport protocols or misconfigured services.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client attempts to access an API endpoint via HTTP;
WHEN the request is made;
THEN the system shall redirect to HTTPS and return a 301 Moved Permanently response.'}, {'id': 'NON-001', 'requirement': 'The API shall support a maximum of 100 concurrent file uploads and maintain a 99.9% uptime during peak load periods.', 'rationale': 'Performance and availability are critical for external clients. The NVD dataset shows that 220k CVEs have CVSS scores, indicating a large volume of applications under scrutiny for performance and stability issues.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a peak load of 100 concurrent uploads;
WHEN the system is under sustained load;
THEN the API shall remain available with less than 100ms latency and no more than 1% downtime.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-REQ-001', 'category': 'Security', 'description': 'All state-changing requests (e.g., fund transfers, password changes) must include a CSRF token in the request payload.', 'rationale': 'CSRF tokens prevent attackers from tricking authenticated users into performing unauthorized actions via forged requests.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user is authenticated and attempts to transfer funds via an AJAX request;
WHEN the request includes a valid CSRF token;
THEN the backend validates the token against the session and processes the request successfully.
AND WHEN the request lacks a token or contains a mismatched token;
THEN the request is rejected with a 403 Forbidden response and a security event is logged.'}, {'id': 'SEC-REQ-002', 'category': 'Security', 'description': 'CSRF tokens must be generated on the server-side, unique per user session, and secret and unpredictable.', 'rationale': 'Server-generated tokens are more secure than client-generated ones and reduce the risk of token prediction or reuse.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user session is established;
WHEN a new request is made;
THEN a new CSRF token is generated using a cryptographically secure random number generator (CSPRNG) and stored in the session.
AND WHEN a request is made with a token;
THEN the token must be validated against the stored session token value and must match exactly.'}, {'id': 'SEC-REQ-003', 'category': 'Security', 'description': 'The application shall not use GET requests for any state-changing operations.', 'rationale': 'GET requests are vulnerable to CSRF because they are not designed to carry state-changing data and are easily forged in cross-site contexts.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user attempts to initiate a fund transfer via a GET request;
THEN the request must be rejected with a 405 Method Not Allowed response and a security event logged.'}, {'id': 'SEC-REQ-004', 'category': 'Security', 'description': 'If the application uses modern browsers, it shall validate the origin of requests using standard headers (e.g., Origin, Sec-Fetch-Site) to verify the request source.', 'rationale': 'Standard headers provide a reliable way to verify that requests originate from the intended domain, reducing the risk of forged requests.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a request is received from a domain other than the application's origin;
WHEN the request includes a state-changing operation;
THEN the request shall be rejected with a 403 Forbidden response and a security event logged."}]

[{'id': 'SEC-REQ-001', 'category': 'Security', 'description': 'All non-GET HTTP requests (POST, PUT, PATCH) to the API must include a CSRF token in the request header.', 'rationale': "CSRF attacks can be mitigated by ensuring that each request includes a token derived from the user's session, which is only available to legitimate users.", 'testable_acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a React component making a POST request to '/users/profile' with valid user data;
WHEN the request is sent;
THEN the request header must contain the 'X-CSRF-Token' value extracted from the 'XSRF-TOKEN' cookie."}, {'id': 'SEC-REQ-002', 'category': 'Security', 'description': "The CSRF token must be extracted from the browser's cookie store using the cookie name 'XSRF-TOKEN'.", 'rationale': 'The token is stored in a browser cookie, and the frontend must reliably parse and retrieve it using standard cookie parsing logic.', 'testable_acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a browser with a cookie named 'XSRF-TOKEN' set to 'abc123';
WHEN the application runs in a browser;
THEN the CSRF token extraction function must return 'abc123'."}, {'id': 'FUNC-REQ-001', 'category': 'Functional', 'description': "The React application must use a CSRF-protected Axios instance configured with a base URL of '/api'.", 'rationale': 'The application must be able to communicate with the backend API using a consistent and secure endpoint structure.', 'testable_acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN an API configuration with baseURL: '/api';
WHEN a POST request is made to '/users/profile';
THEN the request must be sent to 'https://example.com/api/users/profile'."}, {'id': 'NON-REQ-001', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'description': 'CSRF token extraction must not introduce more than 100ms of latency in request processing.', 'rationale': 'Performance impacts on user experience must be minimized, especially during profile updates which are frequent actions.', 'testable_acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user updating their profile;
WHEN the update request is processed;
THEN the total time from request initiation to response receipt must not exceed 100ms.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-DB-01', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'The PostgreSQL database server shall only bind to localhost and shall not accept network connections on any external interface.', 'rationale': 'Prevents unauthorized access from external hosts and reduces the attack surface by limiting database access to the application server.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN the database is configured to bind only to localhost;
WHEN a network scan is performed from an external host;
THEN no database port is accessible.'}, {'id': 'SEC-DB-02', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'All database connections shall use TLSv1.2 or higher with modern ciphers (e.g., AES-GCM or ChaCha20) and require client certificate verification.', 'rationale': 'Ensures encrypted communication between the application server and the database, preventing sensitive data from being transmitted in clear text.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN the application server connects to the database;
WHEN the connection is established;
THEN the connection uses TLSv1.2+ with AES-GCM or ChaCha20 and the server certificate is verified by the client.'}, {'id': 'SEC-DB-03', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'Database user accounts shall be created with the principle of least privilege and shall only have the minimum permissions required for the application to function.', 'rationale': 'Reduces the risk of privilege escalation and limits the impact of compromised accounts by restricting access to only necessary operations (e.g., SELECT, UPDATE, DELETE).', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a database user account is created;
WHEN the application performs a file upload or retrieval;
THEN the account has only the required permissions (e.g., SELECT and UPDATE) and does not have administrative rights or access to system-level functions.'}, {'id': 'SEC-DB-04', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'Database credentials shall not be stored in source code or version control systems and shall be stored in a configuration file outside the web root with restricted file permissions.', 'rationale': 'Prevents credential exposure during development, deployment, or code sharing, reducing the risk of unauthorized access to the database.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a configuration file containing database credentials is used;
WHEN the file is accessed;
THEN it is outside the web root and readable only by the application server process.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-DES-01', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'All user-submitted data shall be validated and parsed using JSON format via `json_decode()` or equivalent standard library functions before any deserialization process begins.', 'rationale': "Using standard JSON serialization avoids the use of vulnerable native deserialization mechanisms (e.g., Python's pickle, Java's ObjectInputStream) that can be exploited for remote code execution.", 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user submits a string containing untrusted data;
WHEN the API receives the data;
THEN it shall parse the data using JSON and reject any input that fails to parse as valid JSON, and no native deserialization functions shall be invoked.'}, {'id': 'SEC-DES-02', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': "No backend service shall use Python's `pickle.loads()` or `PyYAML.load()` with user-controlled input unless the input is explicitly validated and sanitized to prevent execution of arbitrary code.", 'rationale': "Both `pickle.loads()` and `PyYAML.load()` are known to be vulnerable to remote code execution when used with untrusted data, especially with patterns like `!!python/object/apply` or `cos.system(S'dir')tR.`.", 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a user submits a string containing a `!!python/object/apply` or `cos.system(S'dir')tR.` pattern;
WHEN the input is processed;
THEN the API shall reject the input and return a 400 Bad Request error without attempting to deserialize it."}, {'id': 'SEC-DES-03', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'All Java application objects that implement `Serializable` shall have a `private final void readObject()` method that throws an `IOException` to prevent deserialization of any object that is not explicitly allowed.', 'rationale': "The default behavior of Java's `readObject()` can be exploited in 'billion laughs' or arbitrary object deserialization attacks. A final `readObject()` method prevents unauthorized deserialization.", 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a user submits data that would trigger deserialization of a non-allowed object;
WHEN the API attempts to deserialize it;
THEN it shall throw an `IOException` with message 'Cannot be deserialized' and return a 500 error."}, {'id': 'SEC-DES-04', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'All sensitive data members (e.g., financial values, personal identifiers) in serialized objects shall be declared as `private transient` to prevent data leakage during serialization or deserialization.', 'rationale': 'The `transient` keyword ensures that sensitive fields are not serialized or deserialized, reducing the risk of data exposure in untrusted data flows.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a class with sensitive fields (e.g., profit, margin);
WHEN the class is serialized or deserialized;
THEN the sensitive fields shall not be included in the output, and the object shall not contain the field values in any serialized form.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-DES-01', 'requirement': 'The application shall only deserialize data objects that are signed with a valid, pre-defined cryptographic signature prior to deserialization.', 'rationale': "Preventing deserialization of untrusted data reduces the risk of remote code execution via object deserialization vulnerabilities, as outlined in OWASP's 'Deserialization of Untrusted Data' vulnerability.", 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user submits a data object via the API;
WHEN the object is received and validated;
THEN the API shall reject the object if it lacks a valid cryptographic signature, and shall not perform deserialization.'}, {'id': 'SEC-DES-02', 'requirement': 'The application shall use a secure deserialization library that supports allowlists of trusted object types and prevents execution of untrusted classes.', 'rationale': 'Using a secure deserialization library such as SerialKiller or NotSoSerial mitigates risks associated with unsafe object deserialization, particularly in Java environments.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a data object is received;
WHEN it is deserialized;
THEN the deserialization process shall only allow objects defined in a pre-configured allowlist, and shall not instantiate or execute any object outside that list.'}, {'id': 'SEC-DES-03', 'requirement': 'The application shall validate all data objects before deserialization to ensure they conform to a known schema and do not contain malicious payloads.', 'rationale': 'Malicious payloads can be embedded in serialized data, and schema validation helps detect anomalies before deserialization occurs.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a data object is received;
WHEN it is validated against a schema;
THEN any object that deviates from the schema shall be rejected before deserialization, and a clear error message shall be returned to the client.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'All input fields for personally identifiable information (PII) and login credentials (username, password) shall use the HTML5 attributes `spellcheck="false"`, `autocomplete="off"`, `autocorrect="off"`, and `autocapitalize="off"` to prevent browser-based caching and autocomplete of sensitive data.', 'rationale': 'Browser autocomplete and caching can expose credentials to subsequent users on public computers, leading to unauthorized access. These attributes disable browser assistance in storing or suggesting input values.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user submits a login form with username and password on a public computer;
WHEN the form fields are configured with `spellcheck="false"`, `autocomplete="off"`, `autocorrect="off"`, and `autocapitalize="off"`;
THEN the browser shall not cache or suggest the input values for username or password, and no subsequent user shall be able to recover the credentials via browser history or autocomplete.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The application shall implement the HTTP header `X-Frame-Options` with a value of `deny` to prevent Clickjacking attacks and unsolicited framing of the application.', 'rationale': 'The `X-Frame-Options` header mitigates Clickjacking by preventing the application from being embedded in iframes, which could be exploited to trick users into performing unintended actions.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN the application is loaded in an iframe;
WHEN the `X-Frame-Options: deny` header is sent;
THEN the browser shall block the iframe from loading the application, preventing it from being embedded in a malicious page.'}, {'id': 'INF-001', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'text': 'The application shall not use the HTML5 Application Cache (`<html manifest="...">` and `.appcache` files) due to its removal from major browsers. All offline functionality shall be implemented using Service Workers with the Cache API.', 'rationale': 'The HTML5 Application Cache has been deprecated and removed from all major browsers, making it insecure and non-functional in modern environments.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN the application is accessed via a browser;
WHEN the application attempts to use the HTML5 Application Cache;
THEN the browser shall reject the usage and the application shall fall back to Service Workers with the Cache API for offline support.'}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'All responses containing sensitive data (e.g., account balances, personal information) shall include the HTTP header `Cache-Control: no-store` to prevent caching by the browser or service workers.', 'rationale': 'Caching sensitive data could allow unauthorized access to information by malicious actors who gain access to cached responses or browser storage.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a request for a page containing sensitive financial data;
WHEN the response includes `Cache-Control: no-store`;
THEN the browser and any service worker shall not cache the response, and the data shall not be retained in any form of local storage.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The API gateway shall enforce edge-level authorization using mutual authentication (mTLS) to prevent direct, anonymous connections to internal microservices.', 'rationale': 'As per NIST recommendations, mutual authentication at the edge mitigates API gateway bypass risks and ensures only authenticated clients can reach internal services.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a request to an internal microservice endpoint;
WHEN the request is made without valid mTLS credentials;
THEN the API gateway shall reject the request with a 401 Unauthorized response and log the attempt.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'Each microservice shall implement service-level authorization using either a decentralized pattern (PDP and PEP at service code level) or a centralized pattern with an embedded PDP, with access control rules defined via a Policy Administration Point (PAP).', 'rationale': 'Service-level authorization provides granular control and supports dynamic policy enforcement, aligning with NIST SP 800-162 access control components.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user request to a microservice endpoint with authorization metadata;
WHEN the microservice evaluates the request against stored access control rules;
THEN the microservice shall enforce authorization and return a 403 Forbidden response if access is denied, or 200 OK if allowed.'}, {'id': 'PERF-001', 'category': 'Performance', 'text': 'The microservice shall cache authorization policy decisions for up to 10 minutes to reduce latency caused by network calls to a centralized PDP.', 'rationale': 'Caching mitigates the performance impact of remote PDP calls, improving response times in high-volume environments.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a high-volume request stream to a microservice;
WHEN the microservice retrieves an authorization decision from cache;
THEN the response time shall be less than 50 milliseconds.'}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The centralized Policy Decision Point (PDP) shall operate in a high-availability mode with at least two active instances to ensure resilience and service continuity.', 'rationale': 'A single PDP instance is a single point of failure; high availability ensures continuous access control decision capability during outages.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a failure of one PDP instance;
WHEN the system attempts to make an authorization decision;
THEN the request shall be routed to the remaining active instance and processed without interruption.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The mobile application shall authenticate users server-side using OAuth2 or JWT tokens, and shall not store user passwords or sensitive credentials on the device.', 'rationale': 'Prevents credential theft and ensures authentication is not compromised by client-side attacks or tampering.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user attempts to log in with a password;
WHEN the app receives the credentials;
THEN the backend shall validate the credentials and issue a JWT token;
AND the app shall not store the password or token in plaintext on the device.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'All data transmitted between the mobile app and backend shall be encrypted in transit using HTTPS with strong cipher suites and certificates signed by a trusted CA.', 'rationale': 'Prevents interception of sensitive financial data during network communication.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN the app sends financial data to the backend;
WHEN the data is transmitted over the network;
THEN the transmission shall use HTTPS;
AND the cipher suite shall use at least 128-bit keys;
AND the certificate shall be issued by a trusted CA.'}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'Sensitive data stored on the device (e.g., transaction history) shall be encrypted at rest using platform-specific secure storage (e.g., iOS Keychain, Android Keystore), with keys derived from user login credentials.', 'rationale': 'Protects data from unauthorized access if the device is lost or compromised.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a user stores transaction data on the device;
WHEN the device is accessed by an unauthorized party;
THEN the data shall be inaccessible without the user's login credentials."}, {'id': 'SEC-004', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'text': 'The app shall not cache sensitive data such as account balances or transaction details, and shall not log such data in any form.', 'rationale': 'Prevents data leakage through background snapshots or logs.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN the app performs a background operation;
WHEN it accesses sensitive financial data;
THEN no cached or logged version of the data shall be created or retained.'}, {'id': 'SEC-005', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The app shall require re-authentication before performing sensitive operations such as transferring money or updating payment information.', 'rationale': 'Ensures that only authorized users can perform high-risk actions, reducing the risk of unauthorized transactions.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user attempts to transfer money;
WHEN the operation is initiated;
THEN the app shall prompt for re-authentication using a valid session token or biometric method.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'Siri intents that expose sensitive functionality (e.g., fund transfers, account access) shall require user authentication before execution. This shall be enforced by setting `requiresUserAuthentication` to `true` in the intent configuration.', 'rationale': 'Prevents unauthorized actions when the device is locked by ensuring authentication via Face ID, Touch ID, or PIN is required before executing sensitive commands.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a device is locked and a sensitive Siri intent is invoked;
WHEN the user attempts to execute the command;
THEN the device shall prompt for biometric authentication or PIN before proceeding, and no action shall be taken until authentication is completed.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'For any operation that requires explicit user confirmation, the app shall set `INIntent.userConfirmationRequired = true` to ensure the user explicitly approves the action before execution.', 'rationale': 'Provides an additional layer of user control, ensuring that sensitive actions are not executed without explicit user intent.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user initiates a sensitive action via Siri;
WHEN the action requires confirmation;
THEN the user shall be prompted to confirm the action, and the action shall only proceed after explicit user approval.'}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'All deep links used by the app shall include authentication checks on the view controllers or endpoints accessed. Unauthorized users shall be redirected to the login screen, preventing direct access to secure app sections.', 'rationale': 'Prevents bypass of authentication through deep links, which could otherwise allow unauthorized access to secure app features.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user navigates to a deep link (e.g., `msauth://...`);
WHEN the link is accessed without authentication;
THEN the user shall be redirected to the login screen, and no sensitive content shall be displayed.'}, {'id': 'SEC-004', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'All parameters received via deep links shall be sanitized and validated to prevent injection attacks. Universal Links shall be configured using apple-app-site-association files to ensure secure deep linking.', 'rationale': 'Mitigates injection vulnerabilities and ensures secure, authenticated access via deep links.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a deep link with unvalidated or malicious parameters;
WHEN the link is processed;
THEN the parameters shall be sanitized and validated, and any malformed or suspicious input shall trigger a redirect to the login screen.'}, {'id': 'SEC-005', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'Widgets on the lock screen shall not display sensitive data. For iOS 17 and higher, the app shall use `WidgetInfo.isLocked` to detect lock screen state and mask sensitive content when the device is locked.', 'rationale': 'Prevents exposure of sensitive information when the device is not unlocked.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a widget is displayed on the lock screen;
WHEN the device is locked;
THEN sensitive data shall be masked or hidden, and only non-sensitive content shall be shown.'}, {'id': 'SEC-006', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'All cryptographic keys used in the app shall be created using `SecKeyCreateRandomKey` with `kSecAttrTokenID` set to `kSecAttrTokenIDSecureEnclave` to ensure they are stored in the Secure Enclave hardware.', 'rationale': 'Ensures keys are stored in a secure, isolated hardware environment and never leave the device.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a key is created for encryption;
WHEN it is generated using `SecKeyCreateRandomKey` with `kSecAttrTokenIDSecureEnclave`;
THEN the key shall remain within the Secure Enclave and shall not be accessible to the app's main process."}, {'id': 'SEC-007', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'Biometric operations (e.g., authentication for key access) shall use `LAContext` with `evaluatePolicy` to perform authentication directly through the Secure Enclave, without exposing biometric data to the app.', 'rationale': 'Prevents biometric data from being exposed to the app or stored in memory, reducing privacy and security risks.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a biometric authentication request is made;
WHEN `LAContext.evaluatePolicy` is used;
THEN the biometric data shall not be exposed to the app, and authentication shall occur within the Secure Enclave.'}, {'id': 'SEC-008', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'App Attest API (iOS 14+) shall be used to generate and verify attestation keys and assertions server-side to validate app integrity and detect tampering.', 'rationale': 'Provides a mechanism to verify that the app has not been tampered with and is running the expected version.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an app attestation request is made;
WHEN an assertion is generated via `DCAppAttestService`;
THEN the assertion shall be verified server-side, and any tampering or unauthorized version shall be detected and rejected.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-01', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The application shall avoid direct execution of OS commands using user-supplied input. Instead, it shall use built-in library functions (e.g., java.net.URLConnection in Java) to fetch data from a URL, to prevent OS command injection.', 'rationale': 'Direct OS command execution (e.g., system(), Runtime.exec) is vulnerable to command injection attacks. Using built-in libraries eliminates the risk of executing unintended commands via user input.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user submits a URL via the API;
WHEN the application uses a built-in library function to fetch data;
THEN no OS command is executed, and no shell metacharacters are interpreted.'}, {'id': 'SEC-02', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'If direct OS command execution is unavoidable, the application shall escape user input using OS-specific escaping functions (e.g., escapeshellarg in PHP, or ProcessBuilder in Java) to prevent argument injection.', 'rationale': 'Without proper escaping, user input containing shell metacharacters (e.g., & | ;) can modify the command execution flow, leading to unintended command execution or information disclosure.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user submits a URL containing shell metacharacters (e.g., & | ;);
WHEN the input is passed to a system command;
THEN the input is properly escaped so that metacharacters are not interpreted as command operators.'}, {'id': 'SEC-03', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The application shall validate user input against an allowlist of allowed characters and length constraints. The input shall only contain lowercase letters, numbers, and valid URL syntax, with a maximum length of 100 characters.', 'rationale': 'Allowlist validation prevents injection of malicious metacharacters (e.g., $ > < ` \) that could be used to alter command execution or exploit system vulnerabilities.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user submits a URL;
WHEN the input contains metacharacters outside the allowlist (e.g., $, |, &) or exceeds 100 characters;
THEN the request shall be rejected with a 400 Bad Request error and no command execution shall occur.'}, {'id': 'SEC-04', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The application shall run with the minimum required privileges to perform its tasks, and shall not execute commands with elevated system privileges.', 'rationale': 'Running with minimal privileges limits the potential damage from any compromised execution, reducing the risk of privilege escalation and system compromise.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN the application is running in a production environment;
WHEN a command injection vulnerability is exploited;
THEN the attacker shall not be able to execute commands with elevated privileges or access system-level resources.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-01', 'category': 'Functionality', 'text': "The API shall accept a POST request with a JSON body containing a 'url' field and return the fetched data in a JSON response with a 200 OK status code.", 'rationale': 'This defines the expected input and output format for the API endpoint to ensure consistent and predictable behavior.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a valid URL is submitted in a POST request;
WHEN the API processes the request;
THEN it returns a JSON response containing the fetched data with a 200 OK status code.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'All database queries that use user-supplied input must use parameterized queries to prevent SQL injection.', 'rationale': 'SQL Injection is a critical vulnerability that allows attackers to execute arbitrary SQL commands. Parameterized queries separate SQL logic from user input, ensuring that input is treated as data, not executable code.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a user submits a product name via a search form;
WHEN the application constructs a database query using that input;
THEN the query must use parameterized placeholders (e.g., '?' or ':name') and the input must be bound to the query as a parameter, not concatenated into the SQL string."}, {'id': 'FUNC-001', 'category': 'Functional', 'requirement': 'The application shall allow users to search for products by name and return a list of matching product details.', 'rationale': 'The core functionality of the application is to provide a product search feature to users.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a user enters a product name in the search field;
WHEN the user clicks 'Search';
THEN the application shall retrieve and display product details that match the input name."}, {'id': 'NON-001', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'requirement': 'All database queries must be executed on the server side, and no client-side query construction is permitted.', 'rationale': 'Client-side query construction using string concatenation is a known vector for SQL injection attacks.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user attempts to submit a search query via JavaScript or client-side code;
THEN the query must not be executed on the client; instead, it must be sent to the server and processed there.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'All REST API endpoints must use HTTPS to protect data in transit and prevent interception of authentication credentials such as API keys or JSON Web Tokens.', 'rationale': 'HTTPS ensures confidentiality and integrity of data transmitted between client and server, preventing man-in-the-middle attacks and protecting sensitive information.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client makes a request to any API endpoint;
WHEN the request is sent over HTTP;
THEN the system shall reject the request with a 400 Bad Request error and log the attempt.
AND GIVEN a client makes a request over HTTPS;
THEN the system shall accept the request and process it as normal.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': "All API endpoints must perform access control to verify the caller's authorization before allowing any operation (e.g., create, read, update, delete).", 'rationale': 'Access control prevents unauthorized users from modifying or accessing sensitive account data, aligning with REST best practices and reducing attack surface.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user attempts to perform an action on an account resource;
WHEN the user is not authenticated or lacks sufficient permissions;
THEN the system shall return a 401 Unauthorized or 403 Forbidden response, respectively, and not allow the operation to proceed.'}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': "If JWTs are used for authentication, they must be integrity-protected using a cryptographic signature (preferred) or MAC, and must not use the 'alg: none' value.", 'rationale': "Using a signature or MAC ensures JWT integrity; the 'none' algorithm provides no protection and is vulnerable to tampering.", 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a JWT is sent in an authorization header;
WHEN the 'alg' value is 'none';
THEN the system shall reject the token and return a 401 Unauthorized response.
AND GIVEN a JWT is signed with a cryptographic signature;
THEN the system shall verify the signature using a configured key and validate the claims before allowing access."}, {'id': 'SEC-004', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'All JWTs used for access control must include at least the following standard claims: issuer (iss), audience (aud), expiration time (exp), not before time (nbf), and optionally a unique identifier (jti).', 'rationale': 'Standard claims provide a consistent and verifiable way to validate the origin, validity, and identity of a token, reducing the risk of misconfiguration or spoofing.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a JWT is received;
WHEN it lacks any of the claims (iss, aud, exp, nbf, jti);
THEN the system shall reject the token and return a 401 Unauthorized response.'}, {'id': 'SEC-005', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'When a user explicitly logs out or session times out, a unique token identifier (jti) must be submitted to a denylist to invalidate the JWT for all future requests until the token expires.', 'rationale': 'This prevents session fixation and token replay attacks by ensuring that a revoked token cannot be reused after logout or idle timeout.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user performs an explicit logout;
WHEN the system generates a jti and submits it to a denylist;
THEN the system shall prevent any future requests using that jti from being processed, even if the token has not expired.'}, {'id': 'NON-001', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'requirement': 'The API must support standard HTTP verbs (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE) for CRUD operations on account resources.', 'rationale': 'Standard HTTP verbs align with REST principles and ensure consistency across services and clients.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client sends a GET request to /accounts/123;
WHEN the request is valid;
THEN the system shall return a 200 OK response with the account data.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-REQ-01', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'Sensitive data, such as account numbers or personal identifiers, shall not be included in URLs or query parameters. Sensitive data must be transferred only in the request body or headers, and only when explicitly authorized.', 'rationale': 'Prevents exposure of sensitive data through URL parameters or query strings, which can be logged or intercepted.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a request to retrieve account information via GET method;
WHEN the request includes an account number in the URL;
THEN the system shall return a 400 Bad Request error and reject the request.'}, {'id': 'SEC-REQ-02', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'All API responses shall use the semantically appropriate HTTP status code based on the operation outcome.', 'rationale': 'Proper status codes improve error visibility, support client-side handling, and help prevent security misconfigurations.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a successful creation of a new account;
WHEN the POST request is processed correctly;
THEN the system shall return a 201 Created response with a Location header containing the URI of the new resource.'}, {'id': 'SEC-REQ-03', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'For requests that fail due to unauthorized access, the system shall return a 401 Unauthorized response, and for requests where the user has permission but cannot access the resource, a 403 Forbidden response shall be returned.', 'rationale': 'Ensures clear and consistent error messaging that helps clients understand access issues without revealing internal system details.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a request to access a restricted account resource;
WHEN the user is authenticated but lacks permission;
THEN the system shall return a 403 Forbidden response with no detailed error message.'}, {'id': 'SEC-REQ-04', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'text': 'The API shall return a 413 Payload Too Large error when a file upload exceeds the maximum allowed size of 10 MB.', 'rationale': 'Prevents denial-of-service attacks from large file uploads and ensures predictable resource usage.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a file upload request with a file size greater than 10 MB;
WHEN the request is processed;
THEN the system shall return a 413 Payload Too Large response.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'All database accounts used by the application shall be granted only the minimum required privileges (least privilege principle).', 'rationale': 'Minimizing database account privileges reduces the potential damage from successful SQL injection or unauthorized access attempts.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a database account with read-only access to a user table;
WHEN an attacker attempts to inject malicious SQL;
THEN the database only allows the read access defined by the account, and no write or administrative operations are permitted.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'Each web application shall use a dedicated database user account with restricted access, rather than a shared admin account.', 'rationale': 'Separating database users by application reduces the attack surface and enables fine-grained access control.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a login page and a sign-up page;
WHEN each page connects to the database via a separate user account;
THEN the login page has only read access to username and password fields, and the sign-up page has insert access only, with no write or delete privileges.'}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'All database queries shall be constructed using parameterized queries (e.g., prepared statements or stored procedures), and no SQL queries shall be built by string concatenation.', 'rationale': 'Parameterized queries prevent SQL injection by separating SQL logic from user input data.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN user input such as a username or password;
WHEN the input is passed to a database query;
THEN the query is executed using parameterized statements, and no SQL injection is possible via input manipulation.'}, {'id': 'SEC-004', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'If the application stores passwords in plaintext, it shall not be permitted; passwords shall be stored using a strong cryptographic hash (e.g., bcrypt or PBKDF2).', 'rationale': 'Storing passwords in plaintext exposes them to unauthorized access, even if database access is restricted.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user creates an account;
WHEN the password is stored in the database;
THEN it is hashed using a secure algorithm (e.g., bcrypt) and not stored in plaintext.'}, {'id': 'SEC-005', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'A SQL view shall be created to expose only the hashed password field to all application users, and no direct access to the password field shall be allowed in the underlying table.', 'rationale': 'Views limit access to sensitive data, ensuring that even if an attacker gains access to the database, they can only retrieve the password hash, not the plaintext password.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a database query is made to retrieve user data;
WHEN the query uses a view;
THEN the output includes only the hashed password field, and no direct access to the password field is available in the underlying table.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'requirement': 'The server shall use the Set-Cookie header field to send session identifiers to the user agent, with the HttpOnly attribute set to true to prevent access via client-side scripts.', 'rationale': 'Prevents cross-site scripting (XSS) attacks by ensuring session tokens are not exposed to JavaScript.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user logs in to the application;
WHEN the server sends a Set-Cookie header;
THEN the cookie must include the HttpOnly attribute and no JavaScript code should be able to access the session token value.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'requirement': 'The server shall set the Secure attribute in the Set-Cookie header to ensure cookies are only transmitted over HTTPS connections.', 'rationale': 'Prevents interception of session tokens during transmission over unsecured channels.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user accesses the application via HTTP;
WHEN the server attempts to send a session cookie;
THEN the response must redirect to HTTPS or the cookie must not be sent.'}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'requirement': 'The server shall set the Max-Age attribute in the Set-Cookie header to define a session lifetime, with a default value of 3600 seconds (1 hour), and must allow configuration via application settings.', 'rationale': 'Limits session exposure by enforcing a time-bound session validity period, reducing risk of session fixation or replay attacks.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user logs in;
WHEN the server issues a session cookie;
THEN the cookie must have a Max-Age value of at least 3600 seconds and must be configurable within the application.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-001', 'requirement': 'The user agent shall send the Cookie header field in subsequent requests to include the session identifier when the request path matches the path specified in the Set-Cookie header.', 'rationale': 'Ensures session state is maintained across HTTP requests in a stateless protocol.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user navigates to a protected page;
WHEN the request is made;
THEN the Cookie header must be included with the session identifier if the path matches the configured path.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'requirement': 'The web application shall enforce a same-origin policy such that any script injected into an HTML document shall only have authority over objects that the script can designate, and shall not have access to sensitive user data such as medical records.', 'rationale': 'This prevents attackers from gaining unauthorized access to sensitive data through cross-site scripting (XSS) attacks by limiting script authority to designated objects only.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user uploads a medical record to the API AND an attacker injects a script into an HTML document hosted by a third party;
WHEN the script runs in the browser;
THEN the script shall not access or retrieve any user medical records, and the origin authority shall be restricted to only the objects the script can designate.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'requirement': "The application shall use the 'Origin' header in HTTP requests to define the origin of the request and shall validate that the origin matches the expected domain structure.", 'rationale': "The 'Origin' header is standardized in HTTP and is used to enforce origin-based security boundaries, preventing unauthorized access between domains.", 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a request from a client to the API;
WHEN the request includes an 'Origin' header;
THEN the server shall validate the header and reject any request from a domain that does not match the expected origin authority."}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'requirement': 'The application shall support IDNA2008 (RFC 5890) for internationalized domain name handling and shall not allow domain name mappings that result in unintended security boundary changes.', 'rationale': 'Using IDNA2008 ensures consistent and secure domain name representation, avoiding potential security boundary erosion that could occur when migrating from IDNA2003 to IDNA2008.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a domain name with U+00DF or other international characters;
WHEN the domain is resolved by the user agent;
THEN the domain name shall be mapped using IDNA2008, and no security boundary shall be created or destroyed as a result of the mapping.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The service shall register the SETTINGS_ENABLE_CONNECT_PROTOCOL setting (Code: 0x8) with an initial value of 0 and shall only enable the connect protocol when explicitly configured by the server administrator.', 'rationale': 'This setting is defined in the HTTP/2 Settings registry and is used to control the enablement of the connect protocol during the handshake. Setting it to 0 by default ensures backward compatibility and prevents unintended protocol behavior.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client attempts to establish a connection via HTTP/2;
WHEN the SETTINGS_ENABLE_CONNECT_PROTOCOL is set to 0;
THEN the server shall not initiate a connect protocol handshake and shall respond with a standard HTTP/2 error if the request attempts to use the connect protocol.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The service shall register the WebSocket upgrade token (Value: websocket) in the HTTP Upgrade Tokens registry and shall only allow upgrades to the WebSocket protocol when the token is correctly specified and validated.', 'rationale': 'The WebSocket protocol is defined in RFC 6455 and is the only expected version for the upgrade token, ensuring interoperability and preventing spoofing of upgrade requests.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a client sends an HTTP/2 upgrade request with the token 'websocket';
WHEN the server receives the request;
THEN the server shall validate the token and respond with a valid WebSocket handshake if the token is correct and the connection is secure."}, {'id': 'FUNC-001', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'The service shall support the transition from an HTTP/2 connection to a WebSocket connection using the standard HTTP/2 upgrade mechanism.', 'rationale': 'This is the standard method defined in RFC 7230 and RFC 6455 for initiating WebSocket connections over HTTP/2.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a client initiates an HTTP/2 connection;
WHEN the client sends a request with the upgrade token 'websocket';
THEN the server shall respond with a WebSocket handshake message and establish a secure, bidirectional connection."}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "The server shall include a Cache-Control header with the 'no-store' directive for any response containing sensitive user data, such as passwords or personal identification information.", 'rationale': 'Prevents sensitive information from being cached and exposed to unauthorized users via shared caches or browser private caches.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a request for a user profile containing personal data;
WHEN the server responds;
THEN the response header shall include 'Cache-Control: no-store'."}, {'id': 'FUNC-001', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': "The server shall include a Cache-Control header with the 'max-age' directive to specify the freshness lifetime of cached responses for static content, such as images and CSS files, with a value of 3600 seconds (1 hour).", 'rationale': 'Enables efficient reuse of static content without requiring validation, improving performance and reducing server load.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a request for a static image file;
WHEN the server responds;
THEN the response header shall include 'Cache-Control: max-age=3600'."}, {'id': 'FUNC-002', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': "The server shall include a Cache-Control header with the 'must-revalidate' directive for any response that requires validation before reuse, such as dynamic content generated per user session.", 'rationale': 'Ensures that stale content is not served without validation, maintaining data consistency and integrity for time-sensitive content.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a request for a user-specific dynamic page;
WHEN the server responds;
THEN the response header shall include 'Cache-Control: must-revalidate'."}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "The server shall not cache responses to authenticated requests unless explicitly authorized by a 'public' or 'private' cache directive, and only if the response is marked as 'public' in the Cache-Control header.", 'rationale': 'Prevents unauthorized access to sensitive content through cache reuse by other users or intermediaries.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a request for a protected resource with authentication;
WHEN the server responds;
THEN the response header shall include 'Cache-Control: private' or 'Cache-Control: no-cache' if not publicly accessible."}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "The server shall implement validation mechanisms using the 'Age' and 'Expires' headers to detect stale responses and trigger validation requests to the origin server when necessary.", 'rationale': 'Ensures that cached content remains valid and up-to-date, reducing the risk of serving outdated or compromised data.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a cached response that has exceeded its freshness lifetime;
WHEN a client requests the same resource;
THEN the server shall send a validation request to the origin server using the 'If-None-Match' or 'If-Modified-Since' headers."}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The API shall not allow responses to be cached when the origin differs from the request origin, as defined in Section 4.4 of the HTTP specification.', 'rationale': 'Prevents cross-origin data exposure and ensures origin-based integrity of responses.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a request with a different origin than the response origin;
WHEN the API processes the response;
THEN the response shall not be cached or made available to clients via intermediary caches.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-001', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'The API shall use the Cache-Control header field to specify cache directives, including max-age, must-revalidate, and no-cache, to ensure proper freshness and invalidation of responses.', 'rationale': 'Standard HTTP cache directives ensure interoperability and allow clients and intermediaries to manage response freshness appropriately.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a response with a max-age directive of 300 seconds;
WHEN a client requests the same resource;
THEN the client shall use the response if it is within the freshness lifetime and not expired.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-002', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': "The API shall not generate quoted forms of cache directives (e.g., 'max-age') in response headers unless required by an unknown extension, and shall ensure that consumers can parse token and quoted-string forms for known extensions.", 'rationale': 'Avoids interoperability issues caused by quoted forms of directives as specified in Section 5.2.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a response with a cache directive;
WHEN the directive is in token form;
THEN the response shall not include a quoted form, and clients shall be able to parse both token and quoted-string forms for unknown extensions.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "The API shall not allow responses to be marked as 'public' or 'private' in a way that enables reuse under any condition, as defined in Section 5.2.2.", 'rationale': 'Prevents unintended reuse of sensitive or private data in cache systems.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a response with a private cache directive;
WHEN the response is cached;
THEN the cached response shall not be made available to any client, including those with different origins or permissions.'}]

Actors/Assets: Client devices, web servers (HTTP/2 and HTTP/3), caching intermediaries (e.g., CDNs, proxies), and network transport layers (e.g., QUIC).

Key Functional Concerns: Implementation of priority header fields (e.g., 'Priority') to specify urgency and incremental processing capability for HTTP responses; transmission of PRIORITY_UPDATE frames in HTTP/2 and HTTP/3;

Non-Functional Concerns: Performance under load, latency for priority-based response delivery, cache hit/miss behavior with priority headers;

Security Concerns: Server buffering of PRIORITY_UPDATE frames may lead to resource starvation; caching intermediaries must not modify or misrepresent priority values; processing of priority parameters must follow structured field value rules (e.g., RFC 8941);

Assumptions: The priority header field is defined as end-to-end, not hop-by-hop; intermediaries can cache the priority value and serve it in cached responses; clients and servers support HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 with priority features;

Missing Information: Specific thresholds for urgency levels or concurrency values; how priority handling interacts with QUIC congestion control; whether priority settings are configurable per client or per response type; validation mechanisms for priority parameter values.

