[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'description': 'The system shall represent incident data using the IODEF Version 2 data model as defined in RFC 7970.', 'rationale': 'IODEF Version 2 provides a standardized, interoperable model for incident reporting, ensuring consistency across security teams.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a security incident report in IODEF Version 2 format;
WHEN parsed by the system;
THEN all required fields (e.g., incident ID, threat actor, detection pattern) are correctly mapped and validated.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-001', 'category': 'Functional', 'description': 'The system shall support the representation of detection patterns, including port lists and time-based detection intervals.', 'rationale': 'Detection patterns are critical for identifying and correlating threats across systems and timeframes.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a detection event with a port list;
WHEN the system processes the event;
THEN it shall correctly parse and store port numbers in the format defined in Section 2.9 of RFC 7970.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-002', 'category': 'Functional', 'description': 'The system shall support multilingual strings for incident descriptions and contact information.', 'rationale': 'Incident reports may involve international teams or actors, requiring language flexibility.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an incident report with a multilingual description;
WHEN the system receives it;
THEN it shall store and display the text in the specified language without loss of content.'}, {'id': 'NON-001', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'description': 'The system shall ensure all data is represented using well-formed XML schema as defined in RFC 7970, with no schema violations.', 'rationale': 'Schema compliance ensures interoperability and prevents parsing errors in downstream systems.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an incident report generated by the system;
WHEN validated against the IODEF Version 2 XML schema;
THEN the report shall pass all schema validation rules without errors.'}]

Relevant Assets: Incident reports, detection patterns, threat actor profiles, contact details, software versions, network endpoints.
Trust Boundaries: Between internal security teams and external partners; between data producers and consumers; between raw incident data and processed intelligence.
Threats and Risks:
- Unauthorized access to incident data (e.g., exposure of sensitive contact details);
- Data tampering or falsification during exchange;
- Incomplete or inaccurate representation of detection patterns;
- Misattribution of threat actors due to poor data structure.
Mitigations:
- Use of IODEF Version 2 schema to ensure data consistency and prevent schema-based injection;
- Implementation of digital signatures for incident reports to ensure integrity;
- Separation of contact information from core incident data to reduce exposure;
- Validation of detection patterns against known threat indicators to prevent false positives.
Proposed Architecture:
- A centralized IODEF-based data exchange layer that receives raw incident reports in XML format;
- A validation module that checks compliance with RFC 7970 schema and data types;
- A transformation layer that maps incident data to standardized fields (e.g., incident ID, threat actor, detection pattern);
- A secure sharing interface that allows authorized teams to view and act on incidents, with access controlled via role-based permissions; all data is stored in encrypted form and accessible only to authorized users with proper trust boundaries.

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'requirement': 'The system shall augment Reverse Path Filtering (RPF) lists with Route Origin Authorization (ROA) and reliable IRR data to improve source address validation accuracy.', 'rationale': 'Augmenting RPF with ROA and IRR data reduces false positives in spoofing detection by validating the origin of prefixes, as specified in RFC 6811 and RFC 6482.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a BGP prefix announcement from an AS within a customer cone;
WHEN ROA and IRR data are validated;
