[{'id': 'SEC-USM-001', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'SNMPv3 messages shall use the User-based Security Model (USM) to authenticate and encrypt data transmissions between network devices and the management server.', 'rationale': 'USM provides standardized authentication and privacy mechanisms for SNMPv3, ensuring message integrity and confidentiality against tampering and eavesdropping.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an SNMPv3 message sent from a network device to a management server;
WHEN the message includes a valid USM authentication and privacy protocol;
THEN the message shall be authenticated and encrypted as per RFC 3414, and the management server shall verify the authenticity and integrity of the message.'}, {'id': 'SEC-USM-002', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'The USM authentication protocol shall use HMAC-MD5-96 or HMAC-SHA-96 to provide message integrity, with HMAC-SHA-96 preferred due to stronger cryptographic strength.', 'rationale': 'HMAC-MD5-96 and HMAC-SHA-96 are defined in RFC 3414 as digest authentication mechanisms; HMAC-SHA-96 offers better resistance to cryptographic attacks.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an SNMPv3 message with an authentication protocol;
WHEN the message is processed using HMAC-SHA-96;
THEN the message shall be authenticated using a shared secret key and the digest shall match the expected value as defined in RFC 3414, 6.2.4.1.'}, {'id': 'SEC-USM-003', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'The USM privacy protocol shall use a symmetric encryption algorithm to encrypt message payloads, ensuring confidentiality of transmitted data.', 'rationale': 'Privacy protection is required to prevent unauthorized access to sensitive performance metrics during transmission, as defined in RFC 3414, 1.4.3.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an SNMPv3 message with a privacy protocol;
WHEN the message is encrypted using a symmetric key;
THEN the decrypted message at the management server shall match the original payload and no data shall be exposed in plaintext.'}, {'id': 'NON-USM-001', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'requirement': 'The system shall enforce a time window of 30 seconds to protect against message replay attacks.', 'rationale': 'RFC 3414 specifies replay protection via time synchronization and a time window; a 30-second window is a reasonable default to prevent replay attacks without excessive overhead.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an incoming SNMP message with a timestamp;
WHEN the message is received within a 30-second window of the last authoritative engine time;
