[{'id': 'SRS-CONF-001', 'requirement': 'All configuration changes must be applied uniformly across all target network devices or fully reversed, ensuring no device is left in a partially updated state.', 'rationale': 'Partial updates can lead to inconsistent network behavior and future failures when policies are referenced, as demonstrated in the RFC 6241 example of class-of-service definitions.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a configuration change to a class-of-service policy;
WHEN the change is applied to a subset of devices;
THEN the system shall detect the partial update and initiate a full rollback of all changes on all devices.'}, {'id': 'SRS-CONF-002', 'requirement': 'Configuration operations shall be performed with transactional semantics, using parallel execution across all devices to ensure consistency.', 'rationale': 'The NETCONF protocol specifies that operations must be atomic and consistent across all devices, using locks and checkpoints to maintain integrity.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a configuration update operation;
WHEN the operation is initiated;
THEN configuration locks shall be acquired on all target devices and maintained until all devices are updated and changes are made permanent.'}, {'id': 'SRS-CONF-003', 'requirement': 'A checkpoint shall be maintained on each device before and after configuration changes to enable rollback in case of failure.', 'rationale': 'Checkpoints allow the system to restore previous configurations if any step in the update process fails, as defined in RFC 6241.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a configuration change;
WHEN a step in the update process fails;
