[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'The AI agent shall not be permitted to execute shell commands or modify files outside of a sandboxed environment unless explicitly authorized by the developer through a manual approval workflow.', 'rationale': 'Agents with auto-accept enabled can install packages, write to any file, and execute commands with full developer permissions. A sandboxed environment limits the blast radius of any compromised agent behavior.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an AI agent attempts to execute a shell command on a file outside the project root;
WHEN the system detects the command;
THEN it shall block execution and prompt the developer for manual approval before proceeding.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'Every AI-suggested package name shall be verified against public registries (e.g., npm, PyPI) before installation to ensure it exists and has a minimum age of 30 days.', 'rationale': 'AI assistants frequently suggest hallucinated package names that can be exploited via typosquatting. Verification against public registries and age thresholds reduces this risk.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an AI agent suggests a package name;
WHEN the package does not exist in a public registry or was created within the last 30 days;
THEN the system shall block installation and notify the developer.'}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'All AI-generated dependency lists shall be audited using dependency vulnerability tools (e.g., npm audit, pip audit) before merging into the codebase or triggering a CI/CD pipeline.', 'rationale': 'AI models are trained on historical code and may suggest outdated dependency versions with known CVEs. Automated auditing ensures vulnerabilities are detected and prevented from being introduced.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an AI-generated dependency list;
