[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'Access to enterprise resources shall be granted based on the identity and attributes of the requesting subject, including role, department, and device type.', 'rationale': "Aligns with NIST SP 800-207's identity-driven zero-trust approach, ensuring access decisions are based on verified identity and contextual attributes.", 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a user request for access to a resource;
WHEN the system evaluates the user's identity and attributes;
THEN access is granted only if the user has the required privileges as defined in the policy engine."}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The policy engine shall evaluate environmental factors such as network location and device status to adjust the final access confidence level.', 'rationale': 'Environmental context enhances decision accuracy, allowing for dynamic access control (e.g., partial access in restricted networks), as described in NIST SP 800-207.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a request from a device in a non-enterprise network;
WHEN the system evaluates device status and network location;
THEN the system shall grant only partial access to sensitive data, or deny access if confidence level falls below a defined threshold.'}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'text': 'All access decisions shall be logged with timestamps, user identity, resource, and evaluation context (e.g., device status, network location).', 'rationale': 'Provides auditability and supports compliance and incident response, as required by zero-trust architectures with monitoring capabilities.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a successful or denied access event;
WHEN the system processes the event;
THEN a log entry shall be generated and stored for at least 180 days, containing all relevant metadata.'}, {'id': 'SEC-004', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'The policy engine shall be able to dynamically reconfigure access policies in response to detected threats or workflow changes.', 'rationale': 'Ensures the system can adapt to evolving threats, as required by the zero-trust principle of continuous verification and dynamic policy enforcement.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a threat detection event (e.g., anomalous access pattern);
WHEN the system detects a potential threat;
THEN the policy engine shall automatically adjust access controls for affected resources within 30 seconds.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The enterprise shall collect and store metadata (source and destination IP addresses, ports, protocol, timestamps) for all network traffic, including encrypted traffic, to enable detection of anomalous behavior.', 'rationale': 'Metadata provides actionable insights into traffic patterns without requiring decryption, supporting detection of active attackers or malware communication as per NIST SP 800-207.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN encrypted traffic is observed on the network;
WHEN metadata is collected and stored;
THEN the enterprise shall be able to detect anomalous traffic patterns indicative of an active attacker or malware using machine learning models.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'All stored monitoring data and access policies shall be protected with the most restrictive access controls, accessible only by designated administrator accounts with multi-factor authentication (MFA).', 'rationale': 'Storing sensitive information such as access policies and traffic data creates a target for attackers; restricting access mitigates reconnaissance and attack risks as outlined in NIST SP 800-207.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an attacker gains access to stored monitoring data or access policies;
WHEN they attempt to extract or analyze the data;
THEN access shall be denied, and an alert shall be generated to the security operations center.'}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'Any automated agent (e.g., AI-based NPE) used to manage ZTA components shall authenticate using multi-factor authentication (MFA) and shall not be granted administrative privileges beyond those required for its specific function.', 'rationale': 'Automated agents may have lower authentication thresholds than human users, increasing the risk of unauthorized access or impersonation; MFA and privilege minimization reduce this risk as per NIST SP 800-207.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an attacker attempts to interact with an automated agent;
WHEN the agent is authenticated with MFA and operates under limited privileges;
THEN the agent shall not perform privileged actions, and any unauthorized action shall trigger an alert.'}, {'id': 'NON-001', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'text': 'The machine learning model used to analyze encrypted traffic shall be retrained and validated at least quarterly to reduce false positives and false negatives in access decisions.', 'rationale': 'Dynamic threat detection models require continuous tuning to maintain accuracy and avoid erroneous decisions that could compromise security posture, as noted in NIST SP 800-207.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a model has been deployed to classify traffic;
WHEN new data is collected over a 90-day period;
THEN the model shall be retrained and validated, and performance metrics shall show a reduction in false positives and false negatives by at least 10% compared to the previous cycle.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The system shall not store or transmit user passwords or password combinations to any third-party application during authentication or authorization processes.', 'rationale': 'Storing or transmitting user passwords extends the attack surface to third parties, which are outside the control of the system. This violates the principle of least privilege and increases the risk of credential exposure.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a third-party application attempts to access user credentials via a login form;
WHEN the system receives the request;
THEN the system shall reject the request and redirect the user to an OAuth-based authentication flow without exposing any password or credential data.'}, {'id': 'AUTH-001', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'The system shall support OpenID Connect 1.0 (OIDC) for user identity verification and authentication via an ID Token (a signed JWT).', 'rationale': 'OIDC provides a standardized, interoperable identity layer on top of OAuth that enables secure user identity verification using a signed token, reducing the risk of identity spoofing.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a user attempts to log in to the system;
WHEN the user is redirected to an identity provider;
THEN the system shall receive and validate an ID Token containing issuer (`iss`), audience (`aud`), expiration (`exp`), and a valid signature using the provider's JWKs;
AND the user shall be authenticated and redirected to the application with a valid session."}, {'id': 'AUTH-002', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'The system shall use OAuth 2.0 or 2.1 for delegated access to APIs, allowing third-party applications to request specific scopes of data access without storing user credentials.', 'rationale': 'OAuth provides a secure authorization framework that enables third-party applications to access data on behalf of users without requiring the system to store or manage user passwords.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a third-party application requests access to user data;
WHEN the user grants consent for specific scopes;
THEN the system shall issue an authorization code or access token to the third-party application;
AND the system shall not store or transmit the user's password or credentials in any form."}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "The system shall validate the ID Token and authorization response using well-maintained libraries or SDKs and verify the provider's JWKs via a JWKS endpoint.", 'rationale': 'Using trusted libraries and validating JWKs ensures that the ID Token is signed by the correct issuer and has not been tampered with, reducing the risk of forged or malicious tokens.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a user logs in via an identity provider;
WHEN the ID Token is received;
THEN the system shall validate the signature using the provider's public key from the JWKS endpoint;
AND the system shall reject any token that fails validation or lacks required claims (e.g., `iss`, `aud`, `exp`)."}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The system shall not assume that private keys used in FIDO2 or passkey authentication are hardware-backed or non-exportable unless explicitly verified via authenticator properties or attestation.', 'rationale': 'Some authenticators may allow key export or server-side storage, which could introduce security risks. The system must not rely on assumptions about key storage mechanisms.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user authenticates via a passkey;
WHEN the system receives a signed response;
THEN the system shall verify whether the authenticator supports hardware-backed key storage via attestation data;
AND if not verified, the system shall not assume the private key is secure or non-exportable.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The system shall use public-key digital signature schemes (e.g., EdDSA, ECDSA, RSASSA-PSS) for token issuance, with EdDSA preferred for its performance and resistance to known attacks.', 'rationale': 'EdDSA is recommended and has limited support in JWT implementations; it is more secure and efficient than traditional schemes like ECDSA on constrained devices.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a JWT token issued with EdDSA algorithm and valid claims;
WHEN the token is validated using a public key;
THEN the validation shall succeed and no forgery shall be possible.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "The system shall not allow unsecured JWTs with 'alg': 'none' in any configuration or default setting.", 'rationale': 'Unsecured JWTs allow attackers to forge tokens and impersonate users, which is a critical vulnerability.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a JWT with 'alg': 'none';
WHEN the token is attempted to be validated;
THEN the parser shall reject it and return an error."}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The system shall prevent key type confusion by ensuring that public keys intended for digital signatures are not used as MAC secrets during token validation.', 'rationale': 'Using a public key as a MAC secret allows attackers to forge tokens, leading to unauthorized access.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a token with 'alg': 'HS256' and a public key provided;
WHEN the token is validated;
THEN the validator shall detect that the key is not a valid MAC secret and reject the token."}, {'id': 'SEC-004', 'category': 'Key Management', 'text': 'Each (issuer, audience) pair shall use a unique secret for MAC-based tokens; no secret shall be reused across different audiences or issuers.', 'rationale': 'Reusing secrets allows one audience to forge tokens impersonating the issuer, violating authenticity.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN two different audiences with the same issuer;
WHEN a token is issued with a shared secret;
THEN the token shall not be valid for either audience when forged by the other.'}, {'id': 'SEC-005', 'category': 'Secret Generation', 'text': 'All MAC secrets shall be generated using a cryptographically secure random number generator and must have at least 256 bits of entropy for HS256 and 512 bits for HS512.', 'rationale': 'Insufficient entropy or weak randomness sources compromise the security of MACs; secrets must be large and generated securely.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a secret generated using secrets.token_bytes(256//8) for HS256;
WHEN used in a JWT with HS256 algorithm;
THEN the token shall be valid and no forgery shall be possible via brute-force or prediction.'}, {'id': 'SEC-006', 'category': 'Non-functional', 'text': 'The system shall not publish private keys or MAC secrets at any public HTTPS URI or in any configuration file.', 'rationale': 'Publishing secrets leads to immediate compromise and secret leakage.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a public HTTPS endpoint;
WHEN a private key or MAC secret is accessed;
THEN the access shall be denied and an error shall be returned.'}]

[{'id': 'MFA-01', 'requirement': 'Authentication shall require at least two factors: something the user knows (e.g., password) and something the user has (e.g., TOTP or U2F token).', 'rationale': 'Per NIST SP 800-63, multifactor authentication is required for high-assurance systems. Using two factors significantly reduces the risk of account compromise compared to passwords alone.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user attempting to log in to an internal web application;
WHEN they enter a valid password and a valid one-time code from a TOTP or U2F token;
THEN authentication shall succeed and no further factors shall be required.'}, {'id': 'MFA-02', 'requirement': 'The system shall not use security questions as an authentication factor.', 'rationale': 'NIST SP 800-63 explicitly disallows security questions as an acceptable authentication factor due to their susceptibility to guessing and social media exposure.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user attempting to authenticate via security questions;
WHEN the system prompts for a security question;
THEN the system shall reject the authentication attempt and provide an error message indicating that security questions are not supported.'}, {'id': 'MFA-03', 'requirement': 'The authentication system shall be resistant to phishing attacks, particularly for TOTP and U2F tokens.', 'rationale': 'Phishing remains a major threat; U2F tokens are resistant to phishing because private keys remain on the device, while TOTP codes are short-lived and can be invalidated if the device is compromised.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an attacker attempts to trick a user into entering a TOTP code from a fake application;
WHEN the user enters the code;
THEN the system shall reject the authentication attempt and log the event as a potential phishing attempt.'}, {'id': 'MFA-04', 'requirement': 'If a user loses their TOTP or U2F token, a new token must be issued or a new authenticator app must be configured without requiring physical delivery of a new token.', 'rationale': 'Software-based TOTP allows for easier recovery and configuration, reducing administrative overhead and user disruption compared to hardware tokens.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user reports losing their TOTP app or hardware token;
WHEN the user requests a new authenticator;
THEN the system shall allow the user to reconfigure a new TOTP app on their mobile device or issue a new U2F token via secure administrative channel, without requiring physical delivery.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The SAML signing certificate must be self-signed and issued using a private key stored in a Hardware Security Module (HSM) or equivalent secure key storage.', 'rationale': 'Self-signed certificates provide full control over certificate lifecycle and avoid reliance on public or third-party CAs, which may have restrictive policies or limited support for SAML use cases.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a self-signed SAML signing certificate is generated using a private key stored in an HSM;
WHEN the certificate is used to sign SAML assertions;
THEN the certificate must be valid and not expired, and the private key must remain inaccessible to unauthorized processes.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The SAML signing certificate must have a lifetime of at least 398 days, and no longer than two years, to align with NIST and NSA guidance on key lifetime and certificate validity.', 'rationale': 'Longer certificate lifetimes reduce the frequency of rotation, which minimizes operational overhead and reduces the risk of human error during certificate updates.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a certificate is issued with a validity period;
WHEN the certificate is used in a production environment;
THEN the validity period must be between 398 days and 2 years, and the certificate must be rotated only when a private key compromise is detected or when a security incident occurs.'}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'All SAML certificates must include a valid OCSP URL, and the SP must validate the certificate revocation status using the OCSP endpoint if one is present.', 'rationale': 'OCSP provides real-time revocation checking, which enhances security by ensuring that compromised or revoked certificates are not used in authentication flows.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a SAML certificate includes an OCSP URL;
WHEN the SP attempts to validate the certificate;
THEN the SP must successfully retrieve and validate the OCSP response to confirm the certificate is not revoked.'}, {'id': 'NON-001', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'text': 'The certificate rotation process must be automated and triggered only upon detection of a private key compromise or certificate expiration.', 'rationale': 'Manual rotation is error-prone and can lead to outages; automation ensures consistency and reduces operational burden.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a private key is compromised or a certificate is approaching expiration;
WHEN the system detects the event;
THEN an automated workflow must initiate certificate generation, distribution, and SP notification within 24 hours.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-COOKIE-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'All session IDs must be stored in cookies with the `__Host-` prefix, set with the `Secure` attribute, and must not have a `Domain` attribute. The `Path` attribute must be set to `/` to restrict scope to the root path.', 'rationale': 'The `__Host-` prefix prevents subdomain forgery and HTTPS downgrade attacks. Restricting the `Domain` and `Path` attributes minimizes exposure to cross-subdomain and cross-path attacks.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user logs in to the application via HTTPS;
WHEN the browser sends a request to any endpoint;
THEN the session ID cookie must be sent only to the origin server and the root path, and must be transmitted only over HTTPS.'}, {'id': 'SEC-COOKIE-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'No session tokens, authentication tokens, or JWTs shall be stored in `localStorage` or `sessionStorage`.', 'rationale': 'These APIs are accessible to any JavaScript in the origin, making them vulnerable to XSS attacks that can expose credentials to attackers.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a user performs an action that would trigger JavaScript execution;
WHEN an XSS vulnerability exists in the application;
THEN no session token or credential is accessible via the browser's DOM or localStorage API."}, {'id': 'SEC-COOKIE-003', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'All cookies used by the application must be explicitly defined in the SRS, including their name, purpose, and the security attributes applied (e.g., `HttpOnly`, `Secure`, `SameSite=Strict`).', 'rationale': 'Transparency in cookie usage enables audits and ensures consistent security controls are applied across all session-related interactions.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a security audit is performed;
WHEN the audit reviews all cookies used in the application;
THEN every cookie must be listed with its name, purpose, and security attributes, and no cookie is used without a defined rationale.'}, {'id': 'SEC-COOKIE-004', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'Session management must use non-persistent (session) cookies with no `Max-Age` or `Expires` attributes to ensure session data is lost upon browser closure.', 'rationale': 'Persistent cookies increase the risk of session hijacking if the browser cache is compromised or accessed by an attacker.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a user closes their browser;
WHEN the user restarts the browser;
THEN the session ID must not be present in the browser's memory or cache, and no session data must be accessible via stored cookies."}]

[{'id': 'SRS-AC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'All users, including contractors and partners, shall use multi-factor authentication (MFA) with phishing-resistant methods such as FIDO2 hardware security keys or WebAuthn-based passkeys.', 'rationale': 'Phishing-resistant MFA reduces risk of credential theft and is mandated by OMB M-22-09 for federal agencies and aligns with best practices for identity protection.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user attempting access from a new device or outside normal hours;
WHEN using a phishing-resistant MFA method;
THEN access is granted with a validated identity token and the system logs the authentication event.'}, {'id': 'SRS-AC-002', 'category': 'Access Control', 'text': "Access to sensitive data shall follow the principle of least privilege, providing only the minimum permissions required for a user's role and task.", 'rationale': 'Minimizing access scope reduces the attack surface and limits lateral movement in case of compromise.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a finance manager accessing payroll data;
WHEN the request is evaluated against role-based permissions;
THEN only the necessary data fields are accessible, and no broader access is granted.'}, {'id': 'SRS-AC-003', 'category': 'Device Security', 'text': 'All devices must be registered with a unique certificate and undergo continuous health assessment for antivirus status, OS patch levels, and security configurations.', 'rationale': 'Device-based identity and real-time posture monitoring are foundational to Zero Trust, enabling trust decisions based on device integrity.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a new laptop attempting to connect;
WHEN it fails to pass a health check or lacks a valid certificate;
THEN access is blocked and the user is redirected to a secure browser platform.'}, {'id': 'SRS-AC-004', 'category': 'Session Management', 'text': 'All access sessions shall be time-limited, with a default maximum duration of 90 minutes, and require re-verification for high-risk actions such as downloading large data sets.', 'rationale': 'Time-bound sessions reduce exposure and allow for rapid response if a session is compromised.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user accessing sensitive data;
WHEN attempting to download a large file;
THEN a secondary verification step is triggered, and access is granted only after explicit user approval.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The client shall generate a code verifier as a high-entropy cryptographic random string using unreserved characters from RFC 3986, with a minimum length of 43 characters and a maximum length of 128 characters.', 'rationale': 'Ensures sufficient entropy to make brute-force guessing impractical, aligning with PKCE best practices.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client generating a code verifier;
WHEN the verifier is created using unreserved characters and meets length constraints;
THEN the verifier shall have at least 43 characters and be generated using a cryptographically secure random number generator.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "The client shall derive a code challenge from the code verifier using either the 'plain' or 'S256' transformation method, with 'S256' being mandatory to implement (MTI) on the server.", 'rationale': 'S256 provides stronger security against MITM attacks by hashing the verifier before encoding, as required by RFC 7636.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a code verifier;
WHEN the client applies the S256 transformation (SHA-256 hash followed by base64url encoding);
THEN the resulting code challenge shall match the expected format and be stored for verification at the token endpoint.'}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The server shall store the code challenge and code challenge method associated with each issued authorization code in an encrypted form, and shall not include the code challenge in client requests in a form that can be extracted by third parties.', 'rationale': 'Prevents exposure of sensitive cryptographic values and mitigates risks of information leakage.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a server issuing an authorization code;
WHEN the code challenge and method are stored securely;
THEN the server shall not expose the code challenge in any client-facing request or response that could be intercepted.'}, {'id': 'SEC-004', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The token endpoint shall verify the code verifier by computing the code challenge from the received code verifier using the specified method (S256 or plain) and comparing it to the previously stored code challenge.', 'rationale': 'Ensures that only legitimate clients can obtain access tokens, preventing unauthorized access via intercepted authorization codes.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a client sending an authorization code and a code verifier;
WHEN the token endpoint computes the code challenge from the verifier using the specified method;
THEN if the computed challenge matches the stored one, the token endpoint shall issue an access token; otherwise, it shall return an error with 'invalid_grant'."}, {'id': 'NON-001', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'text': 'The system shall support backward compatibility with OAuth 2.0 clients that do not implement PKCE by allowing the server to revert to the standard OAuth 2.0 flow if the code challenge is not provided.', 'rationale': 'Ensures interoperability with legacy clients without requiring them to upgrade.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a client that does not send a code challenge;
WHEN the server receives the authorization request;
THEN the server shall return an 'invalid_request' error with a description indicating that the code challenge is required."}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'type': 'security', 'text': 'The client shall generate a code_verifier with a minimum of 256 bits of entropy using a cryptographically secure random number generator, producing a 32-octet sequence that is base64url-encoded into a 43-octet URL-safe string.', 'rationale': 'A 256-bit entropy ensures the code_verifier is not guessable by an attacker, which is critical for the security model of PKCE.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client application generating a code_verifier;
WHEN the code_verifier is generated with a 32-octet sequence;
THEN the resulting base64url-encoded string shall be at least 43 octets long and contain no padding or non-URL-safe characters.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'type': 'security', 'text': "The client shall use the 'S256' code_challenge method in all authorization requests and shall not downgrade to the 'plain' method under any circumstances.", 'rationale': "The 'S256' method protects against eavesdropping and MITM attacks by ensuring the code_challenge cannot be used without the corresponding code_verifier.", 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a client sending an authorization request;
WHEN the 'S256' method is used for code_challenge;
THEN the server shall accept the request and not respond with an error indicating lack of support for PKCE."}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'type': 'security', 'text': 'If the server does not support PKCE, it shall ignore the code_verifier parameter and return a standard OAuth 2.0 error response, without revealing the presence of a code_verifier or code_challenge.', 'rationale': 'This prevents attackers from inferring the existence of a PKCE flow or attempting downgrade attacks.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a server that does not support PKCE;
WHEN a client sends a request with a code_verifier;
THEN the server shall ignore the parameter and return a standard OAuth 2.0 error response, without disclosing any information about the code_verifier or code_challenge.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The authorization server shall configure its TLS stack to support mutual TLS client authentication and self-signed certificate authentication methods in parallel.', 'rationale': 'To enable flexible client authentication while maintaining compatibility with diverse client environments.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a client attempting to authenticate using mutual TLS or a self-signed certificate;
WHEN the server receives a client certificate;
THEN the server shall accept the certificate and proceed with token issuance, without requiring a trusted CA for validation of the certificate's signature."}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'Access tokens shall be bound to the specific client certificate presented during the TLS handshake, and the token shall be invalidated upon any certificate update by the client.', 'rationale': 'To ensure that access tokens are tied to a specific client and that access is revoked when the client changes its certificate.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client with a valid certificate;
WHEN the client updates its certificate;
THEN the authorization server shall invalidate all existing access tokens bound to the previous certificate;
AND the client shall request a new access token using a refresh token.'}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "The authorization server shall not verify the certificate's trust chain (e.g., CA signature) when validating client certificates for mutual TLS or self-signed certificate authentication.", 'rationale': 'To ensure that client certificate possession is validated via cryptographic proof-of-possession, not trust chain validation, aligning with the principle of certificate-bound tokens.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a client attempting to authenticate with a self-signed or mutual TLS certificate;
WHEN the server receives the certificate;
THEN the server shall not validate the certificate's signature against a trusted CA;
AND shall instead verify that the client can prove possession of the private key via cryptographic proof-of-possession."}, {'id': 'SEC-004', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'text': 'The authorization server shall support TLS 1.2 and TLS 1.3 for client authentication, with a preference for TLS 1.3 where available and supported.', 'rationale': 'To ensure compatibility with current and future secure TLS implementations, as both versions are cited in the source.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client attempting to establish a TLS connection;
WHEN the client uses TLS 1.2 or 1.3;
THEN the server shall accept the connection and proceed with authentication.'}]

[{'id': 'SRS-SEC-001', 'requirement': "The authorization server shall issue a JWT access token with a 'groups' claim containing a list of user group identifiers that are relevant to the resource being accessed.", 'rationale': 'This enables resource servers to validate user membership in specific groups for access control, as defined in RFC7643.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a user accessing a resource with a specific group context;
WHEN the authorization server issues a JWT access token;
THEN the token must contain a 'groups' claim with at least one group identifier that matches the resource's group requirements."}, {'id': 'SRS-SEC-002', 'requirement': "The authorization server shall issue a JWT access token with a 'scope' claim that includes the requested permissions (e.g., openid, profile, reademail), and use the 'resource' parameter to set the 'aud' claim value in the token.", 'rationale': "The 'aud' claim must match the resource identifier to ensure proper audience validation, as specified in RFC8707 and the example request in the source.", 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a request with a 'resource' parameter;
WHEN the authorization server issues a JWT access token;
THEN the 'aud' claim in the token must have the same value as the 'resource' parameter in the request."}, {'id': 'SRS-SEC-003', 'requirement': "The resource server shall validate the JWT access token by verifying the 'iss' claim matches the expected issuer, the 'aud' claim includes the current resource server's identifier, and the token signature is valid using the algorithm specified in the 'alg' header.", 'rationale': 'As per RFC7515 and the validation steps in the source, these checks are mandatory to prevent unauthorized access and ensure token authenticity.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a JWT access token received by a resource server;
WHEN the server validates the token;
THEN it must reject the token if the 'iss' claim does not match the expected issuer or if the 'aud' claim does not include the current resource server's identifier."}, {'id': 'SRS-SEC-004', 'requirement': "If the request does not include a 'resource' parameter, the authorization server shall use a default resource indicator derived from the 'scope' parameter, and if multiple scopes are present, it shall reject the request with an 'invalid_scope' error.", 'rationale': 'The source specifies that the authorization server must use the scope to infer a default resource indicator when no resource is specified, and must reject requests with conflicting scope values.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a request without a 'resource' parameter and with multiple scope values;
WHEN the authorization server attempts to derive a default resource indicator;
THEN it must reject the request with an error code of 'invalid_scope'."}]

[{'id': 'SEC-ERR-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "The system shall reject a token request that includes an asymmetric key the Resource Server cannot process, using a response code equivalent to CoAP 4.00 (Bad Request) and including the error code 'unsupported_pop_key' as defined in Table 3.", 'rationale': 'Ensures that clients using unsupported key formats are rejected early, preventing potential cryptographic failures or security vulnerabilities.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a client submits an asymmetric key not supported by the Resource Server;
WHEN the Authorization Server processes the request;
THEN it shall respond with a CoAP 4.00 (Bad Request) and include the error code 'unsupported_pop_key' in the CBOR payload."}, {'id': 'SEC-ERR-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "The system shall reject a token request when the client and Resource Server do not share a common profile, using a response code equivalent to CoAP 4.00 (Bad Request) and including the error code 'incompatible_ace_profiles' as defined in Table 3.", 'rationale': 'Prevents unauthorized or misconfigured token exchanges by enforcing profile compatibility between client and server.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a client and Resource Server have no shared profile;
WHEN the Authorization Server receives a token request;
THEN it shall respond with a CoAP 4.00 (Bad Request) and include the error code 'incompatible_ace_profiles' in the CBOR payload."}, {'id': 'FUNC-GRANT-001', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'The system shall map grant types to CBOR abbreviations as defined in Table 4 when using CBOR encoding, using the values: password=0, authorization_code=1, client_credentials=2, refresh_token=3.', 'rationale': 'Enables compact and efficient encoding of grant types in constrained environments, aligning with standard practices in OAuth 2.0.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a token request with a grant type parameter;
WHEN the request is encoded in CBOR;
THEN the grant type shall be represented using the corresponding integer value from Table 4.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-TOKEN-001', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': "The system shall use the 'pop' value as the default for the token_type parameter in CBOR-encoded responses, and shall only provide other token types if registered in the IANA OAuth Access Token Types registry.", 'rationale': 'Ensures consistency and interoperability with the PoP token model defined in this framework.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a token request with no specified token_type;
WHEN the system generates a response;
THEN the token_type parameter shall default to 'pop' in the CBOR-encoded response."}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "The system shall register the 'ace_profile' parameter in the IANA OAuth Parameters registry with the parameter name and usage location defined in Section 8.9 of RFC 9200.", 'rationale': 'Ensures interoperability with other OAuth 2.0 implementations that support ACE profiles as defined in RFC 9200.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN an OAuth token response with the 'ace_profile' parameter;
WHEN the parameter is present and valid;
THEN the value must be a valid ACE profile name as registered in the IANA ACE Profile registry and must conform to the CBOR mapping in Section 8.8."}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "The system shall register the error code 'unsupported_pop_key' in the IANA OAuth Extensions Error Registry with the usage location and protocol extension defined in Section 5.8.3 of RFC 9200.", 'rationale': 'Provides a standardized error response for cases where the client uses an unsupported POP key, enabling consistent error handling across systems.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a client request with an unsupported POP key;
WHEN the server processes the request;
THEN it shall return a token error response with the error code 'unsupported_pop_key'."}, {'id': 'FUNC-001', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'The system shall support the PoP token type in the OAuth Access Token Types registry with the response parameters cnf and rs_cnf as defined in RFC 9200 and RFC 9201.', 'rationale': 'Enables secure token exchange using PoP (Proof of Possession) as defined in the ACE-OAuth profile.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client requesting a PoP token;
WHEN the server issues a PoP token;
THEN the token must include the cnf and rs_cnf parameters as specified in RFC 9201 and RFC 9200.'}, {'id': 'NON-001', 'category': 'Non-functional', 'text': 'The system shall assign CBOR values for all registered parameters and error codes according to the IANA registration policies defined in RFC 8126, with values in the range -256 to 255 designated as Standards Action.', 'rationale': 'Ensures alignment with IANA standards and promotes interoperability with existing systems.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a parameter or error code registration;
WHEN the value is assigned;
THEN the value must fall within the appropriate range as defined in RFC 8126 (e.g., -256 to 255 for Standards Action).'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The authorization server shall issue refresh tokens to public clients only when the client presents a valid DPoP proof using the same public key that was used to obtain the initial access token.', 'rationale': 'Ensures that refresh tokens are bound to a specific public key, preventing unauthorized use of tokens by third parties.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a public client requests a refresh token with a valid DPoP proof using the same public key as the initial access token;
WHEN the authorization server processes the request;
THEN the refresh token shall be issued and bound to that public key, and the DPoP proof shall be validated against the public key in the token response.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "The authorization server shall include a 'dpop_bound_access_tokens' metadata parameter set to 'true' in client registration to indicate that all token requests from the client must include a DPoP proof.", 'rationale': 'Enforces DPoP usage across all token requests, enhancing security posture for public clients.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client is registered with the authorization server;
WHEN the client attempts to request an access token without a DPoP proof;
THEN the authorization server shall reject the request and return a 400 Bad Request error with a reason indicating DPoP is required.'}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'Resource servers shall verify that the public key in the DPoP proof matches the public key bound to the access token using the JWK SHA-256 thumbprint method as defined in Section 6.1.', 'rationale': 'Prevents impersonation and ensures that tokens are issued and validated against the correct public key, reducing the risk of token forgery.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN an access token is issued with a DPoP proof;
WHEN the resource server receives the token;
THEN it shall extract the JWK thumbprint from the token's 'jkt' claim and validate that it matches the public key used in the DPoP proof."}, {'id': 'NON-001', 'category': 'Non-functional', 'text': "The authorization server shall support at least one JWS algorithm (e.g., ES256) in its 'dpop_signing_alg_values_supported' metadata for DPoP proofs.", 'rationale': 'Ensures compatibility with a range of client implementations and cryptographic standards.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client requests a token using a DPoP proof;
WHEN the request uses a supported JWS algorithm;
THEN the authorization server shall accept the request and issue a valid token.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-DPOP-001', 'type': 'security', 'text': 'The authorization server shall validate DPoP proof JWTs in both the request body and the DPoP header, and shall reject any request that fails to provide a valid DPoP proof.', 'rationale': 'Ensures compatibility with both client implementations and supports a single code path for all requests to the authorization server.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a PAR request with a valid DPoP proof in the request body OR in the header;
WHEN the authorization server receives the request;
THEN it shall validate the DPoP proof and accept the request if the proof is valid and the public key thumbprint matches the dpop_jkt value.'}, {'id': 'SEC-DPOP-002', 'type': 'security', 'text': 'If both DPoP in the request body and the DPoP header are provided, the authorization server shall reject the request if the JWK thumbprint in dpop_jkt does not match the public key in the DPoP header.', 'rationale': 'Prevents inconsistency in key binding and ensures that the same public key is used across both mechanisms.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a request with both dpop_jkt and a DPoP header;
WHEN the JWK thumbprint in dpop_jkt does not match the public key in the DPoP header;
THEN the authorization server shall reject the request with a 400 error.'}, {'id': 'SEC-DPOP-003', 'type': 'security', 'text': 'The authorization server shall enforce a time-bound validity for DPoP proofs, with a maximum lifetime of 5 minutes, and shall accept proofs only if the iat claim is within a reasonable time window (e.g., ±300 seconds) of the current server time.', 'rationale': 'Mitigates the risk of replay attacks by limiting the window during which a DPoP proof is valid.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a DPoP proof with an iat claim;
WHEN the current server time is within 300 seconds of the iat value;
THEN the proof shall be accepted; otherwise, it shall be rejected.'}, {'id': 'SEC-DPOP-004', 'type': 'security', 'text': 'The authorization server shall store the jti value of each accepted DPoP proof for the duration of its validity window to prevent replay attacks, and shall reject any subsequent request with the same jti value for the same URI and method.', 'rationale': 'Provides strong protection against DPoP proof replay by ensuring single-use of each proof.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a request to the same URI and method with a DPoP proof;
WHEN the jti value has already been seen in a previous request;
THEN the server shall reject the request with a 400 error.'}, {'id': 'SEC-DPOP-005', 'type': 'security', 'text': 'The authorization server shall use server-provided nonces in DPoP proofs to prevent attackers from pre-generating and exfiltrating DPoP proofs for future use.', 'rationale': 'Prevents pre-generation of DPoP proofs and ensures that possession of the proof-of-possession key is demonstrated, not just possession of a proof.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client attempting to use a pre-generated DPoP proof;
WHEN the nonce in the proof is server-provided and not predictable;
THEN the server shall reject the proof if it has been used before or if the nonce is not valid.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-ESP-PAD-BLOCK', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "The payload data, excluding the Initialization Vector (IV) but including ESP trailer fields (Pad Length, Next Header), shall be padded to a multiple of the encryption algorithm's block size. The padding computation shall include any replicated SPI and Sequence Number fields when used in a combined mode algorithm for integrity.", 'rationale': "Ensures that the plaintext is aligned to the block cipher's block size, which is a requirement for correct encryption operation in block cipher modes.", 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an ESP packet with a block cipher algorithm and a combined mode using replicated SPI and Sequence Number;
WHEN the padding is computed;
THEN the resulting payload size shall be a multiple of the block size, and the Pad Length field shall correctly indicate the number of padding bytes.'}, {'id': 'SEC-ESP-PAD-ALIGN-4BYTE', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The padding computation shall ensure that the Pad Length and Next Header fields are right-aligned within a 4-byte word, such that the Integrity Check Value (ICV) field (if present) is aligned on a 4-byte boundary.', 'rationale': 'Ensures proper alignment of the ICV field, which is critical for integrity verification and avoids misalignment errors in decryption.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an ESP packet with an ICV field;
WHEN the packet is processed;
THEN the Pad Length and Next Header fields shall be aligned to a 4-byte boundary, and the ICV field shall be positioned at a 4-byte offset from the start of the payload data.'}, {'id': 'SEC-ESP-PAD-DEFAULT-SCHEME', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'If the encryption algorithm does not specify padding contents, the padding bytes shall be initialized with a monotonically increasing sequence of 1-byte values (1, 2, 3, ...). The first padding byte shall be numbered 1, and subsequent bytes shall increment by 1.', 'rationale': 'Provides a simple, hardware-friendly default scheme that limits exposure to cut-and-paste attacks when padding is validated at the receiver.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an ESP packet with no algorithm-defined padding;
WHEN the receiver decrypts the packet;
THEN it shall inspect the padding field and verify that the sequence values are 1, 2, 3, ... in order, and shall reject any packet with invalid or out-of-sequence padding values.'}, {'id': 'SEC-ESP-DUMMY-PACKET', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The protocol value 59 (no next header) shall be used to designate dummy packets. A transmitter shall be capable of generating dummy packets with this value, and a receiver shall discard such packets without error indication.', 'rationale': 'Dummy packets allow masking of actual traffic patterns, supporting traffic flow confidentiality (TFC) by introducing random, non-traffic packets into the stream.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a network with active ESP tunnels;
WHEN a dummy packet is generated with Next Header value 59;
THEN the receiver shall discard the packet without signaling an error, and the packet shall not be processed as valid traffic.'}, {'id': 'SEC-ESP-TFC-PADDING', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'When Traffic Flow Confidentiality (TFC) is required, the system shall use the optional TFC padding mechanism defined in Section 2.7 of RFC 4303, rather than relying on the standard Padding field (which is limited to 255 bytes).', 'rationale': 'The standard Padding field is insufficient to hide traffic characteristics; the dedicated TFC padding mechanism is designed specifically for TFC and provides effective concealment of traffic patterns.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a scenario where traffic flow patterns must be concealed;
WHEN TFC is enabled;
THEN the system shall use the TFC padding mechanism to add padding beyond 255 bytes, and the actual payload length shall not be inferable from packet size or timing.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The IKEv2 authentication mechanism shall use RSASSA-PKCS1-v1_5 or RSASSA-PSS for digital signature operations, with RSASSA-PSS preferred due to its improved resistance to known cryptographic attacks.', 'rationale': "RSASSA-PKCS1-v1_5 has known vulnerabilities (e.g., Bleichenbacher's low-exponent attack), and RSASSA-PSS provides stronger security by using a more robust padding scheme as defined in RFC 7427.", 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user configures an IPsec tunnel with RSA-based authentication;
WHEN the system validates the signature algorithm;
THEN it shall reject any configuration using RSASSA-PKCS1-v1_5 and shall enforce RSASSA-PSS or a supported alternative.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The system shall not allow the use of MD5 as a hash algorithm in IKEv2 authentication payloads.', 'rationale': 'MD5 is considered unsafe for cryptographic signature use and has been proven vulnerable to collision attacks (as cited in [WY05]).', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user attempts to configure a tunnel using MD5 as a hash algorithm;
WHEN the system evaluates the hash algorithm;
THEN it shall reject the configuration and provide a user-friendly error message indicating MD5 is not supported.'}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The system shall enforce the use of SHA-256 or higher hash algorithms (e.g., SHA-256, SHA-256, SHA-384, SHA-512) in IKEv2 authentication, and shall not allow SHA-1.', 'rationale': 'SHA-1 is deprecated due to known collision vulnerabilities; SHA-256 and higher provide stronger cryptographic security as defined in RFC 7427 and NIST 800-57.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user attempts to configure a tunnel with SHA-1;
WHEN the system validates the hash algorithm;
THEN it shall reject the configuration and require the use of SHA-256 or higher.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-001', 'category': 'Functionality', 'text': 'The system shall provide a user interface that allows end users to select a security level (e.g., low, medium, high) corresponding to a set of cryptographic parameters.', 'rationale': 'End users need a clear way to select security levels, which must be tied to actual cryptographic configurations.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a user selects 'high' security level;
WHEN the system applies the configuration;
THEN it shall enforce the use of SHA-256 or higher and RSASSA-PSS as the signature algorithm."}]

[{'id': 'SEC-KEY-VAL-01', 'requirement': 'Before using any public key in digital signature or key establishment operations, the system shall verify that the public key is arithmetically correct and satisfies the mathematical properties required for the specific algorithm (e.g., DSA, ECDSA, RSA).', 'rationale': 'Invalid public keys can lead to security failures, including information leakage from private keys or incorrect cryptographic operations. Validation ensures that keys are mathematically sound and reduces the risk of cryptographic attacks.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a public key received from a key-pair owner or a CA;
WHEN the system performs validation against algorithm-specific mathematical properties;
THEN the system shall reject any public key that fails validation and log the failure for audit purposes.'}, {'id': 'SEC-DOMAIN-VAL-01', 'requirement': 'Domain parameters used in discrete log public-key algorithms (e.g., finite field or elliptic curve) shall be validated for correctness and authenticity prior to use in key generation, signature creation, or key agreement processes.', 'rationale': 'Invalid domain parameters can void the intended security of all cryptographic operations using those parameters, rendering the entire system vulnerable to attacks.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a domain parameter set (e.g., prime modulus, field size);
WHEN the system performs validation against NIST SP 800-56A standards;
THEN the system shall reject any parameter set that fails validation and record the failure in audit logs.'}, {'id': 'SEC-KEY-POS-01', 'requirement': 'Before using a static public key, the system shall obtain assurance that the corresponding private key is securely possessed by the key-pair owner and has not been modified or compromised.', 'rationale': 'Assurance of private-key possession ensures that the private key is available and intact, preventing information leakage and ensuring the integrity of cryptographic operations.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a public key issued by a key-pair owner or a CA;
WHEN the system verifies that the corresponding private key is available and unmodified;
THEN the system shall only proceed with cryptographic operations if the possession assurance is confirmed.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-04-01', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "The server SHALL validate that the PSK identity in the client's 'pre_shared_key' extension matches the identity stored in the server's PSK database.", 'rationale': 'Ensures only authorized clients can establish a connection using a pre-shared key, preventing unauthorized access.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a client sends a ClientHello with a 'pre_shared_key' extension containing a valid PSK identity;
WHEN the server retrieves the stored PSK identity from its database;
THEN the server SHALL accept the PSK identity if it matches the one provided in the extension."}, {'id': 'SEC-04-02', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The server SHALL verify that the TLS version, cipher suite, and ALPN protocol (if any) in the early data match the values associated with the selected PSK.', 'rationale': 'Prevents downgrade attacks and ensures consistency between early data and final handshake parameters.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client sends early data with a specific TLS version, cipher suite, and ALPN;
WHEN the server checks the associated values for the selected PSK;
THEN the server SHALL reject the early data if any of these values differ, and SHALL discard the early data and proceed with a 1-RTT handshake.'}, {'id': 'SEC-04-03', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The server SHALL validate that the ticket age for the selected PSK identity is within a tolerance of 30 seconds of the time since the ticket was issued.', 'rationale': 'Prevents use of stale or replayed session tickets, ensuring freshness of PSK sessions.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a server receives a NewSessionTicket with an obfuscated_ticket_age;
WHEN the server computes the ticket age by subtracting ticket_age_add from PskIdentity.obfuscated_ticket_age modulo 2^32;
THEN the server SHALL reject the PSK if the computed age is more than 30 seconds from the current time, and SHALL proceed with the handshake but reject 0-RTT.'}, {'id': 'SEC-04-04', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': "The server SHALL accept early data only if it has accepted a PSK cipher suite and selected the first key offered in the client's 'pre_shared_key' extension.", 'rationale': 'Ensures early data is only processed when the PSK is properly established and the client has provided a valid key offer.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a client sends a ClientHello with an 'early_data' extension;
WHEN the server has accepted a PSK cipher suite and selected the first key in the 'pre_shared_key' extension;
THEN the server SHALL accept the early data and proceed to process it as per the 0-RTT handshake rules."}, {'id': 'SEC-04-05', 'category': 'Error Handling', 'text': "If the server fails to decrypt a 0-RTT record after accepting early data, the server SHALL terminate the connection with a 'bad_record_mac' alert.", 'rationale': 'Ensures integrity of early data records and prevents processing of corrupted or tampered data.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a server accepts early data and receives a 0-RTT record;
WHEN the server fails to decrypt the record;
THEN the server SHALL send a 'bad_record_mac' alert and terminate the connection."}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'The TLS handshake must establish a secure connection using at least TLS 1.2, with support for TLS 1.3 if available.', 'rationale': 'TLS 1.2 and above provide stronger cryptographic standards and better protection against known attacks. The protocol version must be supported to avoid downgrade attacks.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client and server attempting to establish a TLS connection;
WHEN the client sends a ClientHello with TLS 1.2 or higher;
THEN the server must respond with a ServerHello using a supported version, and the connection must proceed without a protocol_version alert.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'The TLS handshake must negotiate a cipher suite that includes at least 128-bit symmetric encryption and a key exchange method that produces a shared secret of at least 46 bytes.', 'rationale': 'The key exchange must generate a sufficiently long shared secret to prevent brute-force attacks. Modern cipher suites (e.g., ECDHE-RSA-AES128-GCM-SHA256) provide adequate security for current threats.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client and server attempting to negotiate a cipher suite;
WHEN the client and server select a cipher suite with 128-bit encryption and a key exchange method producing a shared secret of at least 46 bytes;
THEN the handshake must complete successfully without a fatal error or insufficient_security alert.'}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': "The server must validate the certificate chain and verify that the CA is known and trusted. If the CA cannot be located or matched, an 'unknown_ca' alert must be generated and the connection must terminate.", 'rationale': "Certificate validation ensures authenticity and prevents man-in-the-middle attacks. The 'unknown_ca' alert is fatal and must be handled to prevent compromised connections.", 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a server receives a certificate chain;
WHEN the CA certificate cannot be located or matched with a known trusted CA;
THEN the server must generate an 'unknown_ca' alert and terminate the connection immediately."}, {'id': 'FUNC-001', 'category': 'Functionality', 'requirement': 'The client and server must exchange ClientHello and ServerHello messages to agree on protocol version, session ID, cipher suite, and compression method, and exchange random values (ClientHello.random and ServerHello.random).', 'rationale': 'These messages are foundational to the handshake and establish the cryptographic parameters for the session.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client and server initiating a TLS handshake;
WHEN the client sends a ClientHello;
THEN the server must respond with a ServerHello containing the agreed protocol version, cipher suite, and random values, and the handshake must proceed to the next step.'}, {'id': 'SEC-004', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': "The system must not allow negotiation of cipher suites that provide insufficient security (e.g., 3DES with 1024-bit RSA key exchange), and must return an 'insufficient_security' alert if such a negotiation occurs.", 'rationale': "The TLS protocol ensures that if a client offers a weak cipher suite, the server must reject it to maintain security, as per the 'insufficient_security' alert mechanism.", 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a client offers a cipher suite with 3DES and a 1024-bit RSA key exchange;
WHEN the server evaluates the offered cipher suite;
THEN the server must return an 'insufficient_security' alert and terminate the handshake."}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The TLS implementation shall support Finite Field Diffie-Hellman (FFDHE) key exchange using groups with minimum bit sizes of 2048 bits, as defined in the Supported Groups Registry (RFC 7919), and shall only negotiate groups with values in the range 256–511 inclusive for FFDHE groups.', 'rationale': 'The FFDHE group strength directly impacts resistance to computational attacks on session key derivation; NIST and RFC 7919 specify minimum security levels for FFDHE groups to ensure confidentiality and integrity of session keys.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client offering a Supported Groups extension with ffdhe2048 and a cipher suite with TLS_DHE_RSA_WITH_AES_128_CBC_SHA;
WHEN the server receives the ClientHello;
THEN it shall select ffdhe2048 as the negotiated group and complete the handshake without failure.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-002', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'The server shall prioritize the Supported Groups extension over the cipher suite preference list when resolving conflicts between ECDHE and FFDHE group selections.', 'rationale': 'The Supported Groups extension provides a more explicit and secure mechanism for group selection than cipher suite preferences, which may contain conflicting or inconsistent group indications.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client offering cipher suites <TLS_DHE_RSA_WITH_AES_128_CBC_SHA, TLS_ECDHE_RSA_WITH_AES_128_CBC_SHA> and a Supported Groups extension <ffdhe8192, secp384p1, ffdhe3072, secp256r1>;
WHEN the server evaluates both preference lists;
THEN it shall select TLS_ECDHE_RSA_WITH_AES_128_CBC_SHA with secp384p1 as the negotiated cipher suite and group.'}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The TLS implementation shall ensure that the contents of the Supported Groups extension are hashed in the Finished message to prevent active MITM attacks from filtering or omitting groups during the handshake.', 'rationale': 'Hashing the group list in the Finished message provides integrity and prevents an attacker from manipulating the group selection without detection, as any modification would cause handshake failure during final validation.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an attacker attempting to omit FFDHE groups from the Supported Groups extension;
WHEN the handshake proceeds;
THEN the Finished message hash will fail validation, and the handshake shall terminate before the peer completes the handshake.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "The client shall reject any GREASE value when it appears in the 'version' field of a ServerHello or HelloRetryRequest message.", 'rationale': 'GREASE values in version fields are used to detect malformed or unexpected protocol versions; rejecting them prevents potential protocol downgrade attacks or invalid state transitions.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a ServerHello message with a GREASE value in the version field;
WHEN the client processes the message;
THEN the connection shall fail immediately with a TLS handshake failure error.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "The client shall reject any GREASE value when it appears in the 'cipher_suite' field of a ServerHello message or in any ServerHello extension.", 'rationale': 'GREASE values in cipher suites can be used to bypass cryptographic configuration checks; rejecting them ensures only valid and known cipher suites are negotiated.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a ServerHello message with a GREASE value in the cipher_suite field;
WHEN the client processes the message;
THEN the connection shall fail with a TLS handshake failure error.'}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "The client shall reject any GREASE value when it appears in the 'signature_algorithm' field of a client CertificateVerify signature in TLS 1.3.", 'rationale': 'Signature algorithm values must be validated to ensure cryptographic integrity; GREASE values could be used to bypass signature validation.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a CertificateVerify message with a GREASE value in the signature algorithm field;
WHEN the client processes the message;
THEN the connection shall fail with a TLS handshake failure error.'}, {'id': 'SEC-004', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The server shall reject any GREASE value when it appears in any Certificate extension in TLS 1.3 or in the signature algorithm of a client CertificateVerify signature.', 'rationale': 'Server-side rejection of GREASE values ensures that client-provided unknown values are not accepted, maintaining integrity of certificate and signature validation.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a Certificate extension or CertificateVerify message containing a GREASE value;
WHEN the server processes the message;
THEN the connection shall fail with a TLS handshake failure error.'}]

[{'id': 'KR-01', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'Cryptographic keys shall be generated within a cryptographic module that is FIPS 140-2 or FIPS 140-3 compliant, with the random bit generator implemented within the same module.', 'rationale': 'Ensures that key generation occurs in a secure environment with cryptographically strong randomness, as specified in NIST SP 800-133.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a key generation module with FIPS 140-2 or 140-3 compliance;
WHEN a key is generated;
THEN the random value used in generation must be produced by a FIPS 140-2 or 140-3 compliant random bit generator within the same module.'}, {'id': 'KR-02', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'Each cryptographic key shall be used for only one purpose (e.g., encryption, digital signatures, key wrapping), and key strength shall be determined based on the expected duration of data protection and adversary sophistication.', 'rationale': 'Prevents key reuse across functions, which could weaken security or allow broader damage if compromised, as per NIST guidance on key usage.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a key intended for use in digital signatures;
WHEN it is used in an encryption function;
THEN a security violation occurs and the system shall reject such usage.'}, {'id': 'KR-03', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'text': "Keys stored in memory shall be split into components and updated frequently to prevent 'burning in' and loss of protection.", 'rationale': 'Mitigates the risk of long-term memory exposure leading to key compromise, as outlined in NIST SP 800-57.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a key stored in memory for more than 30 minutes;
WHEN the key is accessed;
THEN at least one component of the key shall be rotated or updated to prevent static exposure.'}, {'id': 'KR-04', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'All key distribution shall occur via secure channels and keys shall be used within FIPS 140-2 or 140-3 compliant cryptographic modules.', 'rationale': 'Ensures secure transport and protected execution environments for keys, as per NIST SP 800-133.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a key to be distributed;
WHEN transmitted over a network;
THEN the transmission must occur over an encrypted channel and the key must be used in a FIPS 140-2 or 140-3 compliant module.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The application shall validate server certificates at runtime by comparing the retrieved certificate with a pre-embedded certificate or public key, using either full certificate pinning or public key pinning as defined in the implementation strategy.', 'rationale': 'Prevents man-in-the-middle attacks by ensuring the server presents a valid and expected certificate or public key.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a secure HTTPS connection to a backend server;
WHEN the server presents a certificate;
THEN the app shall validate the certificate against the embedded pin; 
IF the validation fails, the app shall fail the connection and log the error without allowing the user to proceed.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The application shall support public key pinning with the ability to pin either the subjectPublicKeyInfo or a specific key type (e.g., RSAPublicKey, DSAPublicKey), and shall preserve algorithm and OID context for key identification.', 'rationale': 'Provides flexibility and access to public key parameters, enabling better detection of key changes and algorithm mismatches.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a server using a certificate with a public key;
WHEN the app extracts the public key from the certificate;
THEN it shall compare the extracted key with the embedded pin;
IF the keys do not match, the connection shall be rejected and a log entry shall be generated.'}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'text': 'The application shall not allow users to bypass certificate pinning warnings, even if they click through alerts, to prevent user-induced security compromises.', 'rationale': 'Ensures that pinning remains a strict security enforcement mechanism, not a configurable bypass option.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a user attempting to access a server with a mismatched certificate;
WHEN a pinning failure occurs;
THEN the app shall block the connection and display a warning message without providing a 'proceed anyway' option."}, {'id': 'SEC-004', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'text': 'The application shall use a hash-based pinning method only if no access to public key parameters or algorithm context is required, and shall ensure the hash is derived using a secure, standardized algorithm (e.g., SHA-256).', 'rationale': 'Hash-based pinning is convenient and reduces data size, but lacks contextual information; this requirement ensures it is used only when context is not needed.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a certificate or public key;
WHEN a hash is computed;
THEN the hash shall be compared to the embedded pin;
IF the hashes do not match, the connection shall be rejected.'}]

[{'id': 'AUTH-PW-01', 'type': 'functional', 'requirement': "The SSH server shall support password authentication using the SSH_MSG_USERAUTH_REQUEST message with the service name 'password' and a plaintext password encoded in ISO-10646 UTF-8.", 'rationale': 'This aligns with the SSH Authentication Protocol (RFC 4252) and ensures interoperability with standard SSH clients.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client sends a password authentication request with a UTF-8 encoded password;
