WHEN the server receives the request;
THEN it shall respond with SSH_MSG_USERAUTH_PASSWD_CHANGEREQ and include a UTF-8 encoded prompt string for password change.'}, {'id': 'AUTH-PW-03', 'type': 'security', 'requirement': 'The SSH server shall disable password authentication if the transport layer does not provide confidentiality (i.e., no encryption cipher) or if no message authentication code (MAC) is present.', 'rationale': 'This prevents insecure transmission of passwords and ensures that authentication messages are protected from eavesdropping and tampering, as per RFC 4252.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client attempts password authentication with no encryption or MAC;
WHEN the server receives the request;
THEN it shall reject the authentication and return SSH_MSG_USERAUTH_FAILURE.'}, {'id': 'AUTH-PW-04', 'type': 'non-functional', 'requirement': 'The SSH server shall normalize user names and passwords using the rules defined in RFC 4013 whenever they are stored in the database or compared to existing entries.', 'rationale': 'This ensures consistent behavior across different operating systems and client software, supporting internationalization and avoiding mismatches due to encoding differences.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user enters a password with non-ASCII characters;
WHEN the password is stored or compared;
THEN it shall be normalized using RFC 4013 rules before storage or comparison.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The system shall validate the signature on a complete CRL using the public key of the CRL issuer, which must match the trust anchor used to validate the target certificate.', 'rationale': 'Ensures the integrity of the CRL and prevents tampering or forgery of revocation data.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a complete CRL with a valid signature and a target certificate with a known trust anchor;
WHEN the system validates the CRL signature using the trust anchor;
THEN the system shall confirm the signature is valid and proceed to the next step.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The system shall compute the interim_reasons_mask based on the CRL distribution point (DP) and issuing distribution point (IDP) CRL extension, following the rules in RFC 5280 Section 6.3.3.', 'rationale': 'Provides a mechanism to determine which revocation reasons are relevant for a given certificate, reducing unnecessary checks and improving efficiency.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a CRL distribution point with reasons and an IDP CRL extension with onlySomeReasons;
WHEN the system processes the CRL;
THEN the interim_reasons_mask shall be set to the intersection of the DP reasons and the IDP onlySomeReasons.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-001', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'The system shall determine if a certificate is revoked by checking one or more CRLs until either the certificate status is determined to be revoked or sufficient CRLs have been checked to cover all reason codes.', 'rationale': 'Ensures comprehensive revocation status determination in accordance with PKIX standards.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a certificate with a CRL distribution points extension;
WHEN the system checks one or more CRLs in sequence;
THEN if a revocation entry is found, the cert_status shall be set to the corresponding reason code; otherwise, it shall remain UNREVOKED.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-002', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'The system shall verify that the delta CRL issuer matches the complete CRL issuer and that the delta CRL contains a matching IDP CRL extension if the complete CRL includes one.', 'rationale': 'Ensures consistency between complete and delta CRLs and prevents invalid or out-of-sync revocation data.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a complete CRL with an IDP extension and a delta CRL;
WHEN the system processes the delta CRL;
THEN the delta CRL issuer must match the complete CRL issuer, and if the complete CRL has an IDP extension, the delta CRL must have a matching IDP extension.'}]

Relevant Assets: End-entity certificates, CA certificates, CRLs (complete and delta), CRL distribution points, local CRL cache.

Trust Boundaries: The boundary between the client application and the CRL validation engine; the boundary between the CRL cache and the external CRL distribution points.

Threats and Risks:
- Man-in-the-middle attacks on CRL distribution (e.g., forged CRLs);
- Tampering with CRL signatures or revocation reasons;
- Exploitation of missing or incorrect IDP extensions to bypass revocation checks;
- Use of outdated or expired CRLs leading to false revocation status.

Mitigations:
- All CRLs must be validated using a trust anchor that matches the target certificate's chain;
- The system shall verify the issuer and scope of CRLs using IDP extensions to ensure proper distribution;
- The system shall validate the signature on both complete and delta CRLs;
- The interim_reasons_mask shall be computed according to RFC 5280 rules to avoid unnecessary revocation checks;
- The system shall only use CRLs that are within their validity period and have a next update value that is not expired.

Proposed Architecture:
- A modular CRL validation engine that receives a certificate and performs CRL lookup via distribution points;
- The engine maintains a local CRL cache and updates it based on time and freshness rules;
- It processes complete and delta CRLs in sequence, validating signatures and issuer scope;
- It computes the interim_reasons_mask dynamically and uses it to filter revocation reason checks;
- The final cert_status is set only if a matching entry is found in a valid CRL with a valid reason code or if the reason code is unspecified due to absence of the extension.

[{'id': 'SEC-HPKE-MESSAGE-CONF', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The HPKE mode used in the protocol shall provide IND-CCA2 security for message confidentiality, assuming the underlying KEM is IND-CCA2-secure and the DH group satisfies the gap Diffie-Hellman (GDH) hardness property in the appropriate subgroup.', 'rationale': 'The analysis in [HPKEAnalysis] and [ABHKLR20] demonstrates that under these conditions, HPKE achieves IND-CCA2 security for message confidentiality, which is a fundamental requirement for secure communication.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a sender encrypts a message using the HPKE Auth mode with a valid KEM and AEAD scheme;
WHEN the recipient decrypts the message using the correct private key;
THEN the message content shall remain confidential and not be revealed to any unauthorized party, even under a chosen-ciphertext attack.'}, {'id': 'SEC-HPKE-SENDER-AUTH', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The HPKE mode shall provide sender authentication, meaning the recipient can verify that the message originated from the claimed sender, for all modes except Base (where it is N/A).', 'rationale': 'The table in Section 9.1 shows that Auth and AuthPSK modes provide sender authentication (Y), while Base does not (N/A). The Auth mode achieves this through authenticated KEM construction as defined in [ABHKLR20].', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a message is encrypted using the HPKE Auth mode;
WHEN the recipient decrypts the message;
THEN the recipient shall be able to verify that the sender is authenticated, and the message shall not be accepted if the authentication fails.'}, {'id': 'SEC-HPKE-KEY-EXPORT', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The HPKE protocol shall support secure key export via the secret export interface, and the exported secrets shall be independent of the message encryption context.', 'rationale': 'The analysis in [HPKEAnalysis] confirms that the secret export interface produces two independent secrets when used, which are critical for key recovery in case of compromise.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a sender uses the secret export interface after encrypting a message;
WHEN the recipient or a recovery agent retrieves the exported secrets;
THEN the secrets shall be independent of the message and shall not reveal any message content or encryption key.'}, {'id': 'SEC-HPKE-KEM-ASSUMPTIONS', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The KEM used in HPKE shall satisfy the following: (1) its Encap()/Decap() interface is IND-CCA2-secure; (2) its AuthEncap()/AuthDecap() interface satisfies multi-user Outsider-CCA, Outsider-Auth, and Insider-CCA security; (3) the shared secret is a uniformly random byte string of length Nsecret, not just a random element of a set prior to encoding.', 'rationale': 'These are explicitly required by [CS01] and [ABHKLR20], and are necessary to ensure the security properties of HPKE are met under classical and post-quantum assumptions.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a KEM is used in HPKE;
WHEN the KEM's shared secret is derived and used in the protocol;
THEN the shared secret shall be uniformly random as a byte string of length Nsecret, and the KEM shall satisfy the defined security notions for both Encap/Decap and AuthEncap/AuthDecap interfaces."}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "Each owner name within the zone that owns authoritative RRSets shall have a corresponding NSEC3 RR. Owner names corresponding to unsigned delegations may have a corresponding NSEC3 RR, but if no NSEC3 RR exists, there shall be an Opt-Out NSEC3 RR covering the 'next closer' name to the delegation.", 'rationale': 'Ensures proper negative response coverage and delegation boundary integrity as defined in RFC 5155.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a DNS query for a non-existent domain name in a zone;
WHEN the server constructs a negative response;
THEN it shall include an NSEC3 RR for the original owner name or an Opt-Out NSEC3 RR for the next closer name if no NSEC3 RR exists.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'Each empty non-terminal in the zone shall have a corresponding NSEC3 RR, unless the empty non-terminal is derived solely from an insecure delegation covered by an Opt-Out NSEC3 RR.', 'rationale': 'Prevents ambiguity in negative responses by ensuring all intermediate nodes are properly proven.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a domain path with an empty non-terminal between the apex and a subdomain;
WHEN a query is made for a name within that path;
THEN the server shall include an NSEC3 RR for that non-terminal unless it is part of an insecure delegation covered by an Opt-Out NSEC3 RR.'}, {'id': 'PERF-001', 'category': 'Performance', 'text': "The TTL value for any NSEC3 RR shall be equal to the minimum TTL value field in the zone's SOA RR.", 'rationale': 'Ensures consistency in DNS response caching and reduces unnecessary revalidation of negative responses.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a DNS zone with a defined minimum TTL;
WHEN an NSEC3 RR is generated;
THEN its TTL shall be set to the minimum TTL value in the zone's SOA RR."}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The Type Bit Maps field of every NSEC3 RR in a signed zone shall indicate the presence of all types present at the original owner name, excluding types solely contributed by the NSEC3 RR itself.', 'rationale': 'Maintains accuracy of type representation in DNSSEC proofs and prevents false positives in type validation.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a domain name with multiple RR types;
WHEN an NSEC3 RR is constructed for that domain;
THEN the Type Bit Maps field shall include all types present at the original owner name, excluding the NSEC3 type itself.'}, {'id': 'SEC-004', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'When constructing NSEC3 RRs, if a hash collision is detected during the process, the system shall reject the current salt and iteration values and restart the signing process with a new salt value.', 'rationale': 'Prevents incorrect proofs due to hash collisions, which could lead to security vulnerabilities.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a DNS zone being signed with a specific salt and iteration;
WHEN a hash collision is detected during NSEC3 RR construction;
THEN the system shall abort and initiate a new signing process with a different salt value.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'requirement': 'The DNS zone shall use NSEC3 RRs to authenticate negative responses, ensuring that the existence or nonexistence of any name within the zone can be cryptographically proven.', 'rationale': 'NSEC3 provides authenticated denial of existence, which prevents attackers from spoofing negative responses and enables secure zone validation as per RFC 5155.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a DNS query for a non-existent name in the zone;
WHEN the server responds with an NSEC3 RR;
THEN the response must include a valid hash chain that proves the nonexistence of the name, and the response must be verifiable using the zone's DNSKEY and RRSIG records."}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'requirement': 'The Opt-Out Flag (O) shall be used only in exceptional circumstances and shall not be enabled by default in zone signing tools.', 'rationale': 'Unsigned names are insecure and vulnerable to manipulation; using Opt-Out by default exposes the zone to risks of undetectable insertion or deletion of records, as specified in RFC 5155, Section 12.2.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a zone with an unsigned delegation;
WHEN an attacker attempts to insert or delete a resource record in an unsigned subzone;
THEN the system shall detect the unauthorized change and reject the request, or log it for audit purposes.'}, {'id': 'PERF-001', 'requirement': 'The DNS server shall limit the number of hash iterations used in NSEC3 responses to a value that minimizes denial-of-service risk without compromising security.', 'rationale': 'High iteration counts increase computational load on servers, especially during negative or wildcard responses, as per RFC 5155, Section 12.2, which identifies a denial-of-service vulnerability.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a high-volume negative query for a non-existent name;
WHEN the server processes the query;
THEN the response time must not exceed 100 milliseconds, and the server must not perform more than 1000 hash iterations per response.'}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'requirement': 'Wildcard records shall not be used if their expanded names (e.g., *.w.example) are unsigned, as such expansions are subject to the same vulnerabilities as unsigned names in an unsigned zone.', 'rationale': 'The expanded names of wildcard records are unsigned, making them vulnerable to insertion or deletion by attackers, as noted in RFC 5155, Section 12.2, which warns against such configurations.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a wildcard record such as *.w.example;
WHEN a query is made for a name under that wildcard;
THEN the expanded name must be either signed or the wildcard record must be disabled or restricted to signed subzones.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'requirement': 'The system shall support ZSK rollover using the pre-publish method, where a new DNSKEY is introduced into the zone without generating new signatures, and the old ZSK remains in the key set until the data in caches expires.', 'rationale': 'This allows for a rapid key switch in case of compromise, as the new key is already distributed before the old key is removed, minimizing exposure.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a zone with an old ZSK and a new ZSK introduced in the key set;
WHEN the zone data is propagated to authoritative servers and cached data expires;
THEN the system shall allow verification of cached data using the old ZSK, and the new ZSK shall be used for all new signatures.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'requirement': "The system shall ensure that during ZSK rollover, all RRSIGs for zone data are signed exclusively with the new ZSK after the 'new RRSIGs' stage, and the old ZSK signatures are removed from the zone data.", 'rationale': 'This ensures that only valid, current signatures are present in the zone, preventing validation failures due to outdated keys.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a zone with both old and new ZSKs in the key set;
WHEN the new RRSIGs are generated and published;
THEN all RRSIGs in the zone data shall be signed with the new ZSK, and no RRSIGs shall be signed with the old ZSK.'}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'requirement': 'The system shall remove the old ZSK from the zone key set only after the data in all authoritative caches has expired, and the new ZSK has been used to sign all new zone data.', 'rationale': 'This prevents validation failures when resolvers attempt to verify data using outdated or missing keys during the transition phase.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a zone with an old ZSK and a new ZSK;
WHEN the old ZSK is removed from the key set;
THEN the system shall ensure that no cached data from the previous version of the zone remains in authoritative servers or resolvers.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'requirement': "The losing operator shall pre-publish the new DNSKEY RRset and its associated RRSIGs signed with the gaining operator's KSK, and the gaining operator shall publish the same DNSKEY RRset in its zone.", 'rationale': 'Ensures that DNSSEC validation can proceed using the new keys before re-delegation, maintaining chain of trust during the transition.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a zone transition from operator A to operator B;
WHEN the new DNSKEY RRset is published by both operators;
THEN DNSSEC validation can proceed without requiring signatures from the losing operator's keys."}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'requirement': "After the re-delegation of NS records to the gaining operator, the losing operator shall stop publishing all DNSKEY and RRSIG records associated with its keys, and the gaining operator shall stop publishing the losing operator's keys once all signatures have expired from caches.", 'rationale': 'Prevents cache pollution and maintains a clear chain of trust post-transition; avoids long-term exposure of obsolete keys.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN the re-delegation has been completed and propagated;
WHEN all RRSIGs signed by the losing operator have expired from DNS caches;
THEN the gaining operator shall cease publishing keys and signatures from the losing operator's DNSKEY RRset."}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'requirement': "If the losing operator does not cooperate and publishes a DNSKEY with a high TTL, the registry shall remove the DS record pointing to the losing operator's DNSKEY and update the NS RRset to point to the gaining operator.", 'rationale': "Prevents long-term validation failures due to obstructive operators and ensures the zone remains secure and valid in the new operator's domain.", 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a non-cooperative losing operator has published a DNSKEY with a high TTL;
WHEN the registry receives a request to transition;
THEN the registry shall remove the DS record and update the NS RRset to point to the gaining operator.'}, {'id': 'NON-001', 'requirement': 'All DNSKEY and RRSIG records in the zone shall have a TTL of no more than 300 seconds during the rollover process to minimize the impact of cache inconsistencies.', 'rationale': 'Short TTLs reduce the risk of validators using outdated or invalid keys from the losing operator, especially in cases of non-cooperation or expired signatures.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a DNSSEC rollover is in progress;
WHEN a resolver attempts to validate a record;
THEN the resolver shall not use a DNSKEY with a TTL greater than 300 seconds if it is associated with a losing operator.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-01', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "Servers publishing TLSA records with DANE-TA(2) usage MUST include the TA certificate in their TLS server certificate chain unless all TLSA records are '2 0 0' records that publish the server certificate in full.", 'rationale': "Per RFC 7671, if the TA certificate is omitted from the server's certificate chain, DANE clients cannot authenticate the server without the TA certificate, which is required for validation via DNS-based TLSA records.", 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a server with DANE-TA(2) configured using a shared TA and a CNAME-based TLSA record;
WHEN the server's certificate chain is validated by a DANE-compliant client;
THEN the client must be able to authenticate the server using the TA certificate obtained from the TLSA record, and the server certificate chain must include the TA certificate."}, {'id': 'SEC-02', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'TLSA records with a selector of SPKI(1) and a matching type of Full(0) MUST NOT be used for DANE-TA(2) deployments.', 'rationale': 'The use of SPKI(1) with Full(0) does not include full TA certificate content, and clients may not be able to verify path length or name constraints from the TA certificate, leading to potential security violations.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a server publishing a TLSA record with selector SPKI(1) and matching type Full(0);
WHEN a client attempts to validate the server certificate chain using DANE-TA(2);
THEN the client SHALL fail validation due to insufficient TA certificate constraints, and the deployment SHALL be rejected.'}, {'id': 'SEC-03', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'TLSA records with a selector of Cert(0) are RECOMMENDED for DANE-TA(2) deployments, as they include the full TA certificate and allow enforcement of TA constraints (e.g., path length, name constraints).', 'rationale': 'The Cert(0) selector ensures that the full TA certificate is available in the TLSA record, enabling clients to validate the certificate chain against TA constraints as defined in RFC 6125.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a server publishing a TLSA record with selector Cert(0) and matching type DANE-TA(2);
WHEN a client validates the server certificate chain using DANE-TA(2);
THEN the client SHALL apply any relevant TA certificate constraints (e.g., path length, name constraints) to validate the chain, and the validation SHALL succeed if the constraints are met.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-01', 'category': 'Functionality', 'text': 'Servers hosting multiple domains under a shared domain structure SHALL use SNI to select the appropriate certificate for each client request.', 'rationale': 'When multiple unrelated customer domains are hosted on a single server, SNI ensures the correct certificate is presented to the client, avoiding misconfiguration or certificate mismatch issues.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a server hosting multiple domains on a single IP address;
WHEN a client connects to the server with a specific domain name;
THEN the server SHALL present the correct certificate for that domain via SNI.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-02', 'category': 'Functionality', 'text': 'A shared TLSA record SHALL be published via a CNAME alias that points to a common TLSA RRset for all services issued by the same TA.', 'rationale': 'This simplifies certificate rotation and reduces the operational burden of managing individual TLSA records per service, as only one TLSA record needs to be updated when the TA configuration changes.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a server with multiple services under a shared domain;
WHEN a client queries the TLSA record for a service;
THEN the query SHALL resolve to a CNAME that points to a shared TLSA RRset containing the TA certificate.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-REQ-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The DoH server shall use HTTPS with HTTP/2 as the transport protocol for all DNS queries and responses, ensuring end-to-end encryption of DNS data.', 'rationale': 'RFC 8484 mandates that DoH use HTTPS with HTTP/2 to protect DNS queries from eavesdropping and tampering.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a DNS query sent via HTTPS over HTTP/2;
WHEN the server receives the request;
THEN the response shall be encrypted and transmitted using HTTP/2 with TLS 1.2 or higher.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-REQ-002', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'For a DNS query with a failure response (e.g., SERVFAIL, NXDOMAIN), the DoH server shall return a 2xx HTTP status code to indicate success, and the client shall interpret this as a failure in DNS resolution.', 'rationale': 'RFC 8484 specifies that a successful HTTP 2xx status code is used for any valid DNS response, regardless of DNS response code, enabling consistent client behavior.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a DNS query with a SERVFAIL response;
WHEN the DoH client receives a 2xx HTTP status code;
THEN the client shall treat the response as a failure and initiate a retry or error handling mechanism.'}, {'id': 'CACHE-REQ-003', 'category': 'Non-functional', 'text': 'The HTTP freshness lifetime in the response header (via Cache-Control: max-age) shall be less than or equal to the smallest TTL in the Answer section of the DNS response, and shall be equal to it if possible.', 'rationale': 'RFC 8484 requires that the freshness lifetime not exceed the smallest TTL to prevent expired DNS records from being served from HTTP caches.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a DNS response with multiple RRsets having TTLs of 30, 600, and 300 seconds;
WHEN the DoH server generates an HTTP response;
THEN the Cache-Control header must include 'max-age=30' to ensure no expired records are served."}, {'id': 'ERROR-REQ-004', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'If a DoH client receives an HTTP status code 401, 415, or 406, it shall retry the query with the same or a different DoH server, depending on the error type.', 'rationale': 'RFC 8484 specifies that clients should retry on authorization failures (401) or unsupported media types (415), and server unavailability (406), to maintain service availability.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a 401 error from a DoH server;
WHEN the client receives the response;
THEN the client shall retry the query with the same server or a different one, as per error type and server policy.'}, {'id': 'TTL-REQ-005', 'category': 'Non-functional', 'text': 'The DoH client shall adjust the DNS TTL of a response by subtracting the Age header value from the original DNS TTL to determine the remaining lifetime of the record.', 'rationale': 'RFC 8484 requires clients to account for the Age header when calculating the remaining DNS TTL to ensure accurate freshness in cached responses.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a DNS response with TTL of 600 seconds and an Age header of 250 seconds;
