THEN the client shall calculate a remaining TTL of 350 seconds and use it for caching.'}]

[{'id': 'FW-SEC-001', 'requirement': 'Firewall policies shall deny all incoming TCP and UDP traffic by default, and only permit specific traffic based on defined application or service rules.', 'rationale': 'Following the principle of least privilege and defense in depth, deny-by-default policies reduce the attack surface for incoming traffic.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a firewall policy configuration for incoming traffic;
WHEN a packet is received on an unlisted port or protocol;
THEN it shall be dropped or logged, and no traffic shall be allowed to pass.'}, {'id': 'FW-SEC-002', 'requirement': 'Firewall policies shall allow only specific ICMPv6 message types and codes for incoming and outgoing traffic, as defined in RFC 4890, and only when necessary for operational functions such as PMTU discovery or neighbor discovery.', 'rationale': 'ICMPv6 is essential for IPv6 operation, but uncontrolled ICMP traffic can be used for reconnaissance or denial-of-service attacks; strict filtering ensures security without disrupting core functionality.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a packet with an ICMPv6 type and code;
WHEN the type and code are not in the approved list;
THEN the packet shall be dropped or logged, and no response shall be sent.'}, {'id': 'FW-SEC-003', 'requirement': 'For IPsec traffic, firewalls shall block ESP and AH protocols except for traffic destined to or originating from designated IPsec gateways on the internal network.', 'rationale': 'Allowing IPsec traffic to all endpoints increases exposure to attacks; limiting access to gateways ensures only authorized devices can establish secure tunnels.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an IPsec ESP or AH packet from a non-designated IP address;
WHEN the packet is received at the firewall;
THEN it shall be dropped or logged, and no forwarding shall occur.'}, {'id': 'FW-SEC-004', 'requirement': 'Inbound application firewalls or proxies shall be implemented in front of any server that lacks sufficient built-in security features to defend against application-specific attacks.', 'rationale': 'Application-level filtering provides an additional layer of defense, preventing malicious payloads from reaching servers and reducing server load and exposure.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a server exposed to external traffic;
WHEN an application firewall or proxy is deployed in front;
THEN all incoming application traffic shall be validated before reaching the server, and malicious payloads shall be blocked or quarantined.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'The web interface shall use TLS 1.2 or higher to encrypt all data in transit between the client and the server.', 'rationale': 'Unencrypted communication exposes sensor configuration data and commands to eavesdropping and man-in-the-middle attacks, especially in untrusted network segments.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client attempting to access the web interface over an unsecured network;
WHEN the connection is established;
THEN the client shall be redirected to a secure HTTPS connection or receive an error indicating the connection is not secure.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'All user access to the sensor configuration interface shall be authenticated using strong credentials (e.g., username and password) and verified through role-based access control (RBAC).', 'rationale': 'Without authentication, adversaries could impersonate engineers and modify sensor parameters, leading to physical process manipulation or denial of control.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an unauthorized user attempts to access the configuration interface;
WHEN the request is processed;
THEN the system shall reject the request and return a 401 Unauthorized response with no exposure of sensitive data.'}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'All sensor configuration commands shall be transmitted with integrity checks to detect unauthorized modification or tampering during transmission.', 'rationale': 'Without integrity checks, adversaries could modify configuration data in transit and undetectably alter sensor behavior, leading to unsafe process operations.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a configuration command is sent from the client to the server;
WHEN the command is intercepted and modified;
THEN the server shall detect the change and reject the command with a clear error message.'}, {'id': 'NET-001', 'category': 'Network Security', 'requirement': 'Wireless access points serving sensor devices shall enforce strong mutual authentication between clients and access points to prevent rogue access point connections.', 'rationale': 'Unauthorized wireless access allows adversaries to connect to sensor networks and manipulate physical processes directly.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a rogue access point is deployed in the OT environment;
WHEN a client attempts to connect to it;
THEN the access point shall reject the connection and log the event.'}, {'id': 'NET-002', 'category': 'Network Security', 'requirement': 'All data transmitted between wireless clients and access points shall be protected using strong encryption (e.g., WPA3 or AES-256) to prevent unauthorized access to unencrypted data.', 'rationale': 'Unencrypted data in wireless traffic can be intercepted and used to manipulate sensor parameters or exfiltrate sensitive operational data.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a wireless client transmits sensor configuration data;
WHEN the data is intercepted by an adversary;
THEN the data shall be encrypted and no plaintext data shall be visible in network traffic.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'When an UPDATE message contains an unrecognized optional transitive attribute, the Partial bit in the attribute flags octet SHALL be set to 1, and the attribute SHALL be retained for propagation to other BGP speakers.', 'rationale': 'Ensures that malformed or unknown attributes do not disrupt route propagation or cause misrouting by allowing partial attribute processing.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an UPDATE message with an unrecognized transitive attribute;
WHEN the message is processed;
THEN the Partial bit is set to 1 and the attribute is retained for propagation to peers.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-001', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'If a valid OPEN message is received and the CollisionDetectEstablishedState attribute is TRUE, the BGP speaker SHALL check for connection collisions and, if a collision is detected, process an OpenCollisionDump event.', 'rationale': 'Prevents multiple BGP sessions from being established between peers due to overlapping connection attempts, ensuring connection uniqueness.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a valid OPEN message with CollisionDetectEstablishedState set to TRUE;
WHEN a connection collision is detected;
THEN an OpenCollisionDump event SHALL be processed and the connection SHALL be terminated as per defined actions.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-002', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'Upon receiving a NOTIFICATION message with a Cease code or a TcpConnectionFails event, the BGP speaker SHALL delete all routes associated with the connection, release BGP resources, drop the TCP connection, and change its state to Idle.', 'rationale': 'Ensures clean termination of failed connections to prevent stale route advertisements and maintain network stability.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a NOTIFICATION message with Cease code or a TcpConnectionFails event;
WHEN the BGP speaker receives it;
THEN all routes for the connection SHALL be deleted, BGP resources SHALL be released, and the state SHALL transition to Idle.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-003', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'When an UPDATE message is received, the BGP speaker SHALL validate each field as specified in Section 6.3, and if the message contains a non-empty WITHDRAWN ROUTES field, the previously advertised routes with matching IP prefixes SHALL be removed from the Adj-RIB-In.', 'rationale': 'Ensures accurate route withdrawal and prevents propagation of obsolete or invalid routes.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an UPDATE message with a non-empty WITHDRAWN ROUTES field;
WHEN the message is processed;
THEN all routes with destinations matching the WITHDRAWN ROUTES field SHALL be removed from the Adj-RIB-In.'}]

[{'id': 'SRS-IP-ADDR-UNIQUENESS-01', 'category': 'Functional', 'description': 'Each IPv6 address assigned to a host interface shall be tested for uniqueness prior to assignment, regardless of whether it is obtained via stateless address autoconfiguration, DHCPv6, or manual configuration.', 'rationale': 'Prevents address collisions and ensures network integrity by verifying address uniqueness before use.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a host interface with an assigned IPv6 address;
WHEN the address is being assigned;
THEN the address shall be tested for uniqueness using Duplicate Address Detection (DAD) and the test shall succeed without collision.'}, {'id': 'SRS-IP-ADDR-DEPRECATION-02', 'category': 'Functional', 'description': 'When an address is deprecated, the system shall ensure that all active communications using that address are completed before the address becomes invalid.', 'rationale': 'Prevents communication failures during site renumbering by allowing a deprecation period during which the new address is used for ongoing sessions.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a valid address with a deprecation period;
WHEN the address's valid lifetime expires;
THEN all ongoing communications using the address shall have transitioned to a preferred address or completed before the address becomes invalid."}, {'id': 'SRS-IP-ADDR-LIFETIME-03', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'description': 'The prefix lifetime for each subnet shall be configured by system administrators to ensure that deprecation periods are long enough to allow most communications to transition to the new address before expiration.', 'rationale': 'Minimizes disruption during site renumbering by ensuring sufficient time for address transitions in active sessions.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a renumbering event;
WHEN a new address is assigned to a host;
THEN the deprecation period shall be long enough that the majority of active connections are using the new address before the old address becomes invalid.'}, {'id': 'SRS-IP-DAD-CONFIG-04', 'category': 'Functional', 'description': 'A per-interface configuration flag shall allow the administrator to disable Duplicate Address Detection (DAD) on a specific interface, with a default value of enabled (DupAddrDetectTransmits = 1).', 'rationale': 'Provides flexibility for environments where DAD overhead outweighs benefits, while maintaining a default safety mechanism.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an interface with DAD disabled;
WHEN an address is assigned;
THEN the system shall not perform DAD, but the address shall still be validated for uniqueness through other means, and the configuration shall be documented.'}]

Actors/Assets: BGP speaker, RPKI cache, prefix-to-AS mapping database (VRP database), local storage.

Key Functional Concerns: Validation of BGP routes using prefix and origin AS information; lookup of VRP entries to determine validation state (NotFound, Valid, Invalid);

Non-Functional Concerns: Performance of lookup operations; consistency of validation state across route processing;

Security Concerns: Prevention of unauthorized route advertisements; integrity of prefix-to-AS mappings; protection against spoofed or invalid origin AS numbers;

Assumptions: The system operates with a database of validated ROA payloads; all BGP routes are processed for validation; the database is updated in real-time;

Missing Information: How updates to the database are propagated; failure modes during validation; integration with routing policy decisions; specific performance or latency requirements.

[{'id': 'RA-GUARD-001', 'type': 'security', 'text': 'RA-Guard must pass any packet whose IPv6 Source Address is not a link-local address (fe80::/10).', 'rationale': 'Prevents RA-Guard from processing packets that originate off-net and would be discarded by the host per RFC 4861, Section 6.1.2.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a packet with an IPv6 source address outside the fe80::/10 range;
WHEN the packet is received by the RA-Guard;
THEN the packet shall be passed to the next layer without processing or blocking.'}, {'id': 'RA-GUARD-002', 'type': 'security', 'text': 'RA-Guard must pass any packet whose Hop Limit is not 255.', 'rationale': 'Prevents RA-Guard from processing packets that would be discarded by the destination host per RFC 4861, Section 6.1.2.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a packet with a Hop Limit value other than 255;
WHEN the packet is received by the RA-Guard;
THEN the packet shall be passed to the next layer without processing or blocking.'}, {'id': 'RA-GUARD-003', 'type': 'functional', 'text': 'RA-Guard must parse the entire IPv6 header chain in the packet to determine if it is an ICMPv6 Router Advertisement message.', 'rationale': 'Ensures accurate identification of Router Advertisements; incomplete parsing could lead to false positives or false negatives, especially when using Destination Options headers or fragmentation.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a packet with a valid IPv6 header chain;
WHEN the RA-Guard processes the packet;
THEN it shall fully parse the header chain and correctly identify whether the packet is an ICMPv6 Router Advertisement message. If the packet is not an ICMPv6 Router Advertisement, it shall be blocked.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'Caches and routers shall use a transport protocol that provides authentication and integrity protection for routing data. If unprotected TCP is used, the cache and router must operate on the same trusted and controlled network.', 'rationale': 'Unprotected TCP lacks authentication and integrity, making it vulnerable to man-in-the-middle attacks and data tampering. The requirement ensures that only trusted peers can exchange routing data.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a router and cache configured with unprotected TCP;
WHEN they attempt to exchange routing data;
THEN the connection is only allowed if both are on the same trusted and controlled network, and the system logs the peer identity and network context for audit purposes.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'Caches and routers shall implement TCP-AO transport [RFC5925] over the rpki-rtr port (323) when available on all platforms, as it is expected to become the mandatory-to-implement transport.', 'rationale': 'TCP-AO provides strong authentication and integrity protection and is the recommended standard for future-proofing the system against evolving threats.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a router and cache with TCP-AO support;
WHEN they establish a session using TCP-AO over port 323;
THEN the session is authenticated with a message authentication code (MAC) of at least 96 bits, and the cryptographic parameters specified in RFC5926 are implemented and tested.'}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'If TCP-AO is not available, caches and routers MAY use TLS transport [RFC5246] over port rpki-rtr-tls (324), with client-side certificate authentication and subjectAltName extension containing one or more iPAddress identities.', 'rationale': 'TLS provides strong encryption and authentication, and the use of iPAddress identities ensures that connections are validated against known, authorized IP addresses, reducing MITM risks.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client router attempting to connect to a cache via TLS;
WHEN the client presents a certificate with a subjectAltName containing an iPAddress;
THEN the cache must validate the IP address against the certificate and reject the connection if no match is found.'}, {'id': 'SEC-004', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "Client routers shall verify the cache's TLS server certificate using subjectAltName dNSName identities and MUST NOT use Common Name (CN-ID) identifiers for authentication.", 'rationale': 'Using subjectAltName dNSName ensures that certificate validation is based on DNS names, which are more reliable and less prone to spoofing than Common Name fields.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a TLS connection between a router and a cache;
WHEN the cache presents a TLS certificate;
THEN the router must validate the certificate using subjectAltName dNSName identities and must reject the connection if the certificate contains no dNSName or if the dNSName does not match the connection IP address.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-001', 'category': 'Functionality', 'text': 'Caches and routers shall enable keep-alive messages when available in the chosen transport protocol to reduce exposure to dropped but non-terminated sessions.', 'rationale': 'Keep-alives prevent session timeouts due to inactivity, ensuring that routing data remains available and sessions are not prematurely terminated.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a router attempting to connect to a cache;
WHEN the transport protocol supports keep-alives;
THEN the system shall send periodic keep-alive messages to maintain the session, and the session shall remain active for at least 30 seconds after inactivity.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'requirement': 'The system shall parse ROA signed objects in DER format as defined in RFC 6488, including extraction of the AS identifier and IPv6 prefix list.', 'rationale': 'ROA objects are defined in RFC 6488 as signed objects with a DER-encoded payload containing AS identifiers and IPv6 prefixes, which must be parsed for validation.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a DER-encoded ROA object with a valid AS identifier and IPv6 prefix list;
WHEN the system parses the object;
THEN it shall extract the AS identifier and IPv6 prefix list with no errors, and the extracted values shall match the structure defined in RFC 6488.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'requirement': 'The system shall validate the cryptographic signature of the ROA object using X.509 PKIX resource certificates as defined in RFC 6487 and RFC 5280.', 'rationale': 'The integrity of the ROA must be ensured through cryptographic verification using X.509 PKIX certificates, which are the standard for RPKI trust chains.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a ROA object with a signature;
WHEN the system validates the signature against a trusted X.509 PKIX resource certificate;
THEN the signature shall be verified as valid, and the system shall return a success status if the signature is valid and the certificate is trusted.'}, {'id': 'PERF-001', 'requirement': 'The system shall process a single ROA object in less than 100 milliseconds under typical conditions.', 'rationale': 'Real-time route validation requires low-latency processing to support dynamic routing decisions in BGP systems.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a typical ROA object of 1 KB in size;
WHEN the system parses and validates the object;
THEN the total processing time shall be less than 100 milliseconds.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-1-RTT-HEADER-PROTECTION', 'text': 'The header form, fixed bit, spin bit, reserved bits, key phase, and packet number length fields of a 1-RTT packet shall be protected using header protection as defined in Section 5.4 of [QUIC-TLS].', 'rationale': 'Ensures integrity and confidentiality of packet header fields that define packet structure and protocol state.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a 1-RTT packet header field is received by an endpoint;
WHEN the header protection is applied;
THEN the endpoint shall validate that the reserved bits are zero and the packet is not malformed, and shall discard any packet with non-zero reserved bits after header protection removal.'}, {'id': 'SEC-1-RTT-SPIN-BIT-BEHAVIOR', 'text': 'The spin bit in a 1-RTT packet shall be used only after version negotiation and connection establishment are complete, and shall be set to a value that reflects the current spin state for the network path.', 'rationale': 'The spin bit enables passive RTT measurement; its use is restricted to post-handshake to prevent misuse during handshake or early connection phases.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a 1-RTT packet is sent on a network path;
WHEN the packet contains a valid spin bit value;
THEN the endpoint shall maintain a spin value per network path and update it based on packet number progression and peer behavior as specified in Section 17.4 of the source.'}, {'id': 'SEC-1-RTT-SPIN-BIT-ENABLEMENT', 'text': 'Each endpoint shall allow administrators to disable the spin bit globally or on a per-connection basis, and shall disable the spin bit for at least one in every 16 network paths or connection IDs in a random selection.', 'rationale': 'Ensures that QUIC connections disabling the spin bit are commonly observed, preventing signal leakage and enabling accurate RTT estimation across the network.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an endpoint is configured to disable the spin bit;
WHEN a packet is sent on a network path;
THEN the endpoint shall set the spin bit to any value and ignore incoming spin bits, and shall disable the spin bit for at least one in every 16 network paths or connection IDs.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-1-RTT-PACKET-LENGTH', 'text': 'The packet number field in a 1-RTT packet shall be 1 to 4 bytes long, with its length encoded in the Packet Number Length field (which is one less than the actual length in bytes).', 'rationale': 'Ensures correct parsing of packet numbers and prevents misinterpretation of packet sequence during transmission.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a 1-RTT packet is received;
WHEN the packet number length field is parsed;
THEN the actual length of the packet number field shall be the value of the field plus one, and the packet number field shall be correctly interpreted as a sequence number.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The server shall only push resources that are directly referenced in the original request (e.g., via Link headers or embedded links) and shall not push resources outside the scope of the initial request.', 'rationale': 'Prevents unauthorized or out-of-scope resource exposure; aligns with the requirement that PUSH_PROMISE frames are tied to explicit client requests.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client requests a document with embedded image links AND the server pushes an image via PUSH_PROMISE;
WHEN the client receives the PUSH_PROMISE frame;
THEN the image must be referenced in the original request or in a Link header field, and the server must not push a resource for a different domain (e.g., https://www.example.org/doc).'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The server shall validate that the certificate presented by the origin server for a pushed response matches the DNS-ID of the original request domain.', 'rationale': 'Ensures server authority is properly validated, preventing man-in-the-middle attacks or spoofed responses.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client requests a resource from example.com;
WHEN the server pushes a response for a resource on example.com;
THEN the certificate presented by the server must be valid and match the DNS-ID of example.com. If the certificate does not match, the client shall reject the pushed response with a stream error.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-001', 'category': 'Functionality', 'text': 'The server shall send PUSH_PROMISE frames prior to any DATA frames that reference the promised resources, to avoid race conditions where clients discover embedded links after receiving the response.', 'rationale': 'Ensures clients can see that a resource will be pushed before discovering embedded links, improving user experience and avoiding confusion.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client requests a document with embedded image links;
WHEN the server sends a PUSH_PROMISE frame for an image;
THEN the PUSH_PROMISE frame must be sent before any DATA frame that references the image link in the document.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-002', 'category': 'Functionality', 'text': 'The client shall not issue any new requests for a resource that has been pushed, until after the promised stream has closed.', 'rationale': 'Prevents redundant or conflicting requests and ensures consistent resource delivery.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client receives a PUSH_PROMISE for an image;
WHEN the client chooses to accept the pushed response;
THEN the client shall not send a new request for that image until the promised stream has been closed by the server or the client has explicitly canceled it.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'description': "The web application shall enforce clickjacking protection by setting the X-Frame-Options HTTP response header to 'DENY' for all sensitive pages.", 'rationale': 'The X-Frame-Options header is supported by major browsers and prevents pages from being embedded in iframes, which is a primary defense against clickjacking attacks.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a sensitive page is accessed by a user;
WHEN the page is loaded in an iframe;
THEN the browser shall block the iframe from rendering the page, and the response header X-Frame-Options: DENY shall be present in the HTTP response.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'description': 'The web application shall configure the Content Security Policy (CSP) with the frame-ancestors directive to prevent framing, replacing deprecated X-Frame-Options usage.', 'rationale': 'The frame-ancestors directive in CSP Level 2 is the modern standard and supersedes X-Frame-Options, providing better compatibility and support for future browser versions.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a sensitive page is accessed;
WHEN the page is loaded in an iframe;
THEN the response header 'Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors ' shall be present and include the domain of the site, and the iframe shall not render the page."}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'category': 'Security', 'description': 'The web application shall implement a frame-breaking script in the HEAD of every page that should not be framed, to provide fallback protection for legacy browsers that do not support X-Frame-Options or CSP.', 'rationale': 'Legacy browsers may not support X-Frame-Options or CSP, and a frame-breaking script ensures defense in such cases, as described in the best-for-now legacy browser frame-breaking script.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a page is loaded in a legacy browser that does not support X-Frame-Options;
WHEN the page is embedded in an iframe;
THEN the script shall detect if it is in a frame and redirect the top-level window to the current page, or hide the body and prevent rendering of the page.'}, {'id': 'SEC-004', 'category': 'Security', 'description': "The web application shall use the SameSite cookie attribute with a value of 'lax' or 'strict' on all session cookies to prevent them from being sent in requests made to pages within iframes.", 'rationale': 'SameSite cookies prevent session cookies from being included in iframe requests, which mitigates clickjacking attacks that require authentication, as per the RFC 6265bis specification.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user is authenticated and attempts to perform an action on a sensitive page within an iframe;
WHEN the iframe makes a request to the server;
THEN the session cookie shall not be sent, and the action shall not proceed, preventing unauthorized execution of actions.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The ObjectInputStream used to deserialize incoming payloads shall only resolve instances of the Bicycle class, and any attempt to deserialize a class not matching Bicycle.class.getName() shall result in an InvalidClassException.', 'rationale': 'Prevents unauthorized deserialization of malicious gadget classes that could lead to remote code execution.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a request with a serialized object of a class other than Bicycle;
WHEN the ObjectInputStream attempts to deserialize it;
THEN an InvalidClassException shall be thrown with message 'Unauthorized deserialization attempt' and the class name."}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The JVM shall be launched with the -javaagent:name-of-agent.jar parameter to harden ObjectInputStream behavior globally, enabling block-listing of known malicious types.', 'rationale': 'Provides a scalable, non-invasive method to harden deserialization without requiring code changes or re-architecting messaging systems.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a JVM instance launched with the -javaagent:name-of-agent.jar parameter;
WHEN it attempts to deserialize a malicious object;
THEN the deserialization shall be blocked and an exception shall be thrown.'}, {'id': 'NON-001', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'text': 'The system shall maintain backward compatibility with existing serialized object formats for at least one year after deployment, unless re-architecting is required due to new deserialization vulnerabilities.', 'rationale': 'Ensures minimal disruption to existing clients during security hardening.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client using an older version of the API;
WHEN it sends a serialized object;
THEN the service shall accept and process it without failure or degradation in functionality.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-REQ-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The API shall not use any deserialization library that allows untrusted input to define object types, including versions of fastjson < v1.2.68, XStream < v1.4.17, YamlBeans < v1.16, or XMLDecoder in JDK.', 'rationale': 'These libraries allow arbitrary class deserialization from untrusted input, enabling remote code execution via object instantiation.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user input payload containing untrusted data;
WHEN deserialization is attempted using any of the listed unsafe libraries;
THEN the API shall reject the payload and return a 400 Bad Request error with a message indicating invalid input format.'}, {'id': 'SEC-REQ-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The API shall use JSON.Net with `TypeNameHandling` set to `None` when deserializing user input.', 'rationale': 'Setting `TypeNameHandling` to `None` prevents the deserializer from using user-controlled data to determine object types, reducing the risk of arbitrary object instantiation.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a JSON payload with a user-controlled type field;
WHEN deserialization is attempted;
THEN the API shall reject the payload and return a 400 error, and the deserialization process shall not instantiate any object based on user-controlled type data.'}, {'id': 'SEC-REQ-003', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The API shall not use the `JavaScriptSerializer` with a `JavaScriptTypeResolver` or any user-controlled type input.', 'rationale': 'The combination of `JavaScriptSerializer` and `JavaScriptTypeResolver` allows attackers to control object instantiation, leading to potential remote code execution.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a request using JavaScriptSerializer with a user-controlled type;
WHEN the API attempts to deserialize the input;
THEN the API shall reject the request with a 400 error and log the attempt for audit purposes.'}, {'id': 'SEC-REQ-004', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The API shall not use the .NET `BinaryFormatter` for any deserialization operations.', 'rationale': 'The `BinaryFormatter` is inherently unsafe and cannot be secured, as it allows arbitrary object instantiation from untrusted data streams.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a request that would use BinaryFormatter;
WHEN the API attempts to deserialize the input;
THEN the API shall reject the request with a 400 error and return a message indicating unsupported format.'}, {'id': 'SEC-REQ-005', 'category': 'Non-functional', 'text': 'The API shall use only signed data when deserializing messages that are processed from untrusted sources.', 'rationale': 'Signing data before serialization ensures that only authenticated and tamper-proof messages are processed, reducing the risk of malicious payloads being deserialized.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an untrusted message with no signature;
WHEN the API attempts to deserialize it;
THEN the API shall reject the message and return a 401 Unauthorized error.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'Session tokens and user credentials shall not be stored in localStorage or IndexedDB. If stored, they must be encrypted using a key derived from a user-supplied passphrase or a Web Crypto-generated key that is not recoverable from the browser environment.', 'rationale': 'Client-side storage is accessible by JavaScript and can be read by any script running in the same origin. Storing session tokens or credentials in such storage exposes them to XSS and other attacks.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user logs in with a session token;
WHEN the application attempts to store the token in IndexedDB;
THEN the token is encrypted using a Web Crypto key derived from a user-supplied passphrase that is never persisted or stored in the browser.
AND the application does not allow access to the decrypted token via JavaScript.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'All data retrieved from IndexedDB shall be treated as untrusted input and validated against a schema before being used in any application logic or rendered to the user interface.', 'rationale': 'IndexedDB is vulnerable to XSS attacks where malicious data can be injected and executed via script. Input validation must be applied to prevent injection attacks.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user submits data to the application;
WHEN the data is retrieved from IndexedDB;
THEN it is validated against a defined schema;
AND no script or executable content is rendered or evaluated in the application.'}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'All HTML links and JavaScript window.open calls that open new tabs or windows shall use the attribute 'rel="noopener noreferrer"' or the corresponding windowFeatures parameter to prevent tabnabbing and reduce referrer leakage.', 'rationale': "Without proper configuration, a newly opened tab can access the parent page's content via the opener object, enabling tabnabbing attacks. This configuration mitigates such risks and prevents referrer data from being exposed.", 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user clicks a link or uses JavaScript to open a new tab;
WHEN the link or window.open call is made;
THEN the attribute 'rel="noopener noreferrer"' is applied to HTML links;
AND the windowFeatures parameter includes 'noopener,noreferrer' in all window.open calls;
AND the HTTP response header 'Referrer-Policy: no-referrer' is set on all responses.'}, {'id': 'SEC-004', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'The application shall not allow user-supplied input to be used to dynamically create or evaluate JavaScript code (e.g., via eval() or Function() constructors).', 'rationale': "Dynamic script evaluation introduces a DOM-based XSS vulnerability, allowing attackers to execute arbitrary code in the user's browser environment.", 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user submits input that would be used to create a JavaScript function;
WHEN the application attempts to evaluate or execute the input;
THEN the input is rejected and an error is returned;
AND no JavaScript code is executed in the browser context.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': "When serving user-provided files, the server shall set the Content-Disposition header to 'attachment' to force download and prevent inline rendering.", 'rationale': 'Prevents unintended execution of files in the browser, reducing the risk of Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) and malicious code execution.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a user uploads a file and attempts to view it in the browser;
WHEN the server responds with a Content-Disposition header set to 'attachment';
THEN the browser will prompt the user to download the file instead of rendering it inline."}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': "When serving user-provided files, the server shall set the Content-Type header to 'application/octet-stream' for unknown or binary files.", 'rationale': 'Ensures that binary files are not interpreted as executable content, reducing the risk of unintended execution or XSS.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a user uploads a file with an unknown or binary extension;
WHEN the server responds with a Content-Type header set to 'application/octet-stream';
THEN the browser will treat the file as a binary download and not attempt to interpret or execute it."}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': "The server shall set the X-Content-Type-Options header to 'nosniff' for all file responses to prevent MIME type sniffing.", 'rationale': 'Prevents the browser from interpreting file types based on content, which could lead to unintended execution of malicious scripts.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a server responds to a file request with a Content-Type header that may be misinterpreted;
WHEN the X-Content-Type-Options header is set to 'nosniff';
THEN the browser will not perform MIME type sniffing and will treat the file as binary."}, {'id': 'SEC-004', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'The server shall remove or set non-informative values for the Server and X-Powered-By headers to prevent server fingerprinting.', 'rationale': 'Exposure of server technology details can enable attackers to identify known vulnerabilities or misconfigurations.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a request is made to the server;
WHEN the response includes the Server or X-Powered-By header;
THEN the header shall either be absent or set to a non-informative value (e.g., 'webserver') to avoid revealing technology details."}, {'id': 'SEC-005', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': "For private or sensitive content, the server shall set the X-Robots-Tag header to 'noindex, nofollow' to prevent search engines from indexing or following links on the content.", 'rationale': 'Prevents sensitive content from being discovered or indexed by search engines, reducing exposure to public view.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a file is marked as private or sensitive;
WHEN the server responds with an X-Robots-Tag header set to 'noindex, nofollow';
THEN compliant search engine crawlers will not index or follow links on the resource."}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'All user-supplied input used in LDAP search filters shall be escaped using the correct LDAP encoding function to prevent LDAP injection attacks.', 'rationale': 'LDAP injection occurs when user input is directly embedded into LDAP search filters without proper escaping. Using the correct LDAP encoding function ensures that special characters (e.g., \, #, <, >, , ;, =) are properly escaped and do not alter the intended query structure.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user inputs a search term containing special characters such as < or >;
WHEN the application constructs an LDAP search filter with that input;
THEN the filter is properly escaped and does not result in unauthorized queries or access to unintended entries.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'The application shall not use user input to construct attribute names or table/column names in LDAP queries.', 'rationale': 'Using user input to define attribute names in LDAP queries is a known vulnerability. Input validation or query redesign should be applied to ensure only predefined, safe attribute names are used.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user attempts to input a value for an attribute name;
WHEN that value is used in a query;
THEN the application either rejects the input or maps it to a predefined, valid attribute name, and does not use the raw user input in the query.'}, {'id': 'PERF-001', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'requirement': 'The LDAP search functionality shall respond to queries within 500 milliseconds under normal load conditions.', 'rationale': 'Performance is critical for user experience and system responsiveness in real-time search applications.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user performs a search with a valid input;
WHEN the search is executed;
THEN the response time is less than 500 milliseconds, measured under a load of 100 concurrent users.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-01', 'type': 'security', 'description': 'All service-to-service communications (synchronous and asynchronous) must use encrypted protocols (e.g., HTTPS, TLS) to protect data in transit.', 'rationale': 'Prevents eavesdropping and man-in-the-middle attacks during service-to-service communication, as per OWASP ASVS #1.1.2.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a service-to-service communication using HTTP or gRPC;
WHEN data is transmitted between services;
THEN the communication must use TLS 1.2 or higher, and the handshake must be verified with certificate validation.'}, {'id': 'SEC-02', 'type': 'security', 'description': 'Data assets (e.g., user credentials, order details) must not be exposed in service-to-service communications or message queues without proper masking or encryption.', 'rationale': 'Prevents sensitive data exposure, aligning with OWASP Top 10-2017 A3: Sensitive Data Exposure.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a service-to-service call or message queue transmission;
WHEN sensitive data (e.g., user password, credit card) is involved;
THEN the data must be encrypted or masked in the payload, and the transmission must not include plaintext values.'}, {'id': 'SEC-03', 'type': 'non-functional', 'description': 'Each service must have a clearly defined set of permissions that follows the principle of least privilege, based on its required data access and service interactions.', 'rationale': 'Reduces attack surface by limiting service capabilities to only what is necessary, as per OWASP ASVS #1.1.6.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a service-to-storage or service-to-service communication;
WHEN a service is granted access to a data asset;
THEN the access must be limited to the minimal data and operations required for its functionality.'}, {'id': 'ARCH-01', 'type': 'architecture', 'description': 'A graphical representation of the application architecture must be created using a service call graph or data flow diagram, showing all services, message queues, and data flows.', 'rationale': 'Provides a clear view of system structure and data movement, supporting threat modeling and attack surface analysis (OWASP ASVS #1.1.2).', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN the service definitions and data relationships;
WHEN a diagram is generated;
THEN it must include all application functionality services, infrastructure services, data storages, message queues, and the relationships between them in a clear, labeled format.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-01', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The API shall implement a strong authentication mechanism, such as multi-factor authentication (MFA), for all user access attempts.', 'rationale': 'Multi-factor authentication significantly reduces the risk of unauthorized access, especially when credentials are compromised.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user attempts to authenticate via the API;
WHEN the user provides a password and a second factor (e.g., SMS or authenticator app);
THEN the authentication shall succeed only if both factors are valid and properly verified.'}, {'id': 'SEC-02', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'All API responses shall not include technical details such as call stacks or internal error messages in error responses.', 'rationale': "Exposing internal details in error messages can aid attackers in understanding the system's internals and exploiting vulnerabilities.", 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN an error occurs during an API call;
WHEN the client receives an error response;
THEN the response shall contain only a generic error message (e.g., 'An error occurred') and shall not include technical details such as stack traces or internal system messages."}, {'id': 'SEC-03', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'Sensitive information such as API keys, passwords, or tokens shall not be included in URLs or query parameters in any request.', 'rationale': 'URLs are logged by web servers and can be captured by attackers, making them a high-risk vector for credential exposure.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a request containing sensitive data;
WHEN the request is made via a GET method;
THEN the sensitive data shall be transmitted in an HTTP header (e.g., Authorization header) rather than in the URL or query string.'}, {'id': 'SEC-04', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'All API responses shall include the following security headers: `Cache-Control: no-store`, `Content-Type: application/json`, `Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains`, and `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`.', 'rationale': 'These headers prevent sensitive data from being cached, protect against MIME sniffing, and enforce secure communication via HTTPS, reducing exposure to man-in-the-middle attacks and content misinterpretation.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an API response is returned;
WHEN the response is consumed by a client;
THEN the response headers shall include `Cache-Control: no-store`, `Content-Type: application/json`, `Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains`, and `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-COOKIE-01', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'Session cookies shall have the Secure attribute set to ensure transmission only over secure channels (e.g., HTTPS using TLS).', 'rationale': 'The Secure attribute ensures cookies are only sent over secure channels, protecting confidentiality from active network attackers.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user agent making an HTTP request to the application server;
WHEN the request is transmitted over an insecure channel (e.g., HTTP);
THEN the user agent shall not include the session cookie in the Cookie header.'}, {'id': 'SEC-COOKIE-02', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'Session cookies shall have the HttpOnly attribute set to prevent access via JavaScript scripts.', 'rationale': 'The HttpOnly attribute prevents scripts from accessing cookies, reducing the risk of cross-site scripting (XSS) attacks.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a web page loaded in a browser;
WHEN a JavaScript script attempts to access the session cookie via window.document.cookie;
THEN the script shall not be able to retrieve the session cookie value.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-COOKIE-01', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'The application shall parse the Cookie header according to the RFC 6265 grammar, including handling of multiple cookie pairs with the same name.', 'rationale': 'Servers must not rely on the serialization order of cookies in the header to avoid inconsistencies in session handling.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a server receives a Cookie header with two cookies having the same name but different Path or Domain attributes;
WHEN the server processes the header;
THEN the server shall not rely on the order of the cookies in the header to determine which cookie to use.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-COOKIE-02', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'The application shall parse cookie-date values using the RFC 6265 date parsing algorithm, including handling of two-digit years and time fields.', 'rationale': 'Proper parsing ensures accurate interpretation of expiration dates and prevents invalid or malformed date inputs.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a cookie with a date string such as 'Mon, 01 Jan 2025 12:00:00 GMT';
WHEN the server parses the date;
THEN the parsed date shall be correctly interpreted with the year value incremented by 1900 if in the range 70–99."}]

[{'id': 'SEC-WS-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "The server shall perform a TLS handshake on HTTPS connections and reject the connection if the client's hostname in the SNI extension does not match a valid host name in the server's certificate.", 'rationale': 'Ensures mutual TLS validation and prevents man-in-the-middle attacks during the initial connection phase.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client connects to the server using an HTTPS endpoint with a SNI extension containing an invalid hostname;
WHEN the server processes the handshake;
THEN the server shall close the connection and return a 400 Bad Request or 403 Forbidden error, depending on configuration.'}, {'id': 'SEC-WS-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "The server shall validate the Origin header field from the client's WebSocket handshake to ensure it matches a known, approved origin domain or IP range.", 'rationale': 'Prevents cross-origin attacks and unauthorized access from untrusted domains or networks.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client attempts to connect from an unapproved origin;
WHEN the server receives the Origin header;
THEN the server shall return a 403 Forbidden HTTP status code and abort the WebSocket handshake.'}, {'id': 'SEC-WS-003', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The server shall accept only WebSocket connections that include a valid Sec-WebSocket-Key header field with a base64-encoded value of at least 16 bytes.', 'rationale': "The Sec-WebSocket-Key is used in the server's handshake to verify the client's intent to establish a valid connection.", 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client sends a WebSocket handshake without a Sec-WebSocket-Key header;
WHEN the server attempts to process the handshake;
THEN the server shall reject the connection and return a 426 Upgrade Required error code with a Sec-WebSocket-Version header indicating supported versions.'}, {'id': 'SEC-WS-004', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': "The server shall respond to a valid WebSocket handshake with an HTTP 101 Switching Protocols status code, an Upgrade header with value 'websocket', a Connection header with value 'Upgrade', and a Sec-WebSocket-Accept header field computed from the client's Sec-WebSocket-Key and the string '258EAFA5-E914-47DA-95CA-C5AB0DC85B11' using SHA-1 hashing and base64 encoding.", 'rationale': 'Ensures compliance with the WebSocket protocol specification for secure connection establishment.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client sends a valid WebSocket handshake with a Sec-WebSocket-Key header;
WHEN the server processes the handshake;
THEN the server shall generate a Sec-WebSocket-Accept header with the value derived from SHA-1 hashing and base64 encoding as defined in RFC 6455, and include it in the 101 Switching Protocols response.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'All WebSocket connections must use TLS encryption to protect data in transit.', 'rationale': 'Ensures confidentiality and integrity of sensor data during transmission, as per standard secure communication practices.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a WebSocket connection between an IoT device and the dashboard;
WHEN the connection is established;
THEN the data transmitted must be encrypted using TLS 1.2 or higher.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-001', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'The server shall send a Close frame with status code 1000 when the connection is closed normally after data transmission is complete.', 'rationale': 'Standardizes normal closure behavior as defined in RFC 6455, ensuring interoperability and predictable behavior.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a successful data transmission;
WHEN the connection is closed;
THEN a Close frame with status code 1000 must be sent.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-002', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'If a message is received that violates the expected data type (e.g., binary in a text-only context), the endpoint shall terminate the connection with status code 1003 and log the error.', 'rationale': 'Prevents malformed data from being processed, aligning with the defined behavior in Section 7.4.1 of RFC 6455.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a text-only endpoint receives a binary message;
WHEN the message is received;
THEN the connection shall be terminated with status code 1003 and an error log entry shall be created.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-003', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'If a message exceeds the maximum allowed size (e.g., 10MB), the endpoint shall terminate the connection with status code 1009 and return a message indicating the size violation.', 'rationale': 'Prevents memory exhaustion and ensures compliance with defined message size limits in the protocol specification.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a message larger than 10MB is received;
WHEN the message is received;
THEN the connection shall be terminated with status code 1009 and a message indicating size violation shall be returned.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-004', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'Upon abnormal closure (e.g., network loss), the client shall attempt to reconnect using a random initial delay between 0 and 5 seconds, followed by truncated binary exponential backoff for subsequent attempts.', 'rationale': 'Prevents denial-of-service attacks by limiting reconnection frequency and aligns with the recommended recovery strategy in Section 7.2.3 of RFC 6455.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client experiences an abnormal closure;
WHEN the client attempts to reconnect;
THEN the first attempt shall have a random delay between 0 and 5 seconds, and each subsequent attempt shall use a longer delay based on truncated binary exponential backoff.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The QPACK module shall enforce a maximum dynamic table capacity defined by the SETTINGS_QPACK_MAX_TABLE_CAPACITY parameter received from the decoder. Any attempt to set a capacity exceeding this limit shall trigger a connection error of type QPACK_ENCODER_STREAM_ERROR.', 'rationale': 'Prevents denial-of-service via oversized dynamic tables; aligns with HTTP/3 specification.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a decoder stream with SETTINGS_QPACK_MAX_TABLE_CAPACITY set to 1000 AND an encoder attempting to set capacity to 1001;
WHEN the encoder sends a SET_DYNAMIC_TABLE_CAPACITY instruction;
THEN the decoder shall respond with a QPACK_ENCODER_STREAM_ERROR.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-001', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'The QPACK module shall support string literals that begin mid-byte, using an N-bit prefix (N between 2 and 8 inclusive), with a single bit Huffman flag, followed by a (N-1)-bit prefix integer for string length.', 'rationale': 'Extends RFC7541 to allow non-byte-aligned string literals, improving flexibility in data encoding.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a string literal starting mid-byte with a 4-bit prefix;
WHEN the module parses the literal;
THEN it shall correctly extract the Huffman flag, string length, and data, and decode using the Huffman table from RFC7541 Appendix B.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-002', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'The QPACK module shall allow encoder instructions to reference existing entries in the static or dynamic table via name references, or transmit a string literal for new entries.', 'rationale': 'Reduces redundant data transmission by reusing existing entries, improving compression efficiency.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an encoder wishing to add a new entry;
WHEN it references an existing entry in the dynamic table;
THEN the module shall transmit a reference instead of a full entry, and the decoder shall resolve the reference correctly.'}, {'id': 'NON-001', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'text': 'The QPACK module shall not allow closure of either the encoder or decoder stream. Any closure attempt shall be treated as a H3_CLOSED_CRITICAL_STREAM connection error.', 'rationale': 'Ensures stable operation of the encoder-decoder pipeline; prevents disruption of ongoing compression operations.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a connection with active encoder and decoder streams;
WHEN either stream is closed;
THEN the endpoint shall trigger a H3_CLOSED_CRITICAL_STREAM error and terminate the connection.'}]

[{'id': 'SCM-01', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'Pull requests must be reviewed by at least two team members before merging. This requirement shall be enforced through protected branch policies in the SCM system.', 'rationale': 'Prevents unauthorized or untrusted code from being merged into the main branch, reducing the risk of malicious code deployment.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a pull request is submitted to the main branch;
WHEN it is reviewed by at least two team members;
THEN it shall be merged only after both approvals are recorded in the SCM system.'}, {'id': 'SCM-02', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'No auto-merge rules shall be enabled in the SCM system (e.g., GitHub, GitLab). Manual review of pull requests is required for all merges.', 'rationale': 'Auto-merging can bypass human review and introduce unvetted code into production environments.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a pull request is submitted;
WHEN the system is configured to allow auto-merge;
THEN the system shall prevent auto-merging and require manual approval for all merges.'}, {'id': 'PIPELINE-01', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'All pipeline execution must occur in isolated nodes to prevent cross-contamination or privilege escalation.', 'rationale': 'Isolated execution environments reduce the risk of compromised pipeline steps affecting other builds or systems.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a pipeline is triggered;
WHEN it runs on a non-isolated node;
THEN the pipeline shall fail with an error and a security alert shall be generated.'}, {'id': 'SECRETS-01', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'No secrets (e.g., API keys, passwords) shall be hardcoded in code repositories or CI/CD configuration files.', 'rationale': 'Hardcoded secrets can be exposed in source code and lead to unauthorized access to sensitive systems.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a configuration file is scanned for secrets;
WHEN it contains hardcoded credentials;
THEN the scan shall detect the presence of secrets and trigger a failure in the merge process.'}, {'id': 'SECRETS-02', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'All secrets must be encrypted at rest and in transit, and must not be logged or printed to console during pipeline execution.', 'rationale': 'Prevents exposure of sensitive credentials in logs or system history files, reducing the risk of data leakage.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a pipeline step uses a secret;
WHEN the secret is printed to the console or logged;
THEN the pipeline shall fail and a security alert shall be generated.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'All container images must be scanned for vulnerabilities using an open-source scanner (e.g., Trivy or Grype) before being pushed to any registry or deployed to Kubernetes.', 'rationale': 'Early detection of known vulnerabilities (CVEs) in images prevents insecure images from entering production environments, reducing the risk of exploitation.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an image is built and pushed to a registry;
WHEN it is scanned using Trivy or Grype;
THEN the scan result must be reported and the pipeline must fail if any critical or high-severity vulnerabilities are found.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The Kubernetes cluster must enforce image provenance using the ImagePolicyWebhook admission controller to reject pods that use unapproved images.', 'rationale': 'The ImagePolicyWebhook prevents unauthorized or unapproved images from being deployed, ensuring that only images that meet defined security policies are used in production.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a pod definition specifies an image;
WHEN the image is not approved by the ImagePolicyWebhook (e.g., base image not allowed, scan not recent);
THEN the Kubernetes API server must reject the pod creation request with an error message indicating the reason.'}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'All container images must be built using minimal base images (e.g., distroless or empty images) to reduce the attack surface.', 'rationale': 'Minimal images contain fewer packages and no shells or package managers, significantly reducing the potential for unknown vulnerabilities and privilege escalation.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a container image is built;
WHEN it is based on a distroless or empty image;
THEN it must not include a shell, OS package manager, or unnecessary system packages, and the image size must be verified to be under 100 MB.'}, {'id': 'SEC-004', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'text': 'The deployment pipeline must provide visibility into what is being deployed, including image details, pod configuration, and deployment targets (clusters, namespaces, nodes).', 'rationale': 'Visibility into deployment details enables timely detection and response to security policy violations and helps ensure compliance with organizational policies.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a deployment is initiated;
WHEN the pipeline runs;
THEN the output must include the image name, version, components, vulnerabilities, target namespace, cluster, and node, and this information must be logged and accessible in the pipeline UI.'}, {'id': 'SEC-005', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'All containers must run with a non-root user and a read-only root filesystem, and the pod security context must enforce these settings for all containers.', 'rationale': 'These settings prevent privilege escalation and unauthorized file system modifications, mitigating attacks that rely on root access or file system manipulation.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a pod definition is created;
WHEN the security context is applied;
THEN the container must run as a non-root user, and the root filesystem must be read-only, as defined in the YAML specification.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'All secrets must be stored in an external secrets manager rather than in Kubernetes Secrets resources.', 'rationale': 'Kubernetes Secrets are vulnerable to exposure if a container has access to the filesystem or if the etcd database is compromised. External secrets managers provide centralized control, rotation, and cross-cluster support, reducing the risk of secret exposure.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a container image with a secret embedded in its filesystem;
WHEN the image is deployed to the cluster;
THEN the secret is not accessible via the container filesystem or any running process, and the secret is managed via an external secrets manager.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The etcd database must be encrypted at rest using Kubernetes encryption at rest feature (v1.13+ beta), and all backups must be encrypted using a well-reviewed backup and encryption solution.', 'rationale': 'Access to etcd could provide attackers with full visibility into the cluster state, including secrets. Encryption at rest and backups ensures that even if an attacker gains access to backups or etcd, they cannot read the secret content.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an attacker has access to an unencrypted etcd backup;
WHEN the backup is decrypted;
THEN the content of any Kubernetes Secret is not readable by the attacker.'}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'All running containers must be monitored for anomalous behavior, including shell execution, mounting of sensitive host paths, and unexpected access to sensitive files such as /etc/shadow.', 'rationale': 'Runtime anomalies like shell execution or unauthorized file access can indicate compromise. Monitoring enables early detection and response to threats in real time.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a container is running with elevated privileges;
WHEN a shell is executed or a sensitive file is accessed;
THEN an alert is generated and logged within 1 second of the event occurrence.'}, {'id': 'SEC-004', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'text': "The container runtime must enforce a restricted Pod Security Admission policy with the 'restricted' level as the default for all pods.", 'rationale': "The 'restricted' level enforces pod hardening best practices, such as disallowing privileged containers and restricting host access, reducing the attack surface of deployed workloads.", 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a new pod is deployed;
WHEN it is created with a security context that allows privileged access or direct host path mounting;
THEN the deployment is rejected by the Pod Security Admission controller.'}, {'id': 'SEC-005', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'All container images must be scanned using open-source tools such as Trivy or Gitleaks before deployment to detect exposed secrets (e.g., API tokens, passwords) in the filesystem.', 'rationale': 'Exposed secrets in container images can be accessed by any user with access to the unencrypted filesystem, whether during build, in a registry, or at runtime.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a container image is built;
WHEN it is scanned using Trivy or Gitleaks;
THEN no sensitive resources (e.g., API keys, passwords) are found in the container filesystem.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The database shall be hosted exclusively within a private subnet and shall not have any direct internet-facing endpoints or public IP addresses.', 'rationale': 'Prevents direct exposure of sensitive data to external parties and reduces the attack surface.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN the database is deployed in a private subnet;
WHEN a network scan is performed;
THEN no direct internet-facing endpoints or public IP addresses are detected on the database.'}, {'id': 'NET-001', 'category': 'Network Infrastructure', 'text': 'The frontend application shall be accessible via an API gateway or internet-facing load balancer located in a public subnet.', 'rationale': 'Provides a secure, managed entry point for user traffic and enables centralized logging and monitoring.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user attempts to access the application;
WHEN the request is routed through an API gateway;
THEN the request is successfully forwarded to the frontend server in the public subnet.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'All communication between the frontend, backend server, and database shall occur over encrypted channels using TLS 1.2 or higher.', 'rationale': 'Ensures data confidentiality and integrity during transit between components.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a request is made from the frontend to the backend;
WHEN the request is transmitted;
THEN the connection uses TLS 1.2 or higher and is validated by the backend server.'}, {'id': 'TRUST-001', 'category': 'Trust Boundaries', 'text': 'A trust boundary shall exist between the API gateway and the backend server, and between the backend server and the database, requiring mutual authentication and identity verification for each component.', 'rationale': 'Establishes clear trust boundaries to prevent unauthorized access and ensures components only trust verified peers.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a backend server attempts to access the database;
WHEN it sends a request;
THEN the request is authenticated using an ephemeral identity and verified by the database server.'}, {'id': 'DEV-001', 'category': 'Development & Access', 'text': 'Developer access to the backend server shall be provided only through a bastion host, and the bastion host shall be configured with strict access controls and logging.', 'rationale': 'Reduces the risk of unauthorized access during development and ensures auditability of access events.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a developer attempts to access the backend server;
WHEN they connect via a bastion host;
THEN access is granted only after authentication and logging is recorded.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The application shall implement authentication and authorization mechanisms at the application layer, using secure tokens (e.g., JWT) and role-based access control (RBAC), ensuring only authorized users can access protected resources.', 'rationale': 'The application must manage its own authentication and authorization in PaaS or IaaS models, as per the shared responsibility model, and these are developer-responsible components.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user attempting to access the application;
WHEN the user provides valid credentials;
THEN the system shall authenticate the user and assign appropriate permissions based on role.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The containerized application shall be deployed within a secure network environment with network access control lists (NACLs) configured to restrict inbound and outbound traffic to only necessary ports and services.', 'rationale': 'Network access control is a developer responsibility in IaaS and PaaS models, and proper NACLs are essential to prevent unauthorized access and mitigate DDoS attacks.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a containerized instance running on the cloud platform;
WHEN an unauthorized IP attempts to connect to the instance;
THEN the instance shall block the connection based on NACL rules.'}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The application shall be protected against DDoS attacks using a cloud-based DDoS protection service (e.g., AWS Shield, GCP Cloud Armor, or Azure DDoS Protection) with rate limiting and traffic routing capabilities enabled.', 'rationale': 'Cloud providers offer DDoS protection services that can be implemented at the infrastructure or application layer, and enabling them is a best practice for handling traffic spikes and malicious payloads.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a high-volume traffic event;
WHEN the system detects abnormal traffic patterns;
THEN the DDoS protection service shall automatically mitigate the attack and maintain service availability.'}, {'id': 'NON-001', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'text': 'The application shall support at least 10,000 concurrent users under normal load conditions and maintain 99.9% uptime during peak traffic periods.', 'rationale': 'Scalability and availability are critical for containerized applications, especially when handling traffic spikes, and PaaS/IaaS models offer scalability through containerization.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a traffic spike of 10,000 concurrent users;
WHEN the application is under load;
THEN the system shall maintain performance and availability with no more than 0.1% downtime.'}]

[{'id': 'LOG-01', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'The network ingress filter shall log all packets that are dropped due to source address spoofing or invalid source IP addresses.', 'rationale': 'Logging dropped packets provides visibility into suspicious traffic patterns and enables monitoring for potential attacks.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a packet with a source address outside the allowed prefix range;
WHEN it is dropped by the ingress filter;
THEN a log entry shall be generated with the source IP address, destination IP address, protocol, and timestamp.'}, {'id': 'FILT-01', 'category': 'Functional', 'requirement': 'The ingress filter on each router shall allow only traffic originating from source addresses within a known, intentionally advertised prefix range.', 'rationale': 'Restricting traffic to known prefixes prevents source address spoofing and mitigates attacks such as SYN floods and UDP/ICMP flooding.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a packet with a source address outside the configured prefix range;
WHEN it arrives at the router;
THEN the packet shall be dropped and not forwarded to internal systems.'}, {'id': 'FILT-02', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'The system shall not allow forged packets destined for system diagnostic ports (e.g., UDP ports 37, 53) from outside the administrative domain to reach internal systems.', 'rationale': 'Such packets can be used in UDP flooding attacks to exhaust system resources or trigger unintended behavior in diagnostic services.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a UDP packet destined for a diagnostic port from an external source;
WHEN it arrives at an internal system;
THEN it shall be blocked and logged if the source address is outside the administrative domain.'}, {'id': 'MON-01', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'requirement': 'The system shall provide real-time monitoring of dropped packets and generate alerts when the drop rate exceeds a threshold of 100 packets per second.', 'rationale': 'Monitoring dropped packets helps detect ongoing attacks and enables timely response actions.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a sustained drop rate of 100 packets per second;
WHEN detected;
THEN an alert shall be generated and logged for network operations personnel.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'requirement': 'The router shall implement rate limiting on internal communications between the control processor and forwarding engine to prevent denial-of-service attacks that exploit high-speed forwarding engine capabilities.', 'rationale': 'The forwarding engine supports high-speed links, and the control processor cannot handle similar traffic rates. Without rate limiting, an attacker could overwhelm the internal link, causing synchronization issues or service disruption.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a high-volume packet stream sent from the forwarding engine to the control processor;
WHEN the control processor receives more than 10,000 packets per second;
THEN the router shall drop excess packets and log the event with a severity level of 'High'."}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'requirement': 'The router shall detect and prevent cache thrashing in the forwarding table by monitoring cache hit/miss ratios and triggering a warning when the ratio drops below 70% for more than 30 seconds.', 'rationale': 'Cache thrashing can overload internal communications by causing repeated requests to the control processor for routing decisions, leading to performance degradation or out-of-sync routing tables.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a sustained stream of packets with non-standard forwarding rules;
WHEN the forwarding cache hit ratio falls below 70% for 30 consecutive seconds;
THEN the system shall generate a warning log entry and notify the administrator via an alert channel.'}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'requirement': 'The router shall prevent the installation of more than 100 dynamically generated ACL entries per hour, and shall reject any ACL installation triggered by intrusion detection system alerts if the total ACL count exceeds 90.', 'rationale': 'Exhausting ACL resources can prevent future attacks from being filtered and may cause ACL processing to be handled by the route processor, which is not designed for such tasks.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN an intrusion detection system triggers an ACL installation;
WHEN the total number of ACL entries exceeds 90;
THEN the router shall reject the installation and log the event with a severity level of 'Critical'."}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'requirement': 'The network shall not instantiate state for unverified hosts without completing a handshake to validate their address.', 'rationale': 'Prevents attackers from establishing persistent connections and consuming server resources without authentication.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client attempting to connect to a server without a valid handshake;
WHEN the server receives the connection;
THEN the server shall not hold state and shall reject the connection without initiating any resource-intensive operations.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'requirement': 'The network shall implement mechanisms to make it difficult for attackers to simulate legitimate user behavior, such as using computational puzzles or reachability testing.', 'rationale': 'Legitimate users can perform tasks that are computationally expensive for bots, thereby discriminating against automated attacks.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an attacker attempting to initiate a resource consumption attack;
WHEN the system requires a computational puzzle or reachability test;
THEN the attack shall be delayed or rejected, and legitimate users with standard computing power shall be able to complete the test within a reasonable time frame (e.g., under 10 seconds).'}, {'id': 'FUNC-001', 'requirement': 'Routing protocols shall support graceful degradation under overload conditions, including the ability to drop invalid routes and send infinite metrics to peers.', 'rationale': 'Ensures that routing systems do not fail catastrophically when under attack, allowing for recovery once the attack is mitigated.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a router receiving a route update during overload;
WHEN the router lacks sufficient memory;
THEN the router shall drop the route and send an infinite metric to its peers;
AND the route shall be re-established once the overload condition resolves.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-002', 'requirement': 'Network devices shall not perform actions on unauthenticated hosts, and autoconfiguration shall only allow access to network services after authentication is established.', 'rationale': 'Unauthenticated autoconfiguration creates attack surfaces that can be exploited by malicious actors to gain unauthorized access to network resources.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an unauthenticated device attempting to autoconfigure;
WHEN the device is not authenticated;
THEN the device shall be denied access to network services and shall not be allowed to establish any network-level connections.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'requirement': 'The router shall rate-limit the generation of ICMP Destination Unreachable messages by the control plane to prevent DoS amplification and ensure operational stability.', 'rationale': 'Uncontrolled generation of ICMP messages can be exploited in DoS attacks, especially when messages are sent in large volumes to trigger responses from other systems.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a router under normal operation with legitimate traffic;
WHEN a DoS attack attempts to flood the control plane with ICMP messages;
THEN the router shall generate no more than 100 ICMP messages per second, and the rate limiter shall be activated only when traffic exceeds this threshold.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'requirement': "The router shall apply different rate limits for distinct ICMP Destination Unreachable codes (e.g., 'fragmentation needed and DF set', 'Packet Too Big') to allow for differentiated traffic handling.", 'rationale': 'Different ICMP error types correspond to different network conditions; treating them separately allows for more granular and accurate traffic management.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a router receiving packets with a 'fragmentation needed and DF set' error;
WHEN the router processes such packets;
THEN the rate limit for this error type shall be set to 50 messages per second, and the system shall generate a monitoring alert when this limit is reached."}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'requirement': 'The router shall rate-limit TTL/hop limit expired packets before forwarding them to the control plane component that generates ICMP errors.', 'rationale': 'TTL-expired packets may be sent to the control plane as part of a DoS attack; limiting their processing prevents resource exhaustion in the control plane.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a flood of TTL-expired packets;
WHEN the router receives such packets;
THEN no more than 100 such packets per second shall be processed by the control plane, and the router shall generate an alert when the limit is reached.'}, {'id': 'SEC-004', 'requirement': 'The router shall distribute the generation of ICMP error messages to Line Card CPUs to prevent overloading the control plane component.', 'rationale': 'Distributing error message generation reduces the risk of the control plane being overwhelmed during high-volume attacks or normal traffic spikes.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a high volume of TTL-expired packets;
WHEN the router processes them;
THEN at least 75% of the ICMP error messages shall be distributed to Line Card CPUs, and the control plane shall not generate more than 50 messages per second.'}]

[{'id': 'R-6.1.1', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The system shall reject any BGP route announcement that includes a prefix not globally routable.', 'rationale': 'Non-globally routable prefixes may originate from private or invalid address spaces, which could lead to misrouting or security vulnerabilities.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a BGP route announcement with a prefix not globally routable;
WHEN the system processes the announcement;
THEN it shall reject the route and log the event with a severity level of 'Warning'."}, {'id': 'R-6.1.3', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The system shall reject BGP routes that are too specific (e.g., /32 prefixes) unless explicitly allowed by policy.', 'rationale': 'Overly specific routes can result in routing instability or incorrect routing decisions, especially when not aligned with network topology.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a BGP route announcement with a /32 prefix;
WHEN the system evaluates the route against the policy;
THEN it shall reject the route if it is not explicitly permitted by configuration, and log the event with a severity level of 'Info'."}, {'id': 'R-6.1.4', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The system shall reject BGP routes that belong to the local AS, unless explicitly permitted by policy.', 'rationale': 'Local AS routes may be misinterpreted as external routes, leading to incorrect routing decisions or potential loops.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a BGP route announcement from a prefix within the local AS;
WHEN the system evaluates the route;
THEN it shall reject the route unless explicitly allowed by configuration, and log the event with a severity level of 'Warning'."}, {'id': 'R-6.1.5', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The system shall reject BGP route announcements for IXP LAN prefixes.', 'rationale': 'IXP LAN prefixes are internal and not intended for external routing, and their presence in BGP tables can cause routing confusion or misdirect traffic.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a BGP route announcement for an IXP LAN prefix;
WHEN the system evaluates the route;
THEN it shall reject the route and log the event with a severity level of 'Error'."}, {'id': 'R-6.1.6', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The system shall accept only the default route for customer or upstream peer relationships when explicitly requested, and reject all other routes in such cases.', 'rationale': 'Providing full routing tables to customers or upstreams may expose them to unnecessary complexity or security risks; a default route is sufficient for basic connectivity.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a customer or upstream peer requesting only the default route;
WHEN the system receives a BGP update;
THEN it shall accept only the default route and reject all other routes, and log the event with a severity level of 'Info'."}, {'id': 'R-7.1', 'category': 'Non-functional', 'text': 'The system shall implement BGP route flap dampening with adjusted thresholds as defined in RFC 7196 and RIPE recommendations.', 'rationale': 'Route flap dampening helps prevent network instability caused by frequent route changes, but must be configured carefully to avoid unintended harm.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a BGP route that flaps frequently;
WHEN the system detects the flapping;
THEN it shall apply dampening and only allow the route to be re-advertised after a configured timeout, and log the event with a severity level of 'Info'."}, {'id': 'R-8.1', 'category': 'Non-functional', 'text': "The system shall enforce a maximum number of routes accepted from each peer, with limits configured based on the peer's expected routing table size and router memory capacity.", 'rationale': 'Unbounded route advertisements can overwhelm routers and degrade performance or cause outages.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a peer advertising more than the configured maximum number of routes;
WHEN the system receives the update;
THEN it shall drop the excess routes and log the event with a severity level of 'Warning'."}]

[{'id': 'LOG-001', 'type': 'functional', 'description': 'The system shall record the detection time of an incident event from the source system.', 'rationale': 'Detection time is a key metric for incident response and is required by IODEF to define the timeline of an incident.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an incident event is detected by a network device;
WHEN the event is logged;
THEN the system shall record the detection time with a precision of at least millisecond-level accuracy.'}, {'id': 'LOG-002', 'type': 'functional', 'description': 'The system shall record the start time of incident response actions initiated by an operator.', 'rationale': 'Response initiation time is critical for assessing response effectiveness and is part of the IODEF incident timeline structure.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a response action is initiated by a security operator;
WHEN the action is executed;
THEN the system shall record the start time of the action with a precision of at least second-level accuracy.'}, {'id': 'LOG-003', 'type': 'functional', 'description': 'The system shall record the report time when an incident is formally reported to stakeholders.', 'rationale': 'Report time is a required field in IODEF and supports accountability and compliance reporting.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an incident is reported to a stakeholder;
WHEN the report is submitted;
THEN the system shall record the report time with a precision of at least second-level accuracy.'}, {'id': 'LOG-004', 'type': 'non-functional', 'description': 'The system shall retain incident data for a minimum of 180 days.', 'rationale': 'Retention period is necessary to support forensic analysis and compliance with incident reporting standards.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an incident record is generated;
THEN the record shall be stored for at least 180 days from the report time.'}]

[{'id': 'SRS-URPF-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The system shall implement enhanced feasible-path uRPF (EFP-uRPF) on all customer-facing interfaces to validate source addresses of incoming packets.', 'rationale': 'EFP-uRPF avoids dropping legitimate packets by including all prefixes with the same origin AS in the RPF list, preserving directionality and ensuring no false drops due to route selection conflicts.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a packet with a source address in a customer prefix P1 received at AS4 via AS2 or AS3;
WHEN the origin AS of the prefix is AS1 and the prefix is learned via different interfaces;
THEN the packet shall be accepted on any interface without being dropped, and the RPF list shall include all prefixes with origin AS1.'}, {'id': 'SRS-URPF-002', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'text': 'The system shall maintain a set of unique origin ASes from customer-facing interface routes and apply EFP-uRPF only to customer interfaces for prefixes sharing the same origin AS.', 'rationale': 'This ensures that the EFP-uRPF algorithm operates on relevant customer traffic while avoiding unnecessary application on lateral peer or transit provider interfaces.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a set of prefixes learned via different interfaces with the same origin AS;
WHEN the system processes Adj-RIBs-In for customer interfaces;
THEN it shall form a set of prefixes with common origin AS and include them in the RPF list for all customer interfaces where any of those prefixes were received.'}, {'id': 'SRS-URPF-003', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The system shall not apply loose uRPF on customer-facing interfaces, as it compromises directionality and may drop legitimate traffic.', 'rationale': 'Loose uRPF only validates that the source address is reachable in the FIB, which may allow packets from unreachable or spoofed sources to pass, violating integrity and directionality.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a packet with a source address in a customer prefix P1;
WHEN the packet is received via a customer-facing interface;
THEN the system shall reject the packet only if the source address is unreachable in the FIB, and shall use EFP-uRPF instead to validate directionality.'}]

[{'id': 'REQ-SEC-01', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'Routers connecting the enterprise to external networks shall be configured with packet and routing filters at both ends of the link to prevent packet and routing information leakage.', 'rationale': 'Prevents exposure of internal private address space to external networks, reducing the risk of ambiguous routing and potential spoofing.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a router connecting the enterprise to an external network;
WHEN packet and routing information is transmitted;
THEN no private IP addresses or routing information shall be visible to external networks.'}, {'id': 'REQ-INT-02', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'DNS clients outside the enterprise shall not see private IP addresses used by the enterprise.', 'rationale': 'Avoids ambiguity in routing and potential misrouting due to private addresses being visible externally.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a DNS query from an external client;
WHEN the query targets an enterprise host;
THEN only public IP addresses shall be returned, and no private IP addresses shall appear in the response.'}, {'id': 'REQ-INT-03', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'The enterprise shall operate two DNS authority servers for each zone containing both public and private hosts: one visible from public networks with only public addresses, and one accessible only from private networks with full address data.', 'rationale': 'Ensures consistency and prevents ambiguity in address resolution while maintaining privacy of internal addresses.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a DNS zone with both public and private hosts;
WHEN a public client queries the zone;
THEN only public addresses are returned;
AND WHEN a private client queries the zone;
THEN all addresses (public and private) are returned and are consistent across both servers.'}, {'id': 'REQ-OP-04', 'category': 'Operational', 'text': 'Private address space shall be allocated from a randomly selected subset of the reserved private address pool to minimize the risk of address uniqueness violations during future network merges.', 'rationale': 'Reduces the likelihood of overlapping private addresses when multiple organizations use private addressing and later establish connectivity.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a private address allocation scheme;
WHEN two organizations merge and establish IP connectivity;
THEN no overlapping private addresses shall exist between the two networks.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-USM-001', 'type': 'security', 'description': "The system shall authenticate incoming SNMPv3 messages using the user's localized authentication key and parameters as defined in the USM model.", 'rationale': 'Ensures message integrity and prevents tampering by verifying the authenticity of the message using a shared secret key.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN an incoming message with a valid msgUserName and msgAuthoritativeEngineID;
WHEN the authentication module is invoked with the user's authKey and wholeMsg;
THEN the system shall return success if the authentication is valid, and an error indication (authenticationFailure) if the authentication fails."}, {'id': 'SEC-USM-002', 'type': 'security', 'description': "The system shall validate that the message is within the time window defined by the authoritative engine's boot and time values.", 'rationale': 'Prevents replay attacks by ensuring messages are not older than a defined time window (±150 seconds).', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a message with msgAuthoritativeEngineID, msgAuthoritativeEngineBoots, and msgAuthoritativeEngineTime;
WHEN the local snmpEngineBoots and snmpEngineTime are compared;
THEN if the message is outside the time window (e.g., boot value differs or time drift exceeds 150 seconds), the system shall increment usmStatsNotInTimeWindows and return an error indication (notInTimeWindow) with a securityLevel of authNoPriv.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-USM-003', 'type': 'functional', 'description': 'The system shall increment the usmStatsUnknownUserNames counter and return an error indication (unknownSecurityName) if the user name is not found in the LCD.', 'rationale': 'Provides visibility into failed access attempts due to invalid user names.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a message with a msgUserName not present in the LCD;
WHEN the system attempts to retrieve user data;
THEN the usmStatsUnknownUserNames counter shall be incremented and an error indication (unknownSecurityName) shall be returned with the OID and value of the counter.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-USM-004', 'type': 'functional', 'description': 'The system shall increment the usmStatsUnsupportedSecLevels counter and return an error indication (unsupportedSecurityLevel) if the requested securityLevel is not supported by the user.', 'rationale': 'Ensures that only supported security levels are processed, preventing invalid or insecure operations.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a message requesting a securityLevel not supported by the user;
WHEN the system checks user capabilities;
THEN the usmStatsUnsupportedSecLevels counter shall be incremented and an error indication (unsupportedSecurityLevel) shall be returned with the OID and value of the counter.'}]

Actors/Assets: SNMP engines (both originators and recipients), user accounts with authentication keys, network devices that exchange SNMP messages.

Key Functional Concerns: Authentication of outgoing and incoming SNMP messages using HMAC-MD5 and HMAC-SHA-96 protocols; derivation of K1 and K2 keys from authKey; computation of Message Authentication Code (MAC) via MD5 or SHA-96;

Non-Functional Concerns: Performance of key derivation and MAC computation; message size and processing overhead;

Security Concerns: Protection against message tampering and replay attacks; prevention of unauthorized message origin; integrity of authentication key usage across engines;

Assumptions: All SNMP engines involved have access to the same user database and shared authentication keys; the authKey is stored securely and not transmitted;

Missing Information: No details on key management lifecycle, key rotation policies, or handling of failed authentication attempts; no specification of error logging or monitoring for authentication failures; no mention of support for multiple authentication protocols in parallel or fallback mechanisms.

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'When a create request for an instance-level access control is received and the implementation does not support instance-level granularity, an inconsistentName error must be returned.', 'rationale': 'Ensures that invalid access requests are rejected at the protocol level, preventing malformed or inconsistent access control configurations.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an access control request for instance-level granularity;
WHEN the system detects that instance-level granularity is not supported;
THEN an inconsistentName error shall be returned to the client.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-001', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': "A 'family' of subtrees is defined by a combination of vacmViewTreeFamilySubtree and vacmViewTreeFamilyMask values. For each sub-identifier of the subtree, if the corresponding bit in the mask is zero, any sub-identifier value matches; if the bit is one, the sub-identifier must be exactly equal.", 'rationale': 'Provides a clear and precise rule for subtree matching, enabling consistent and predictable access control behavior.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a subtree and a mask;
WHEN a subtree is evaluated against a family;
THEN the object identifier is included in the family if, for each sub-identifier, either the mask bit is zero or the sub-identifier values match exactly.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-002', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': "When creating MIB views, the 'excluded' vacmViewTreeFamilyEntries must be created before the 'included' entries.", 'rationale': 'Ensures that access control configurations are built in a safe, deterministic order to avoid unintended access exposure.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a view creation request;
WHEN the system is creating entries for a view;
THEN all excluded entries shall be created before any included entries are created.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-003', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': "When deleting MIB views, the 'included' vacmViewTreeFamilyEntries must be deleted before the 'excluded' entries.", 'rationale': 'Prevents potential access exposure during view deletion by ensuring that included entries are removed first.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a view deletion request;
WHEN the system is deleting entries for a view;
THEN all included entries shall be deleted before any excluded entries are deleted.'}, {'id': 'NON-001', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'text': 'The number of families of view subtrees for a given MIB view shall not be restricted beyond resource constraints on the overall number of entries in the vacmViewTreeFamilyTable.', 'rationale': 'Ensures scalability and avoids artificial limits that could prevent effective configuration of complex MIB views.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a MIB view with multiple subtrees;
WHEN the system is configured with a large number of families;
THEN the system shall not impose artificial limits on the number of families, except as dictated by overall resource constraints.'}]

[{'id': 'SNMP-REQ-001', 'category': 'Functional', 'description': 'Upon receipt of a GetNextRequest-PDU, the SNMP entity shall process each variable binding in the variable-binding list to locate the lexicographically next variable accessible by the request.', 'rationale': 'This enables traversal of MIB tables in a structured, tabular manner as defined in RFC 3416.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a GetNextRequest-PDU with variable bindings for sysUpTime, ipNetToMediaPhysAddress, and ipNetToMediaType;
WHEN the request is processed;
THEN the response shall contain a variable binding for the next lexicographic successor of the requested variable name, with the value field populated accordingly.'}, {'id': 'SNMP-REQ-002', 'category': 'Functional', 'description': "If no lexicographic successor exists for a variable binding, the response shall set the value field of the corresponding variable binding to 'endOfMibView' and retain the name field as the original request name.", 'rationale': 'This provides a clear endpoint for table traversal, preventing infinite loops or invalid responses.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a GetNextRequest-PDU with a variable binding whose name has no lexicographic successor;
WHEN the request is processed;
THEN the Response-PDU shall include a variable binding with value 'endOfMibView' and name matching the original request name."}, {'id': 'SNMP-REQ-003', 'category': 'Non-functional', 'description': 'The size of the Response-PDU message shall not exceed the maximum message size of the originator and a local constraint of the receiving entity.', 'rationale': 'Ensures message transmission feasibility and avoids message fragmentation or loss due to size constraints.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a Response-PDU generated from a GetNextRequest-PDU;
WHEN the message size is evaluated;
THEN the size shall be less than or equal to both the local constraint and the maximum message size of the originator.'}, {'id': 'SNMP-REQ-004', 'category': 'Error Handling', 'description': "If variable binding processing fails due to an unexpected error, the Response-PDU shall be re-formatted with the same request-id and variable-bindings as the original request, error-status set to 'genErr', and error-index set to the index of the failed binding.", 'rationale': 'Provides a standardized error response to allow clients to identify and recover from processing failures.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a failed variable binding processing;
WHEN the Response-PDU is generated;
THEN the error-status field shall be 'genErr' and error-index shall point to the failed binding's index."}, {'id': 'SNMP-REQ-005', 'category': 'Non-functional', 'description': "If the generated Response-PDU exceeds the maximum message size, an alternate Response-PDU shall be generated with error-status 'tooBig', error-index zero, and an empty variable-bindings field.", 'rationale': 'Prevents transmission of oversized messages and ensures system stability under size constraints.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a Response-PDU that exceeds the maximum message size;
WHEN the message is too large;
THEN an alternate Response-PDU shall be generated with error-status 'tooBig', error-index zero, and empty variable-bindings field."}]

[{'id': 'SEC-01', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The system shall use SNMPv2c with community-based authentication to retrieve metrics from managed devices.', 'rationale': 'Community-based authentication is defined in the snmpCommunityGroup object group and is mandatory for SNMP entities supporting community-based authentication as per RFC 3418.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an SNMPv2c-enabled device with a valid community string;
WHEN a Get-Request is sent to retrieve system metrics;
THEN the request shall be authenticated using the community string and return valid data without error.'}, {'id': 'MON-01', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'The system shall collect and store system-level metrics including sysDescr, sysUpTime, sysContact, and sysLocation from managed devices.', 'rationale': 'The systemGroup object group defines common objects for all managed systems, and these metrics are essential for device health monitoring.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a device with SNMP enabled;
WHEN the monitoring system retrieves metrics via SNMP;
THEN sysDescr, sysUpTime, sysContact, and sysLocation shall be collected and stored in the database with a timestamp.'}, {'id': 'ALERT-01', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'The system shall generate an alert when an authentication failure is detected via the snmpInBadCommunityNames object.', 'rationale': 'The snmpCommunityGroup object group includes snmpInBadCommunityNames, which tracks invalid community name attempts, indicating potential unauthorized access attempts.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a device receiving a request with an invalid community name;
WHEN the SNMP entity processes the request;
THEN the monitoring system shall increment the counter and trigger an alert within 5 seconds.'}, {'id': 'PERF-01', 'category': 'Non-functional', 'text': 'The system shall process SNMP Get-Request and Get-Next PDUs with a response latency of less than 2 seconds for 95% of requests under normal load.', 'rationale': 'The snmpInGetRequests and snmpInGetResponses objects track successful requests and responses, and performance must be maintained to ensure real-time monitoring.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a network of 100 devices with moderate traffic;
WHEN the monitoring system processes 1000 Get-Requests;
THEN 95% of responses shall be delivered within 2 seconds.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The NETCONF server shall implement a comprehensive authorization scheme that maps user identities to configuration access rights, ensuring that users are only permitted to perform operations they are authorized to execute.', 'rationale': 'The RFC specifies that authorization is not defined in the protocol but is critical for preventing unauthorized changes to sensitive configuration data, such as IP addresses or user credentials.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a user attempting to commit a configuration change to the running configuration;
WHEN the user lacks authorization for that operation;
THEN the system shall return a 'access denied' response with a status code of 403."}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'All configuration data transmitted over the NETCONF channel shall be protected with confidentiality and integrity, using transport-level encryption (e.g., SSH) and authentication mechanisms.', 'rationale': 'Configuration data often contains sensitive information such as passwords and topologies, making it vulnerable to eavesdropping and data injection if transmitted in the clear.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a configuration retrieval request;
WHEN the data is transmitted over an unencrypted channel;
THEN the system shall reject the request or fail the connection with an error indicating lack of encryption.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-001', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': "The <get-config> operation shall support XPath-based filtering via the 'select' attribute in a <filter> element when the 'type' attribute is set to 'xpath'.", 'rationale': 'The RFC defines that XPath expressions can be used to filter configuration data, allowing precise retrieval of specific data nodes.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN an RPC request with a filter element containing type='xpath' and select='/t:top/t:users/t:user[t:name='fred']';
WHEN the request is processed;
THEN the response shall return only the user data for 'fred' with fully specified ancestor nodes in the data model."}, {'id': 'FUNC-002', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'The <copy-config> and <edit-config> operations shall disallow changes to configuration elements that a user does not have explicit authorization to modify.', 'rationale': 'Global configuration operations are inherently sensitive and must be restricted to authorized users to prevent unintended or insecure device states.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a user with limited access to interface IP settings;
WHEN attempting to commit a configuration that includes an IP address change;
THEN the system shall reject the operation and return a 'access denied' response."}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "The 'listen-ipv4-address' type shall be defined using a derived type from 'inet:ipv4-address' to ensure consistency with standard IP address formats.", 'rationale': 'Aligns with YANG built-in types to prevent malformed or invalid IP address inputs, reducing configuration errors and attack surface.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a YANG submodule defining a custom type for IPv4 addresses;
WHEN the type is defined with 'inet:ipv4-address' as the base type;
THEN the type shall be valid according to RFC 7950 YANG 1.1 and shall support standard IPv4 address syntax."}, {'id': 'FUNC-001', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': "The 'listen-ipv4-address' type shall have a default value of '0.0.0.0' and be defined within a submodule that belongs to a parent module.", 'rationale': 'Provides a consistent default for service endpoints, ensuring that configurations are valid even when no explicit value is provided.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a configuration that does not specify an IPv4 address;
WHEN the service endpoint uses the 'listen-ipv4-address' type;
THEN the value shall default to '0.0.0.0' and shall be valid according to the 'inet:ipv4-address' type constraints."}, {'id': 'NON-001', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'text': "The 'listen-ipv4-address' type shall be defined with a description and reference to the source specification (RFC 7950).", 'rationale': 'Ensures traceability and compliance with YANG standards, supporting auditability and interoperability.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a YANG submodule;
WHEN the 'listen-ipv4-address' type is defined;
THEN it shall include a description and a reference to RFC 7950, and the description shall explain the purpose of the type."}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "All leaf data values in configuration data trees MUST match the type constraints defined in the leaf's type, including 'range', 'length', and 'pattern' properties.", 'rationale': 'Ensures data integrity and prevents invalid or malformed inputs that could lead to misconfigurations or system instability.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a configuration payload with a leaf value that violates a 'range' or 'length' constraint;
WHEN the payload is parsed;
THEN the server MUST return an 'invalid-value' error with the appropriate error-app-tag and error-message."}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'All key leafs MUST be present for every list entry in the configuration data tree.', 'rationale': 'Ensures referential integrity and prevents partial or missing entries that could cause inconsistent or invalid configurations.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a list entry with missing key leafs;
WHEN the payload is parsed;
THEN the server MUST return a 'missing-element' error in the <rpc-error>."}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "There MUST be no nodes tagged with 'when' present in the data tree if the 'when' condition evaluates to 'false' in the server.", 'rationale': 'Prevents the presence of invalid or conditional nodes that could lead to inconsistent or unintended configuration states.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a node with a 'when' condition that evaluates to false;
WHEN the data tree is validated;
THEN the server MUST remove the node or return an 'unknown-element' error in the <rpc-error>."}, {'id': 'SEC-004', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "During the <edit-config> operation, if a modification causes a node's 'when' condition to evaluate to false, the node MUST be deleted from the data tree.", 'rationale': 'Ensures that only valid, conditionally enabled nodes remain in the configuration, preventing invalid or orphaned configurations.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a configuration update that causes a 'when' condition to evaluate to false;
WHEN the <edit-config> operation is applied;
THEN the server MUST delete the affected node or return an 'unknown-element' error in the <rpc-error>."}, {'id': 'SEC-005', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'text': 'The running configuration datastore MUST always be valid and satisfy all constraints defined in YANG models.', 'rationale': 'Ensures that the live configuration is consistent and compliant with defined data rules at all times.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a running configuration datastore;
WHEN the system performs a validation check;
THEN all constraints defined in YANG models MUST be satisfied.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The RESTCONF server shall validate all requests for configuration and operational data using strong authentication (e.g., TLS with mutual authentication) and role-based access control to prevent unauthorized access.', 'rationale': 'Ensures only authorized personnel can access or modify device configurations, reducing the risk of accidental or malicious changes.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a request to retrieve configuration data from a device;
WHEN the request is made without valid authentication;
THEN the server shall return a 401 Unauthorized response with a clear error message and no configuration data.'}, {'id': 'FUNC-001', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': 'The RESTCONF server shall expose a list of available RPC operations via a GET request to /restconf/operations, with each operation represented as an empty leaf in the appropriate namespace.', 'rationale': 'Enables clients to discover available operations (e.g., system-restart) before invoking them, supporting interoperability and usability.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a client makes a GET request to /restconf/operations;
WHEN the server responds;
THEN the response shall include a list of RPC operations, each with a namespace and name, such as 'ietf-system:system-restart'."}, {'id': 'ERR-001', 'category': 'Error Handling', 'text': "The server shall return a structured error report in the 'errors' container when a request fails, including the error type, error tag, error path, and error message.", 'rationale': 'Provides clear, actionable feedback to clients about the nature and location of failures, enabling effective debugging and recovery.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a malformed configuration request;
WHEN the server processes it and detects an error;
THEN the server shall return a response with the 'errors' container containing at least one 'error' entry with error-type, error-tag, error-path, and error-message fields."}, {'id': 'DATA-001', 'category': 'Data Representation', 'text': 'All data returned by the RESTCONF server shall be encoded in either XML or JSON, with the choice specified in the message header or content type, and the YANG module namespace used to identify data origin.', 'rationale': 'Ensures compatibility with existing client tools and supports interoperability across different client implementations.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a request to retrieve device configuration;
WHEN the response is returned in JSON;
THEN the response shall include a field such as 'ietf-system:system-restart' to identify the module namespace for the operation."}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'The <copy-config> operation shall only be permitted if the client has read access to all data nodes in the source datastore. Any data nodes to which the client lacks read access shall be silently omitted from the copy operation.', 'rationale': "Prevents unauthorized exposure of sensitive configuration data during copy operations, aligning with RFC 8341's requirement for access control during data transfer.", 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client attempting to copy from a source datastore with partial read access;
WHEN the copy operation is executed;
THEN data nodes without read access are omitted, and no error is returned to the client.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "The <copy-config> operation shall require explicit access permissions for any data nodes that are created or modified as a result of the operation. If the operation would result in creating a node, the user must have 'create' access; if it would delete a node, the user must have 'delete' access; if it would modify a node, the user must have 'update' access.", 'rationale': 'Ensures that users have appropriate privileges for any data node changes, preventing unauthorized modifications or deletions.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a client attempting to perform a <copy-config> operation that would create or modify a node;
WHEN the operation is executed;
THEN if the user lacks the required access (create, delete, or update), the operation shall be rejected with an 'access-denied' error."}, {'id': 'FUNC-001', 'category': 'Functional', 'text': "The server shall determine the exact nodes in the running configuration datastore that are actually different from the previous state and only validate 'create', 'update', and 'delete' access permissions for those specific nodes during a <commit> operation.", 'rationale': 'Improves efficiency by reducing unnecessary permission checks and ensures only relevant nodes are validated.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a session that can read the entire datastore but only modifies one leaf;
WHEN the session performs a <commit> operation;
THEN the server shall only check 'update' access permission for that one leaf, not for all nodes."}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "No data content from restricted datastore nodes shall be exposed in any <rpc-error> elements within the server's reply, regardless of the operation performed.", 'rationale': 'Prevents leakage of sensitive configuration data through error messages, as specified in the RFC.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client performs an operation that results in an error;
WHEN the error is returned in an <rpc-error> element;
THEN the error message shall not contain any data from restricted datastore nodes.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "The NETCONF server shall enforce access control on all data nodes that are marked with the 'default-deny-write' extension, allowing only a designated recovery session to have write access, and denying all other users.", 'rationale': 'This ensures that sensitive security parameters are not modified by unauthorized users, reducing the risk of misconfiguration or compromise.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a data node with the 'default-deny-write' extension and a user attempting to write to it;
WHEN the user is not part of the designated recovery session;
THEN the server shall deny the write operation and log the attempt."}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'text': "The NETCONF server shall enforce access control on all data nodes that are marked with the 'default-deny-all' extension, allowing only a designated recovery session to have read, write, or execute access, and denying all other users.", 'rationale': 'This provides the highest level of protection for extremely sensitive parameters, such as device identity or cryptographic keys.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a data node with the 'default-deny-all' extension and a user attempting to perform any operation (read, write, execute);
WHEN the user is not part of the designated recovery session;
THEN the server shall deny the operation and log the attempt."}, {'id': 'FUNC-001', 'category': 'Functionality', 'text': "The server shall allow a user to perform a read operation on a data node if no explicit rule matches, unless the 'read-default' setting is set to 'deny'.", 'rationale': 'Provides a default behavior for read operations when no specific rule is defined, ensuring usability without compromising security.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a read request on a data node with no matching access rule;
WHEN the 'read-default' setting is 'permit';
THEN the server shall allow the read operation."}, {'id': 'FUNC-002', 'category': 'Functionality', 'text': "The server shall deny a write operation (create, update, delete) on a data node if no explicit rule matches, unless the 'write-default' setting is set to 'permit'.", 'rationale': 'Prevents unauthorized modifications to configuration data, especially for sensitive parameters.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a write operation on a data node with no matching access rule;
WHEN the 'write-default' setting is 'deny';
THEN the server shall deny the operation and increment the 'denied-data-writes' counter."}]

[{'id': 'SEC-11.5.1', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'All random numbers and strings intended to be non-guessable must be generated using a cryptographically secure pseudo-random number generator (CSPRNG) and must have at least 128 bits of entropy.', 'rationale': 'To prevent predictable keys and secrets, which could lead to cryptographic compromise. The use of a CSPRNG with sufficient entropy ensures that generated keys and tokens are not susceptible to brute-force or prediction attacks.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a system generating cryptographic keys or non-guessable strings;
WHEN the system uses a CSPRNG with at least 128 bits of entropy;
THEN the output cannot be predicted by an attacker with less than 2^128 attempts, and all generated values pass entropy testing as defined in NIST SP 800-90A.'}, {'id': 'SEC-11.6.1', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'Only approved cryptographic algorithms and modes of operation must be used for key generation, seeding, and digital signature generation and verification. Key generation algorithms must not produce insecure keys vulnerable to known attacks, such as RSA keys vulnerable to Fermat factorization.', 'rationale': 'To ensure that cryptographic operations are based on well-vetted, secure algorithms that are resistant to modern cryptanalytic attacks.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a key generation process;
WHEN RSA keys are generated;
THEN the key size must be at least 2048 bits, and the key must not be generated using algorithms or parameters vulnerable to known mathematical attacks (e.g., Fermat factorization).'}, {'id': 'SEC-12.1.1', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'Only the latest recommended versions of the TLS protocol (TLS 1.2 and TLS 1.3) must be enabled, with TLS 1.3 as the preferred option.', 'rationale': 'To ensure forward compatibility and resistance to known TLS protocol vulnerabilities, such as POODLE and BEAST attacks.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a TLS configuration;
WHEN a client connects to the application;
THEN the connection must use TLS 1.3 if available, or TLS 1.2 if TLS 1.3 is not supported, and no older versions (e.g., TLS 1.0, 1.1) must be enabled.'}, {'id': 'SEC-12.3.1', 'category': 'Security', 'text': 'All inbound and outbound connections to and from the application, including monitoring systems, management tools, remote access, SSH, middleware, databases, and external APIs, must use an encrypted protocol such as TLS and must not fall back to insecure or unencrypted protocols.', 'rationale': 'To protect data in transit between services and prevent interception or eavesdropping by unauthorized parties.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a service communicating with another service or system;
WHEN the connection is established;
THEN TLS must be used, and no fallback to unencrypted protocols (e.g., HTTP, FTP) must occur, regardless of the underlying transport.'}]

[{'id': 'SRS-ONB-001', 'category': 'Functional', 'requirement': "When the onboarding workflow starts, the system shall create a persistent state record on the server, keyed to the user ID, with an initial state of 'awaiting_email_verification'.", 'rationale': 'Ensures state is stored on the server and not in client-side form fields, preventing client-side manipulation.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a new user begins onboarding;
WHEN the system initializes the workflow;
THEN a record with state 'awaiting_email_verification' is created in the database and is accessible only via server-side operations."}, {'id': 'SRS-ONB-002', 'category': 'Functional', 'requirement': 'Each step endpoint shall validate the current state of the workflow before performing an action and update the state only if the current state matches the expected transition.', 'rationale': 'Prevents invalid or out-of-order operations by enforcing state transitions in a defined sequence.', 'acceptance_criteria': "GIVEN a request to proceed to the next step;
WHEN the current state is not 'awaiting_email_verification';
THEN the request shall be rejected with a 400 error and no state update shall occur."}, {'id': 'SRS-ONB-003', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'Once a step has been completed, any subsequent attempt to re-run that step shall be rejected and return an error.', 'rationale': 'Prevents abuse of one-time operations such as bonus claims or coupon redemptions by ensuring they are not re-executed after completion.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user has completed email verification;
WHEN a request is made to re-run the email verification step;
THEN the request shall be rejected with a 409 error and no action shall be taken.'}, {'id': 'SRS-ONB-004', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'requirement': 'Partial states (e.g., email verification, KYC) shall expire after a maximum of 24 hours if no user action is taken, and the workflow shall require restart.', 'rationale': 'Prevents long-lived, inconsistent partial states that can be exploited by attackers or lead to data inconsistencies.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user starts email verification;
WHEN no action is taken within 24 hours;
THEN the state shall be invalidated and the user shall be redirected to the start of the workflow.'}, {'id': 'SRS-ONB-005', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'For any operation that checks a condition and then acts on it (e.g., check balance, then debit), the system shall use a database transaction with a SERIALIZABLE isolation level or an explicit row lock to prevent race conditions.', 'rationale': 'Ensures atomicity of critical operations to prevent duplicate actions such as bonus issuance or balance depletion.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a request to claim a one-time bonus;
WHEN the system checks if the user has not already claimed it;
THEN the check and update shall occur within a single atomic transaction, and if the user has already claimed it, the request shall fail with a 409 error.'}, {'id': 'SRS-ONB-006', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'For external operations (e.g., sending an email, charging a card), the system shall accept an idempotency key from the client, store it in the database, and return a cached result on retry to prevent duplicate actions.', 'rationale': 'Prevents duplicate execution of non-idempotent operations when retries occur, such as sending a password reset email or applying a coupon.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a client sends a request to send a password reset email with an idempotency key;
WHEN the same request is sent again with the same key;
THEN the system shall return the previously stored result and not send a new email.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'All access to the legacy financial reporting application must be authenticated before any user can initiate a session.', 'rationale': 'The application currently lacks authentication, which exposes it to unauthorized access. Authentication ensures only authorized users can initiate interactions with the application, aligning with the principle of least privilege.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user attempts to access the application via the public IP;
WHEN the user does not provide valid credentials;
THEN access is denied and an error message is returned indicating authentication is required.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'The legacy application must be hosted within a restricted internal subnet, and access to this subnet must be restricted to authenticated users via a VPN or other secure tunnel.', 'rationale': 'Hosting the application in a restricted subnet reduces exposure to external threats and aligns with network-level access control recommendations for legacy systems.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user attempts to access the application from an external network;
WHEN the user attempts to connect to the public IP;
THEN the connection is blocked and the user is redirected to a secure access portal requiring VPN authentication.'}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'All ports used by the application must be closed unless strictly necessary for its operation, and firewall rules must restrict access to only required ports.', 'rationale': 'Closing unused ports reduces the attack surface and limits potential exploitation vectors for the application.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a port scan is performed on the host running the application;
WHEN the scan identifies a port not required for application functionality;
THEN the port is closed and a firewall rule is applied to block access to that port.'}, {'id': 'SEC-004', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'The application must not allow direct access to administrative features or functions; any such functionality must be disabled or restricted to authorized users only.', 'rationale': 'Administrative features pose a high risk of privilege escalation and unauthorized data manipulation, especially in legacy systems with limited oversight.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a user attempts to access an administrative function;
WHEN the user is not authenticated or lacks administrative privileges;
THEN the request is rejected with a generic error message and no access is granted.'}, {'id': 'SEC-005', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'requirement': 'The application must be subject to regular vulnerability scanning using an industry-standard tool (e.g., Nessus or Qualys) at least quarterly, with scans scheduled automatically.', 'rationale': 'Regular vulnerability scanning helps identify and prioritize security risks, especially in legacy systems with known vulnerabilities.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a vulnerability scan is conducted quarterly;
WHEN the scan identifies a known vulnerability with a CVE;
THEN a report is generated and the vulnerability is logged in the organization’s vulnerability management system.'}]

[{'id': 'SEC-001', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'All AI-generated changes to build scripts, CI/CD configurations, or deployment infrastructure must be reviewed and approved by a human developer before merging.', 'rationale': 'AI-generated changes can introduce security vulnerabilities, especially in build or deployment scripts that execute with elevated privileges. Human ownership and accountability ensure that changes are understood and verified.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an AI-generated change to a build script or CI/CD configuration;
WHEN the change is proposed;
THEN a human developer must review and explicitly approve the change before it is merged into the repository.'}, {'id': 'SEC-002', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'AI-generated GitHub Actions must reference third-party actions by a specific commit SHA, not by a mutable tag.', 'rationale': 'Using a mutable tag can result in unintended updates to third-party actions, potentially introducing security flaws or breaking functionality. A pinned SHA ensures reproducibility and stability.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN a GitHub Actions workflow generated by an AI tool;
WHEN it references a third-party action;
THEN the reference must be a specific commit SHA, not a tag or branch name.'}, {'id': 'SEC-003', 'category': 'Security', 'requirement': 'AI-generated content in PR comments, commit messages, or IDE chat panes must be sanitized to remove Markdown image tags, hidden links, and zero-width characters.', 'rationale': 'Unsanitized content can contain exfiltration links, bidi overrides, or invisible characters that may be used to manipulate agent behavior or deceive reviewers.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an AI-generated PR comment or commit message;
WHEN rendered in the IDE or PR interface;
THEN all Markdown image tags and zero-width characters must be stripped or escaped before display.'}, {'id': 'SEC-004', 'category': 'Non-Functional', 'requirement': 'All AI-generated code changes must be attributed to a specific human developer who is responsible for the security and maintainability of the change.', 'rationale': 'Human accountability ensures that developers are responsible for the correctness and security of AI-generated code, not the AI tool itself.', 'acceptance_criteria': 'GIVEN an AI-generated code change;
WHEN merged into the repository;
THEN an audit trail must record the human developer who approved the change, the AI tool used, and the model version.'}]
